            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Inversion about \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(r\\).  \nRecall the standard facts (all angles are measured modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\)):\n\n* If a circle passes through \\(A\\) then its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a line not passing through \\(A\\); conversely, a line not passing through \\(A\\) is sent to a circle through \\(A\\).\n* If a line passes through \\(A\\) it is invariant as a set; only the point \\(A\\) itself is fixed pointwise.\n* Inversion preserves oriented angles; in particular, orthogonality of circles is preserved.\n* For a circle \\(\\kappa\\) passing through the centre of inversion, the image of its centre is the foot of the perpendicular from the centre of inversion onto the image line of \\(\\kappa\\).\n\nDenote by \\(X'\\) the image of a point \\(X\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\nBecause the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nare two distinct lines, neither of which passes through \\(A\\).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does **not** contain \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\omega_{MN}= \\mathcal I(MN)\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nis a circle that **passes through** \\(A\\) (its centre is not \\(A\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Images of the auxiliary points\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nC'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThe ray \\(AP\\) is invariant as a set, hence the second intersection of this ray with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with the line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); we denote this image by \\(E'\\).  \nSimilarly we set \\(F'=\\mathcal I(F)\\), the second intersection of the ray with \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP,\\qquad E'\\neq A,\\;F'\\neq A.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Image of the circumcenter \\(P\\)\n\nThe circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) passes through the centre of inversion \\(A\\); consequently its image is the line \\(C'D'\\).  \nBy the property quoted above, the image of the centre of a circle through \\(A\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) onto the image line. Hence\n\n\\[\nP' \\text{ is the foot of the perpendicular from } A \\text{ to } C'D'.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nIn particular \\(AP'\\perp C'D'\\) and \\(P'\\in C'D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Images of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n*Altitude from \\(P\\).*  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\), its image \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) is a circle through \\(A\\).  \nThe image of a line not through \\(A\\) is a circle through \\(A\\); therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is sent to the circle\n\n\\[\n\\gamma_{P}= \\mathcal I(\\text{altitude from }P),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\(A\\) and \\(P'\\) and is orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) (orthogonality is preserved by inversion).\n\n*Altitudes from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).*  \nAnalogously, the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is sent to a circle\n\n\\[\n\\gamma_{M}= \\mathcal I(\\text{altitude from }M)\n\\]\n\nthat passes through \\(A\\) and \\(M'\\) and is orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{MN}\\); the altitude from \\(N\\) is sent to a circle \\(\\gamma_{N}\\) through \\(A\\) and \\(N'\\) orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{MN}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Determination of \\(H'\\)\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes; consequently its image \\(H'\\) is the common intersection of the three circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\).\n\nBecause each of \\(\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{MN}\\), the centre of each lies on the line joining the centre of \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) (which is the centre of the inversion, i.e. the point \\(A\\)) to the point of orthogonal intersection.  Hence the two circles \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) and \\(\\gamma_{N}\\) intersect the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively at right angles.  \n\nConsequently the second intersection of \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P'\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); denote this foot by \\(E'\\).  \nSimilarly the second intersection of \\(\\gamma_{N}\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P'\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); denote this foot by \\(F'\\).\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nE'=\\operatorname{proj}_{\\ell_{\\Omega}}P',\\qquad \nF'=\\operatorname{proj}_{\\ell_{\\Gamma}}P'.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nSince the three circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\) meet at a single point, the two feet in (2.6) must coincide; therefore\n\n\\[\nE'=F'.\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nBecause the orthocenter is the common intersection of the three altitudes, we have\n\n\\[\nH' = E' = F'.\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to the ray \\(AP\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through the centre of inversion \\(A\\); therefore its image\n\n\\[\n\\ell' = \\mathcal I(\\ell)\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nis a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nSince inversion preserves oriented angles, the angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the ray \\(AP\\) equals the angle between \\(\\ell'\\) and the same ray.  Because \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), the angle is \\(0^{\\circ}\\); consequently \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the point where the ray meets \\(\\ell'\\).  By (2.8) this point is precisely \\(H'=E'=F'\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ passing through }E'\\text{ and tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'.\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nPoints \\(B,E,F\\) are transformed respectively into \\(B',E',F'\\).  \nBecause \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), its image lies on both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); thus\n\n\\[\nB' = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.8) we have \\(E'=F'\\).  Therefore the image \\(\\omega'\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the circle through \\(A\\) that has a double point at \\(E'\\) and also passes through \\(B'\\); equivalently, \\(\\omega'\\) is the **tangent line** to the ray \\(AP\\) at \\(E'\\) together with the point \\(B'\\).  In particular \\(\\omega'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at \\(E'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nFrom (2.10) the circle \\(\\ell'\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(E'\\) and is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at \\(E'\\).  \nFrom the previous paragraph the circle \\(\\omega'\\) also passes through \\(A\\) and is tangent to the same ray at the same point \\(E'\\).  \nHence the two circles \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) are tangent to each other at \\(E'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9  Pull\u2011back to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency: if two curves are tangent at a point different from the centre of inversion, then their images are tangent at the corresponding images.  \nSince \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) are tangent at \\(E'\\neq A\\), their pre\u2011images \\(\\ell\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) are tangent at the pre\u2011image of \\(E'\\), which is precisely the point \\(E\\) (the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n\nTherefore the line \\(\\ell\\) \u2013 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
