cal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n> \\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)                                   \\tag{2.1}\n> \\]\n> are two distinct lines, none of which passes through\u202fA.  \n> The line\u202fMN does **not** contain\u202fA; therefore\n> \\[\n> \\omega_{MN}= \\mathcal I(MN)                                          \\tag{2.2}\n> \\]\n> is a circle that passes through\u202fA.  \n> Let\u202fO be the centre of\u202f\u03c9_{MN}; in general\u202fO\u2260A.\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Since \u03a9 and \u0393 pass through A, their images are indeed lines not through A. Since MN does not pass through A, its image is a circle through A. The statements are correct.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Images of the auxiliary points (continued)\n\n> \u201cSince\u202fC\u2208\u03a9 and\u202fD\u2208\u0393,\n> \\[\n> C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.                        \\tag{2.3}\n> \\]  \n> The circumcircle\u202f(ACD) passes through\u202fA; its image is the line\u202fC\u2032D\u2032.  \n> By the property quoted above, the image of its centre\u202fP is the foot of the perpendicular from\u202fA onto that line:\n> \\[\n> P' \\text{ is the foot of the perpendicular from }A\\text{ to }C'D'. \\tag{2.4}\n> \\]  \n> Hence\u202fAP\u2032\u22a5C\u2032D\u2032 and\u202fP\u2032\u2208C\u2032D\u2032.\u201d\n\n**Verification:** The image of a circle through the inversion centre is a line; the image of its centre is indeed the foot of the perpendicular from\u202fA onto that line. Thus (2.4) is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Images of\u202fE\u202fand\u202fF  \n\n> \u201cThe ray\u202fAP is invariant as a set under\u202f\ud835\udcd8.  \n> Consequently the second intersection of this ray with\u202f\u03a9 (point\u202fE) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with the line\u202f\u2113_\u03a9; denote this image by\u202fE\u2032.  \n> Similarly define\u202fF\u2032 as the second intersection of the ray with\u202f\u2113_\u0393. Thus\n> \\[\n> E',F'\\in AP,\\qquad E'\\neq A,\\;F'\\neq A.                           \\tag{2.5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:** A ray from the inversion centre maps to itself; the second intersection with a circle through\u202fA maps to the second intersection with the image line. Hence (2.5) is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Images of the three altitudes of\u202f\u0394PMN  \n\n> *Altitude from\u202fP.*  \n> \u201cThe altitude from\u202fP is the line through\u202fP perpendicular to\u202fMN.  \n> Since\u202fMN does not pass through\u202fA, its image\u202f\u03c9_{MN} is a circle through\u202fA.  \n> The image of a line not through\u202fA is a circle through\u202fA; therefore the altitude from\u202fP is sent to a circle\n> \\[\n> \\gamma_{P}= \\mathcal I(\\text{altitude from }P)\n> \\]\n> that passes through\u202fA and\u202fP\u2032 and is orthogonal to\u202f\u03c9_{MN} (orthogonality is preserved).\u201d\n\n**Verification:** The altitude from\u202fP is a line not through\u202fA, so its image is a circle through\u202fA containing\u202fP\u2032. Because the original altitude is perpendicular to\u202fMN, the images intersect orthogonally. This part is correct.\n\n> *Altitudes from\u202fM\u202fand\u202fN.*  \n> \u201cAnalogously, the altitude from\u202fM (the line through\u202fM perpendicular to\u202fPN) is sent to a circle\n> \\[\n> \\gamma_{M}= \\mathcal I(\\text{altitude from }M)\n> \\]\n> that passes through\u202fA and\u202fM\u2032 and is orthogonal to\u202f\u03c9_{MN}; the altitude from\u202fN is sent to a circle\u202f\u03b3_N through\u202fA and\u202fN\u2032 orthogonal to\u202f\u03c9_{MN}.\u201d\n\n**Verification \u2013 **Critical Error**.  \nThe altitude from\u202fM is **perpendicular to\u202fPN**, not to\u202fMN. Under inversion, the image of the altitude is a circle through\u202fA and\u202fM\u2032 that is orthogonal to the image of\u202fPN, **not** to\u202f\u03c9_{MN}. The claim that\u202f\u03b3_M (and similarly\u202f\u03b3_N) is orthogonal to\u202f\u03c9_{MN} is false. Consequently the subsequent reasoning that relies on this orthogonality is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Equality\u202fE\u2032\u202f=\u202fF\u2032  \n\n> \u201cBecause\u202f\u03b3_M is orthogonal to\u202f\u03c9_{MN}, the centre of\u202f\u03b3_M lies on the line\u202fOA (the line joining the centre\u202fO of\u202f\u03c9_{MN} to the centre\u202fA of the inversion).  \n> Consequently\u202f\u03b3_M meets the line\u202f\u2113_\u03a9 at right angles.  \n> The same reasoning shows that\u202f\u03b3_N meets\u202f\u2113_\u0393 orthogonally.  \n> \u2026  \n> \u2026 the second intersection of\u202f\u03b3_M with\u202f\u2113_\u03a9 is precisely the foot of the perpendicular from\u202fP\u2032 to\u202f\u2113_\u03a9. Denote this foot by\u202fE\u2032.  \n> \u2026 similarly the second intersection of\u202f\u03b3_N with\u202f\u2113_\u0393 is the foot of the perpendicular from\u202fP\u2032 to\u202f\u2113_\u0393; denote it by\u202fF\u2032.  \n> \u2026 the three circles\u202f\u03b3_P,\u202f\u03b3_M,\u202f\u03b3_N \u2026 intersect in a single point, namely the image\u202fH\u2032 of the orthocenter\u202fH.  \n> Hence the two feet just constructed must coincide:\n> \\[\n> E'=F'. \\tag{2.6}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification \u2013 Multiple Critical Errors**\n\n1. **Centre of\u202f\u03b3_M on\u202fOA** \u2013 **Critical Error**.  \n   For two circles intersecting orthogonally at\u202fA, the line joining their centres is **perpendicular** to\u202fOA, not collinear with\u202fOA. Hence the statement that the centre of\u202f\u03b3_M lies on\u202fOA is false.\n\n2. **\u03b3_M meets\u202f\u2113_\u03a9 orthogonally** \u2013 **Critical Error**.  \n   Orthogonality of\u202f\u03b3_M to\u202f\u03c9_{MN} gives no information about its intersection with the unrelated line\u202f\u2113_\u03a9. The claim is unjustified.\n\n3. **Second intersection of\u202f\u03b3_M with\u202f\u2113_\u03a9 is the foot from\u202fP\u2032** \u2013 **Critical Error**.  \n   There is no reason for the second intersection of\u202f\u03b3_M and\u202f\u2113_\u03a9 to be the perpendicular foot from\u202fP\u2032; this identification is not derived from any proven property.\n\n4. **Conclusion\u202fE\u2032\u202f=\u202fF\u2032** \u2013 **Critical Error**.  \n   The equality is inferred from the (false) claim that both feet are the common intersection of\u202f\u03b3_M,\u202f\u03b3_N, and\u202f\u03b3_P. Since the preceding statements are invalid, the equality is unsupported.\n\nThus Lemma\u202f1 is entirely unsound.\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Image of the orthocenter  \n\n> \u201cFrom the definition of the orthocenter,\u202fH is the common intersection of the three altitudes of\u202f\u0394PMN.  \n> Under inversion, these altitudes become the circles\u202f\u03b3_P,\u202f\u03b3_M,\u202f\u03b3_N; consequently their common intersection is the image\u202fH\u2032 of\u202fH.  \n> By the construction in Lemma\u202f1 the common intersection is exactly the point\u202fE\u2032=F\u2032. Hence\n> \\[\n> H' = E' = F'. \\tag{2.7}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification \u2013 Critical Error (propagation).**  \nLemma\u202f2 relies on Lemma\u202f1, which has been shown to be false. Therefore the identification\u202fH\u2032\u202f=\u202fE\u2032\u202f=\u202fF\u2032 is not established. The step is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 2.8  Image of the line through\u202fH\u202fparallel to\u202fAP  \n\n> \u201cLet\u202f\u2113 be the line through\u202fH that is parallel to the ray\u202fAP.  \n> Since\u202f\u2113 does **not** pass through the centre\u202fA, its image\n> \\[\n> \\ell' = \\mathcal I(\\ell)                                            \\tag{2.8}\n> \\]\n> is a circle passing through\u202fA.  \n> Inversion preserves oriented angles; the oriented angle between\u202f\u2113 and the ray\u202fAP equals the oriented angle between\u202f\u2113\u2032 and the same ray.  \n> Because\u202f\u2113 is parallel to\u202fAP, this angle is\u202f0\u00b0, therefore\u202f\u2113\u2032 is tangent to the ray\u202fAP at the point where the ray meets\u202f\u2113\u2032.  \n> By (2.7) that point is\u202fH\u2032=E\u2032=F\u2032. Consequently\n> \\[\n> \\ell' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ passing through }E'\\text{ and tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'. \\tag{2.9}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification \u2013 Critical Error.**  \nA line\u202f\u2113 parallel to\u202fAP does **not** intersect\u202fAP; the angle between them is defined as 0\u00b0 at infinity. Under inversion, the image\u202f\u2113\u2032 is a circle through\u202fA, and the image of\u202fAP is the same line\u202fAP. The angle preservation yields that **\u2113\u2032 and\u202fAP are tangent at\u202fA**, not at\u202fH\u2032 (or\u202fE\u2032). Hence the claim that\u202f\u2113\u2032 is tangent to\u202fAP at\u202fE\u2032 (or\u202fH\u2032) is false. The reasoning misapplies the angle\u2011preservation principle.\n\n---\n\n### 2.9  Image of the circumcircle of\u202f\u0394BEF  \n\n> \u201cThe point\u202fB lies on both\u202f\u03a9 and\u202f\u0393; therefore its image lies on both\u202f\u2113_\u03a9 and\u202f\u2113_\u0393:\n> \\[\n> B' = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}.                                 \\tag{2.10}\n> \\]  \n> The circumcircle of\u202f\u0394BEF passes through\u202fA; its image\u202f\u03c9\u2032 is a line (the image of a circle through the centre of inversion).  \n> Since the line must contain the images of the three points\u202fB,\u202fE,\u202fF, we have\n> \\[\n> \\omega' = \\text{the line through }B' \\text{ that is tangent to the ray }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'. \\tag{2.11}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification \u2013 Critical Error.**  \nThe circumcircle of\u202fBEF indeed passes through\u202fA, so its image is a line. This line must contain\u202fB\u2032,\u202fE\u2032,\u202fF\u2032. Because\u202fE\u2032 and\u202fF\u2032 lie on the ray\u202fAP, the line\u202f\u03c9\u2032 is simply the line **through\u202fB\u2032 and\u202fE\u2032** (or\u202fF\u2032). There is no reason for this line to be *tangent* to the ray\u202fAP; a line intersecting a ray at a point is not \u201ctangent\u201d to the ray unless it coincides with the ray, which it does not (since\u202fB\u2032 is not on\u202fAP). Hence the description \u201ctangent to the ray\u202fAP at\u202fE\u2032=F\u2032\u201d is false.\n\n---\n\n### 2.10  Tangency in the inverted picture  \n\n> \u201cFrom (2.9) the circle\u202f\u2113\u2032 passes through\u202fA and is tangent to the ray\u202fAP at\u202fE\u2032.  \n> From (2.11) the line\u202f\u03c9\u2032 also passes through\u202fA and is tangent to the same ray at the same point\u202fE\u2032.  \n> Hence the two curves\u202f\u2113\u2032 and\u202f\u03c9\u2032 are tangent to each other at\u202fE\u2032.\u201d\n\n**Verification \u2013 Critical Error.**  \nBoth premises (2.9) and (2.11) are incorrect, as shown above. Consequently the claimed tangency of\u202f\u2113\u2032 and\u202f\u03c9\u2032 at\u202fE\u2032 is unfounded.\n\n---\n\n### 2.11  Pull\u2011back to the original configuration  \n\n> \u201cInversion preserves tangency: if two curves are tangent at a point distinct from the centre of inversion, their images are tangent at the corresponding images.  \n> Since\u202f\u2113\u2032 and\u202f\u03c9\u2032 are tangent at\u202fE\u2032\u2260A, their pre\u2011images are tangent at the pre\u2011image of\u202fE\u2032, which is precisely the point\u202fE.  \n> Therefore the pre\u2011image of\u202f\u2113\u2032, namely the line\u202f\u2113 through\u202fH parallel to\u202fAP, is tangent to the pre\u2011image of\u202f\u03c9\u2032, i.e. to the circumcircle of\u202f\u0394BEF.\u201d\n\n**Verification \u2013 Partial correctness.**  \nThe principle that inversion preserves tangency (away from the centre) is correct. However, because the preceding steps establishing tangency of\u202f\u2113\u2032 and\u202f\u03c9\u2032 are invalid, this conclusion does not follow. Hence the final statement is not justified.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution correctly recalls basic inversion properties (Sections\u202f2.1\u20132.3) and correctly describes the image of the altitude from\u202fP (Section\u202f2.5, first bullet). **However, the core of the argument collapses** due to a cascade of critical errors:\n\n1. Misidentification of the images of the altitudes from\u202fM and\u202fN (they are not orthogonal to\u202f\u03c9_{MN}).\n2. Incorrect geometric consequences drawn from the false orthogonality (centres on\u202fOA, orthogonal intersection with\u202f\u2113_\u03a9, identification of feet).\n3. Unjustified equality\u202fE\u2032\u202f=\u202fF\u2032 and consequent identification\u202fH\u2032\u202f=\u202fE\u2032\u202f=\u202fF\u2032.\n4. Misapplication of angle preservation leading to an erroneous claim that the image of the line through\u202fH parallel to\u202fAP is tangent to\u202fAP at\u202fE\u2032 rather than at\u202fA.\n5. Incorrect description of the image of the circumcircle of\u202fBEF as a line tangent to\u202fAP.\n6. The final tangency claim in the inverted picture rests on the false statements above.\n\nBecause these errors break the logical chain, the solution does **not** constitute a valid proof of the problem statement."
