nter\n\nFrom the definition of the orthocenter, \\(H\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nUnder inversion, these altitudes become the circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\); consequently their common intersection is the image \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\).  \nBy the construction in Lemma\u202f1 the common intersection is exactly the point \\(E'=F'\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nH' = E' = F'. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to the ray \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(A\\), its image\n\n\\[\n\\ell' = \\mathcal I(\\ell)                                            \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nis a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nInversion preserves oriented angles; the oriented angle between \\(\\ell\\) and the ray \\(AP\\) equals the oriented angle between \\(\\ell'\\) and the same ray.  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\), this angle is \\(0^{\\circ}\\); therefore \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the point where the ray meets \\(\\ell'\\).  \nBy (2.7) that point is \\(H'=E'=F'\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\ell' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ passing through }E'\\text{ and tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nThe point \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore its image lies on both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\):\n\n\\[\nB' = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}.                                 \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nThe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image \\(\\omega'\\) is a line (the image of a circle through the centre of inversion).  \nSince the line must contain the images o