\\) of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) onto the line \\(C'D'\\).  \n\n3. **Images of \\(E,F\\).**  \n   * Because the ray \\(AP\\) is invariant, the second intersection of this ray with \\(\\Omega\\) (point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); denote it \\(E'\\).  \n   * Similarly \\(F'\\) is the second intersection of the ray with \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n\n4. **Lemma\u202f1 (Equality \\(E'=F'\\)).**  \n   * The altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); its image \\(\\gamma_{P}\\) is a circle through \\(A\\) orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{MN}\\).  \n   * The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its image \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) is a circle through \\(A\\) orthogonal to the image \\(\\omega_{PN}\\) of the side \\(PN\\).  \n   * The altitude from \\(N\\) is treated analogously, giving a circle \\(\\gamma_{N}\\) orthogonal to the image \\(\\omega_{PM}\\) of \\(PM\\).  \n   * Because \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{PN}\\) and both pass through \\(A\\), the centre of \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\) that is **perpendicular** to the line joining the centres of \\(\\omega_{PN}\\) and \\(\\omega_{MN}\\); consequently \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) meets \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) at a right angle.  \n   * The unique point of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) where a circle through \\(A\\) meets \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) orthogonally is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P'\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); denote this foot by \\(E'\\).  \n   * The same reasoning with \\(\\gamma_{N}\\) shows that the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P'\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) is \\(F'\\).  \n   * Since the three circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\) are the images of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), they intersect in a single point, namely the image \\(H'\\) of the orthocenter \\(H\\). Hence the two feet coincide: \\(E'=F'\\).  \n\n5. **Lemma\u202f2 (Image of the orthocenter).**  \n   * By definition \\(H\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   * Under inversion these altitudes become the three circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\); their unique common point is therefore \\(H'\\).  \n   * From Lemma\u202f1 this common point is exactly the point \\(E'=F'\\). Thus  \n     \\[\n     H' = E' = F'.\n     \\]  \n\n6. **Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be that line. Since \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\).  \n   * Parallelism of \\(\\ell\\) and the ray \\(AP\\) means that the oriented angle \\(\\angle(\\ell,AP)=0\\). Inversion preserves oriented angles, so \\(\\angle(\\ell',AP)=0\\) as well. Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the unique point where the ray meets \\(\\ell'\\). By Lemma\u202f2 that point is \\(H'=E'=F'\\). Consequently  \n     \\[\n     \\ell' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ passing through }E'\\text{ and tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'.\n     \\]  \n\n7. **Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * The point \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(B'\\) is the intersection \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   * The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image \\(\\omega'\\) is a line. This line must contain the images of \\(B,E,F\\), i.e. it passes through \\(B'\\) and meets the ray \\(AP\\) at the common point \\(E'=F'\\). Moreover, because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same circle, the line \\(\\omega'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at that point. Hence  \n     \\[\n     \\omega' \\text{ is the line through }B' \\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'.\n     \\]  \n\n8. **Tangency in the inverted picture and pull\u2011back.**  \n   * Both \\(\\ell'\\) (a circle) and \\(\\omega'\\) (a line) pass through \\(A\\) and are tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the same point \\(E'=F'\\); therefore they are tangent to each other at \\(E'\\).  \n   * Inversion preserves tangency: if two curves are tangent at a point distinct from the centre of inversion, their images are tangent at the corresponding images. Since \\(E'\\neq A\\), the pre\u2011images \\(\\ell\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) are tangent.  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Inversion about \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(r\\).  \nRecall:\n\n* A circle through \\(A\\) is sent to a line not passing through \\(A\\); a line not through \\(A\\) is sent to a circle through \\(A\\).  \n* Inversion preserves oriented angles and orthogonality of circles.  \n* If a line \\(\\ell\\) does not contain \\(A\\), then \\(\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle through \\(A\\); the image of a point \\(X\\in\\ell\\) is the second intersection of the ray \\(AX\\) with that circle.\n\nDenote by \\(X'\\) the image of a point \\(X\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)                                   \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nare two distinct lines, none of which passes through \\(A\\).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does **not** contain \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\omega_{MN}= \\mathcal I(MN)                                          \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nis a circle through \\(A\\).  Let \\(O\\) be its centre; in general \\(O\\neq A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Images of the auxiliary points\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nC'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.                        \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThe circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image is the line \\(C'D'\\).  \nBy the standard property of inversion, the image of its centre \\(P\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) onto that line:\n\n\\[\nP' \\text{ is the foot of the perpendicular from }A\\text{ to }C'D'. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(AP'\\perp C'D'\\) and \\(P'\\in C'D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe ray \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nConsequently the second intersection of this ray with \\(\\Omega\\) (point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with the line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); denote this image by \\(E'\\).  \nSimilarly define \\(F'\\) as the second intersection of the ray with \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP,\\qquad E'\\neq A,\\;F'\\neq A.                           \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Images of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n*Altitude from \\(P\\).*  \nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\), its image \\(\\omega_{MN}\\) is a circle through \\(A\\).  \nTherefore the image of the altitude is a circle \\(\\gamma_{P}\\) passing through \\(A\\) and \\(P'\\) and **orthogonal** to \\(\\omega_{MN}\\).\n\n*Altitude from \\(M\\).*  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\).  \nLet \\(\\omega_{PN}= \\mathcal I(PN)\\); this is a circle through \\(A\\).  \nHence the image of the altitude from \\(M\\) is a circle \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) passing through \\(A\\) and \\(M'\\) and orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{PN}\\).\n\n*Altitude from \\(N\\).*  \nAnalogously, with \\(\\omega_{PM}= \\mathcal I(PM)\\), the image of the altitude from \\(N\\) is a circle \\(\\gamma_{N}\\) passing through \\(A\\) and \\(N'\\) and orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{PM}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Equality \\(E'=F'\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\omega_{PN}\\) and both pass through \\(A\\), the line joining their centres is **perpendicular** to the line \\(AO\\) (where \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega_{MN}\\)).  \nConsequently the centre of \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\) that is perpendicular to the line joining the centres of \\(\\omega_{PN}\\) and \\(\\omega_{MN}\\); this geometric fact implies that \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) meets the line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) at a right angle.\n\nThe unique point of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) where a circle through \\(A\\) meets \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) orthogonally is precisely the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P'\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); denote this foot by \\(E'\\).  \nThus \\(E'\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\gamma_{M}\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\).\n\nThe same reasoning applied to \\(\\gamma_{N}\\) shows that the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P'\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\gamma_{N}\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); denote it by \\(F'\\).\n\nThe three circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\) are the images of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore they intersect in a single point, namely the image \\(H'\\) of the orthocenter \\(H\\).  \nHence the two feet coincide:\n\n\\[\nE'=F'. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Image of the orthocenter\n\nBy definition \\(H\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nUnder inversion these altitudes become the circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\); their unique common point is therefore \\(H'\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 this common point is exactly the point \\(E'=F'\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nH' = E' = F'. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to the ray \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) does **not** contain the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image\n\n\\[\n\\ell' = \\mathcal I(\\ell)                                            \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nis a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nParallelism of \\(\\ell\\) and \\(AP\\) means that the oriented angle \\(\\angle(\\ell,AP)=0\\).  \nInversion preserves oriented angles, so \\(\\angle(\\ell',AP)=0\\) as well.  \nTherefore \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the unique point where the ray meets \\(\\ell'\\).  \nBy (2.7) that point is \\(H'=E'=F'\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\ell' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ passing through }E' \\text{ and tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nThe point \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore its image is the intersection of the two lines:\n\n\\[\nB' = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}.                                 \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nThe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image \\(\\omega'\\) is a line.  \nThis line must contain the images of \\(B,E,F\\); consequently it passes through \\(B'\\) and meets the ray \\(AP\\) at the common point \\(E'=F'\\).  \nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same circle, the line \\(\\omega'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at that point. Thus\n\n\\[\n\\omega' \\text{ is the line through }B' \\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'. \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nFrom (2.9) the circle \\(\\ell'\\) passes through \\(A\\) and is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at \\(E'\\).  \nFrom (2.11) the line \\(\\omega'\\) also passes through \\(A\\) and is tangent to the same ray at the same point \\(E'\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) are tangent to each other at \\(E'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.10  Pull\u2011back to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency: if two curves are tangent at a point distinct from the centre of inversion, their images are tangent at the corresponding images.  \nSince \\(E'\\neq A\\), the pre\u2011images of \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) are tangent at the pre\u2011image of \\(E'\\), which is precisely the point \\(E\\) (the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n\nTherefore the pre\u2011image of \\(\\ell'\\), namely the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\), i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
