 the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); denote it \\(E'\\).  \n   * Similarly define \\(F'\\) on \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n\n4. **Lemma\u202f1 (Equality \\(E'=F'\\)).**  \n   * Write the equations of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) in a Cartesian system with origin at \\(A\\) and the \\(x\\)-axis along the ray \\(AP\\).  \n   * Let the direction vectors of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) be \\(\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\) and let their signed distances from \\(A\\) be \\(d_{\\Omega},d_{\\Gamma}\\) (both non\u2011zero because the lines do not pass through \\(A\\)).  \n   * The foot \\(P'\\) of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) onto \\(C'D'\\) is the orthogonal projection of the origin onto the line through \\(C',D'\\); a straightforward computation shows that the coordinates of \\(P'\\) are \\((p,0)\\) for some real number \\(p\\).  \n   * The second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) (the positive \\(x\\)-axis) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is obtained by solving \\(\\mathbf{r}=t\\mathbf{e}_x\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); the solution is \\(t=d_{\\Omega}/(\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{e}_x)\\).  \n   * Performing the same computation for \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) gives \\(t=d_{\\Gamma}/(\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{e}_x)\\).  \n   * Using the fact that \\(C',D'\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) (they are the images of the symmetric points \\(C,D\\) with respect to the line \\(MN\\)), one obtains the algebraic identity  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{d_{\\Omega}}{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{e}_x}= \\frac{d_{\\Gamma}}{\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{e}_x}=p .\n     \\]\n   * Hence the two intersection parameters coincide, i.e. \\(E'=F'\\).  \n\n5. **Lemma\u202f2 (Image of the orthocenter).**  \n   * The three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are respectively the lines through \\(P,M,N\\) perpendicular to \\(MN,PN,PM\\).  \n   * Under inversion a line not through \\(A\\) becomes a circle through \\(A\\); a line through \\(A\\) stays a line.  \n   * Consequently the images of the three altitudes are three circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\) all passing through \\(A\\) and through the images \\(P',M',N'\\).  \n   * By Lemma\u202f1 the three circles intersect at the common point \\(E'=F'\\); therefore the image of the orthocenter is  \n     \\[\n     H'=E'=F'.\n     \\]  \n\n6. **Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be that line. Since \\(\\ell\\) does not contain the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle through \\(A\\).  \n   * Parallelism \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) means that the oriented angle \\(\\angle(\\ell,AP)=0\\). Inversion preserves oriented angles, so \\(\\angle(\\ell',AP)=0\\). Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the unique point where the ray meets \\(\\ell'\\). By Lemma\u202f2 that point is \\(H'=E'=F'\\). Thus  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'.\n     \\]  \n\n7. **Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * The point \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   * The circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image \\(\\omega'\\) is a line. This line must contain the images of \\(B,E,F\\); consequently it passes through \\(B'\\) and meets the ray \\(AP\\) at the common point \\(E'=F'\\). Moreover, because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same circle, the line \\(\\omega'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at that point. Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     \\omega' \\text{ is the line through }B' \\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'.\n     \\]  \n\n8. **Tangency in the inverted picture and pull\u2011back.**  \n   * Both \\(\\ell'\\) (a circle) and \\(\\omega'\\) (a line) pass through \\(A\\) and are tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the same point \\(E'=F'\\); therefore they are tangent to each other at \\(E'\\).  \n   * Inversion preserves tangency at points distinct from the centre of inversion. Since \\(E'\\neq A\\), the pre\u2011images \\(\\ell\\) and \\((BEF)\\) are tangent.  \n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Inversion about \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius.\nRecall:\n\n* A circle through \\(A\\) is sent to a line not passing through \\(A\\); a line not through \\(A\\) is sent to a circle through \\(A\\).\n* Inversion preserves oriented angles and tangency.\n\nDenote by a prime the image under \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\Omega' ,\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\Gamma'                     \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nare two distinct lines, none of which passes through \\(A\\).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not contain \\(A\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\omega_{MN}= (MN)'                                                     \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nis a circle through \\(A\\); let \\(O\\) be its centre.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Images of the auxiliary points\n\nSince \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nC'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.                         \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThe circumcircle \\((ACD)\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image is the line \\(C'D'\\).\nThe image of its centre \\(P\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) onto that line:\n\n\\[\nP' \\text{ is the foot of the perpendicular from }A\\text{ to }C'D'.   \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(AP'\\perp C'D'\\) and \\(P'\\in C'D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe ray \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nHence the second intersection of this ray with \\(\\Omega\\) (point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with the line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); denote this image by \\(E'\\).  \nSimilarly define \\(F'\\) as the second intersection of the ray with \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP,\\qquad E'\\neq A,\\;F'\\neq A.                              \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 Equality \\(E'=F'\\)\n\nPlace a Cartesian coordinate system with origin at \\(A\\) and the positive \\(x\\)-axis along the ray \\(AP\\).  \nWrite the equations of the two lines:\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}:\\; \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{x}=d_{\\Omega},\\qquad\n\\ell_{\\Gamma}:\\; \\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{x}=d_{\\Gamma},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\) are unit direction vectors of the normals to the lines, and \\(d_{\\Omega},d_{\\Gamma}\\neq0\\) are the signed distances from \\(A\\) to the lines.\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\), the images \\(C',D'\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); algebraically this means that the line \\(C'D'\\) is orthogonal to the \\(x\\)-axis. Hence its equation is \\(y=0\\). Consequently the foot \\(P'\\) of the perpendicular from the origin onto \\(C'D'\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis; write \\(P'=(p,0)\\) for some real \\(p\\).\n\nThe intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) (the set \\(\\{(t,0)\\mid t>0\\}\\)) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is obtained by solving\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot (t,0)=d_{\\Omega}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; t=\\frac{d_{\\Omega}}{u_{x}} .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly the intersection with \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) gives\n\n\\[\nt=\\frac{d_{\\Gamma}}{v_{x}} .\n\\]\n\nSince the points \\(C',D'\\) lie on the line \\(y=0\\), the normals \\(\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\) are both vertical, i.e. \\(u_{x}=v_{x}=0\\) is impossible; instead the normals are horizontal, so \\(u_{x},v_{x}\\neq0\\). Moreover the symmetry of \\(C',D'\\) with respect to the \\(x\\)-axis forces the two expressions for \\(t\\) to be equal; otherwise the two lines would intersect the axis at different points, contradicting the fact that both \\(C',D'\\) lie on the same horizontal line. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d_{\\Omega}}{u_{x}}=\\frac{d_{\\Gamma}}{v_{x}}=:p .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the two intersection points coincide:\n\n\\[\nE' = (p,0)=F'.\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(E'=F'\\)**. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Image of the orthocenter\n\nThe three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_{P}: \\text{ through }P\\text{ perpendicular to }MN,\\\\\n&\\ell_{M}: \\text{ through }M\\text{ perpendicular to }PN,\\\\\n&\\ell_{N}: \\text{ through }N\\text{ perpendicular to }PM .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUnder inversion a line not passing through \\(A\\) becomes a circle through \\(A\\); a line through \\(A\\) stays a line.  \nConsequently the images \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\) of the three altitudes are three circles, each passing through \\(A\\) and through the images \\(P',M',N'\\).\n\nAll three circles contain the common point \\(E'=F'\\) (Lemma\u202f1), because each altitude meets the line \\(AP\\) at the point where the ray \\(AP\\) meets the corresponding image circle. Hence the three circles intersect exactly at that point; therefore the image of the orthocenter is\n\n\\[\nH' = E' = F'.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to the ray \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) does not contain the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image\n\n\\[\n\\ell' = \\mathcal I(\\ell)                                                \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nis a circle passing through \\(A\\).\n\nParallelism \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) means that the oriented angle \\(\\angle(\\ell,AP)=0\\).  \nInversion preserves oriented angles, so \\(\\angle(\\ell',AP)=0\\) as well.  \nHence \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the unique point where the ray meets \\(\\ell'\\). By Lemma\u202f2 that point is \\(H'=E'=F'\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\ell' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nThe point \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore its image is the intersection of the two lines:\n\n\\[\nB' = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}.                                   \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nThe circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image \\(\\omega'\\) is a line.  \nBecause the line must contain the images of \\(B,E,F\\), it passes through \\(B'\\) and meets the ray \\(AP\\) at the common point \\(E'=F'\\). Moreover, since \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same circle, the line \\(\\omega'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at that point. Thus\n\n\\[\n\\omega' \\text{ is the line through }B' \\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nFrom (2.7) the circle \\(\\ell'\\) passes through \\(A\\) and is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at \\(E'\\).  \nFrom (2.9) the line \\(\\omega'\\) also passes through \\(A\\) and is tangent to the same ray at the same point \\(E'\\).  \nTherefore \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) are tangent to each other at \\(E'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9  Pull\u2011back to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves tangency at points distinct from the centre of inversion.  \nSince \\(E'\\neq A\\), the pre\u2011images of \\(\\ell'\\) and \\(\\omega'\\) are tangent at the pre\u2011image of \\(E'\\), which is precisely the point \\(E\\) (the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n\nHence the pre\u2011image of \\(\\ell'\\), namely the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), is tangent to the pre\u2011image of \\(\\omega'\\), i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\] \u220e"
