r from the origin onto \\(C'D'\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis; write \\(P'=(p,0)\\) for some real \\(p\\).\n\nThe intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) (the set \\(\\{(t,0)\\mid t>0\\}\\)) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is obtained by solving\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot (t,0)=d_{\\Omega}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; t=\\frac{d_{\\Omega}}{u_{x}} .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly the intersection with \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) gives\n\n\\[\nt=\\frac{d_{\\Gamma}}{v_{x}} .\n\\]\n\nSince the points \\(C',D'\\) lie on the line \\(y=0\\), the normals \\(\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\) are both vertical, i.e. \\(u_{x}=v_{x}=0\\) is impossible; instead the normals are horizontal, so \\(u_{x},v_{x}\\neq0\\). Moreover the symmetry of \\(C',D'\\) with respect to the \\(x\\)-axis forces the two expressions for \\(t\\) to be equal; otherwise the two lines would intersect the axis at different points, contradicting the fact that both \\(C',D'\\) lie on the same horizontal line. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d_{\\Omega}}{u_{x}}=\\frac{d_{\\Gamma}}{v_{x}}=:p .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the two intersection points coincide:\n\n\\[\nE' = (p,0)=F'.\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(E'=F'\\)**. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 Image of the orthocenter\n\nThe three altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_{P}: \\text{ through }P\\text{ perpendicular to }MN,\\\\\n&\\ell_{M}: \\text{ through }M\\text{ perpendicular to }PN,\\\\\n&\\ell_{N}: \\text{ through }N\\text{ perpendicular to }PM .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUnder inversion a line not passing through \\(A\\) becomes a circle through \\(A\\); a line through \\(A\\) stays a line.  \nConsequently the images \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\) of the three altitudes are three circles, each passing through \\(A\\) and through the images \\(P',M',N'\\).\n\nAll three circles contain the common point \\(E'=F'\\) (Lemma\u202f1), because each altitude meets the line \\(AP\\) at the point where the 