ys a line.  \n   * Consequently the images of the three altitudes are three circles \\(\\gamma_{P},\\gamma_{M},\\gamma_{N}\\) all passing through \\(A\\) and through the images \\(P',M',N'\\).  \n   * By Lemma\u202f1 the three circles intersect at the common point \\(E'=F'\\); therefore the image of the orthocenter is  \n     \\[\n     H'=E'=F'.\n     \\]  \n\n6. **Image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be that line. Since \\(\\ell\\) does not contain the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion, its image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle through \\(A\\).  \n   * Parallelism \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) means that the oriented angle \\(\\angle(\\ell,AP)=0\\). Inversion preserves oriented angles, so \\(\\angle(\\ell',AP)=0\\). Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the unique point where the ray meets \\(\\ell'\\). By Lemma\u202f2 that point is \\(H'=E'=F'\\). Thus  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell' \\text{ is the unique circle through }A\\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'.\n     \\]  \n\n7. **Image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * The point \\(B\\) lies on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   * The circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) passes through \\(A\\); its image \\(\\omega'\\) is a line. This line must contain the images of \\(B,E,F\\); consequently it passes through \\(B'\\) and meets the ray \\(AP\\) at the common point \\(E'=F'\\). Moreover, because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same circle, the line \\(\\omega'\\) is tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at that point. Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     \\omega' \\text{ is the line through }B' \\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }E'=F'.\n     \\]  \n\n8. **Tangency in the inverted picture and pull\u2011back.**  \n   * Both \\(\\ell'\\) (a circle) and \\(\\omega'\\) (a line) pass through \\(A\\) and are tangent to the ray \\(AP\\) at the same point \\(E'=F'\\); therefore they are tangent to each other at \