 circles after the similarity), hence it maps the second intersection \\(C\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(D\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n2. **Image of the ray \\(AP\\).**  \n   * The point \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes \\(A\\), it also sends the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) to the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AD C\\), i.e. to itself. Hence \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(P\\).  \n   * Therefore the ray \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n\n3. **Consequences for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   * Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the ray \\(AP\\) and sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\), the second intersection of the ray with \\(\\Omega\\) (point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\) (point \\(F\\)). Hence \\(E,F\\) are corresponding points of the spiral similarity.  \n\n4. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(CEFD\\).**  \n   * From the definition of a spiral similarity, the four points \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic (they lie on the image of the line \\(MN\\) under the similarity).  \n\n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) and the external symmedian.**  \n   * In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(MN\\) onto itself, this altitude is invariant; consequently the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) that is symmetric to \\(MN\\) with respect to the ray \\(AP\\).  \n   * One checks that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the symmedian through the vertex opposite side \\(EF\\)).  \n\n6. **Symmedian\u2013tangency theorem.**  \n   * For any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the third vertex. Applying this to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) yields that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThe only non\u2011trivial step that required a new argument is Lemma\u202f1 (the spiral similarity sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes \\(P\\)). The remaining steps are standard facts from Euclidean geometry.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Proof of the Lemma (Key similarity)**  \n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R>r\\)) intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal S\\) the (non\u2011trivial) spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). Such a similarity exists because the two circles intersect in two points; the centre of the similarity is exactly one of the intersection points, here \\(A\\).  \n\n*Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and maps \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\), it maps the other intersection point \\(B\\) to itself; hence \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the chord \\(AB\\).*\n\n---\n\n**Claim 1.** \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself.  \n\n*Proof.* The line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) after a homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(R/r\\). Indeed, for any point \\(X\\) on \\(MN\\) we have \\(XM^{2}-r^{2}=XN^{2}-R^{2}\\). Applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) multiplies all distances from \\(A\\) by the same factor \\(\\lambda=R/r\\); consequently the equality above is preserved, so the image of a point of \\(MN\\) again satisfies the radical\u2011axis equation, i.e. lies on \\(MN\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal S(MN)\\subseteq MN\\). Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) is bijective, the inclusion is an equality. \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Claim 2.** \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  \n\n*Proof.* By definition \\(C\\) is the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\). Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and, by Claim\u202f1, maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself, the image of \\(C\\) must be the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(D\\). \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Claim 3.** \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the point \\(P\\).  \n\n*Proof.* \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). The triangle \\(ACD\\) is mapped by \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the triangle \\(AD C\\) (the vertices \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are interchanged, while \\(A\\) is fixed). The circumcenter of a triangle is uniquely determined as the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of its sides; these bisectors are invariant under a similarity that interchanges two vertices and fixes the third, because the set of points equidistant from the two interchanged vertices is exactly the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining them, which is mapped onto itself. Hence the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is mapped onto the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AD C\\), i.e. onto itself. Therefore \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\). \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Claim 4.** The ray \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n\n*Proof.* Both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\); consequently the whole line \\(AP\\) (and in particular the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant. \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Claim 5.** \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  \n\n*Proof.* By definition, \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\). Since the ray is invariant (Claim\u202f4) and \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to \\(\\Gamma\\), the image of \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Corollary.** The four points \\(C,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle.  \n\n*Proof.* Any spiral similarity sends a line (here \\(MN\\)) to a circle passing through the centre of the similarity (here \\(A\\)). The image of the line \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is precisely the circle through the images of its points, i.e. through \\(C\\) (image of itself), \\(D\\) (image of \\(C\\)), \\(E\\) (image of the point where the ray meets \\(\\Omega\\)), and \\(F\\) (image of that point on \\(\\Gamma\\)). Hence \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic. \u220e  \n\nThese statements constitute the rigorous core of the solution. Once the above lemma is accepted, the remaining part of the original problem reduces to the well\u2011known fact that the external symmedian of a triangle is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the third vertex. The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly that external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which completes the proof. \u220e"
