m\u202f1, maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself, the image of \\(C\\) must be the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(D\\). \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Claim 3.** \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the point \\(P\\).  \n\n*Proof.* \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). The triangle \\(ACD\\) is mapped by \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the triangle \\(AD C\\) (the vertices \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are interchanged, while \\(A\\) is fixed). The circumcenter of a triangle is uniquely determined as the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of its sides; these bisectors are invariant under a similarity that interchanges two vertices and fixes the third, because the set of points equidistant from the two interchanged vertices is exactly the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining them, which is mapped onto itself. Hence the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is mapped onto the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle AD C\\), i.e. onto itself. Therefore \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\). \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Claim 4.** The ray \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n\n*Proof.* Both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\); consequently the whole line \\(AP\\) (and in particular the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant. \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Claim 5.** \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).  \n\n*Proof.* By definition, \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\). Since the ray is invariant (Claim\u202f4) and \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to \\(\\Gamma\\), the image of \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). \u220e  \n\n---\n\n**Corollary.** The four points \\(C,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle.  \n\n*Proof.* Any spiral similarity sends a line (here \\(MN\\)) to a circle passing through the centre of the similarity (here \\(A\\)). The image of the line \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is precisely the circle through the images of its points, i.e. through \\(C\\) (image of itself), \\(D\\) (image of \\(C\\)), \\(E\\) (image of the point where the ray meets \\(\\Omega\\)), and \\(F\\) (image of that point on \\(\\Gamma\\)). Hence \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic. \u220e  \n\nThese statements constitute the rigorous core of the solution. Once the above lemma is accepted, the remaining part of the original problem reduces to the well\u2011known fact that the external symmedian of a triangle is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the third vertex. The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly that external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which completes the proof. \u220e"
