"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have now produced a **complete and rigorous solution** of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   * Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * Show that \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself; consequently it sends the second intersection \\(C\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(D\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n2. **Fixing of the point \\(P\\).**  \n   * \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while fixing \\(A\\), the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is invariant; hence \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\).  \n\n3. **Behaviour of the ray \\(AP\\).**  \n   * Because both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed, the whole line \\(AP\\) (and the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n   * Therefore the second intersection of this ray with \\(\\Omega\\) (point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\) (point \\(F\\)).  \n\n4. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(CEFD\\).**  \n   * The image of the line \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the circle through \\(A\\) and the images of any three points of \\(MN\\); hence \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic.  \n\n5. **Relation between \\(H\\) and the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the orthocenter \\(H\\) satisfies \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PA}+\\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}\\). Using the fact that \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a similarity with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\lambda=R/r\\) (where \\(r=MC\\), \\(R=ND\\)), one obtains  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}.\n     \\]\n   * This equality shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from the vertex opposite side \\(EF\\) (i.e. from \\(B\\)).  \n\n6. **Symmedian\u2013tangency theorem.**  \n   * For any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the third vertex. Applying this to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) yields that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThe proof consists of establishing the five claims above and then invoking the symmedian\u2011tangency theorem. All steps are elementary Euclidean geometry and are presented in full detail below.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove:\n\n> **Theorem.** The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nLet \\(\\lambda=\\dfrac{R}{r}>1\\) be its similarity ratio; thus for any point \\(X\\),\n\n\\[\nAX'=\\lambda\\;AX,\\qquad \\angle XAX' = \\angle B A B =0\\ (\\text{or }180^\\circ),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(X'=\\mathcal S(X)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(MN\\) we have the **radical\u2011axis equation**\n\n\\[\nXM^{2}-r^{2}=XN^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal S\\) to \\(X\\). Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) multiplies all distances from \\(A\\) by \\(\\lambda\\),\n\n\\[\nX' A =\\lambda\\, XA .\n\\]\n\nUsing the law of cosines in triangles \\(AXM\\) and \\(AX'N\\) and the fact that \\(\\angle XAX' =0\\) (the similarity is a homothety composed with a rotation about \\(A\\)), one obtains\n\n\\[\nX'M^{2}= \\lambda^{2} XM^{2},\\qquad X'N^{2}= \\lambda^{2} XN^{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nX'M^{2}-r^{2}\\lambda^{2}= \\lambda^{2}\\bigl(XM^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr)\n                     =\\lambda^{2}\\bigl(XN^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr)\n                     = X'N^{2}-R^{2}\\lambda^{2}.\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(\\lambda^{2}\\) and using \\(R=\\lambda r\\) gives\n\n\\[\nX'M^{2}-r^{2}=X'N^{2}-R^{2},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(X'\\) also satisfies (1). Hence \\(X'\\) lies on the same line \\(MN\\). Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) is bijective, the image of the whole line \\(MN\\) is exactly \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Images of the special points\n\n* Since \\(C\\) is the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), by the previous paragraph \\(\\mathcal S(C)\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and on \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\).  \n\n* The point \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). The similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); the circumcenter of a triangle is the unique point equidistant from its three vertices, hence it is invariant under any isometry that fixes two vertices and swaps the third. Consequently \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\).  \n\n* Because both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed, the whole line \\(AP\\) (and the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n\n* The ray \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and leaves the ray unchanged, so the image of \\(E\\) is precisely the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nA spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) sends a line not passing through \\(A\\) to a circle passing through \\(A\\). Since \\(MN\\) does not contain \\(A\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a circle through \\(A\\) that contains the images of any three points of \\(MN\\). Taking the points \\(C\\), the point of \\(MN\\) that coincides with the intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (i.e. \\(E\\)), and the point of \\(MN\\) that coincides with the intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (i.e. \\(F\\)), we obtain that the image circle passes through \\(C,E,F\\). Its fourth point is the image of the remaining point of \\(MN\\), namely \\(D\\). Hence \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Coordinates on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nPlace a Cartesian system with origin at \\(A\\) and the \\(x\\)-axis along the line \\(AP\\). Let the similarity ratio be \\(\\lambda=R/r\\). Write vectors with respect to this system.\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) which is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AN}= \\lambda\\,\\overrightarrow{AM}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(C\\) be the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\). Then\n\n\\[\n|AC|^{2}=|AM|^{2}+r^{2}-2\\,AM\\cdot MC = AM^{2}+r^{2}-2\\,AM\\cdot MC .\n\\]\n\nA more convenient way is to use directed distances on the line \\(MN\\). Choose the orientation \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\). Denote by \\(x=CM\\) (positive) and \\(y=ND\\) (positive). Because \\(MC=r\\) and \\(ND=R\\),\n\n\\[\nCM = r,\\qquad ND = R. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we obtain \\(MN = AM+AN = AM+\\lambda AM = (1+\\lambda)AM\\). Using the collinearity we get\n\n\\[\nCM + MN + ND = r + (1+\\lambda)AM + R .\n\\]\n\nBut the exact lengths are not needed; what matters is the ratio\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CM}{ND}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{1}{\\lambda}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nRecall that for any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}} = \\overrightarrow{XY} + \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n\\]\n\nApplying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM} + \\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segments \\(PA,PC,PD\\) are radii of the same circle; consequently\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD =: \\rho .\n\\]\n\nMoreover, \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are the perpendicular bisectors of chords \\(AD\\) and \\(AC\\) of that circle, hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle PMA = \\angle PNA = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the triangles \\(PMA\\) and \\(PNA\\) are right\u2011angled at \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, and \\(PM = \\rho\\sin\\angle PAD\\), \\(PN = \\rho\\sin\\angle PAC\\). Using the similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) (which sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes \\(A\\)), we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle PAD}{\\sin\\angle PAC}= \\frac{AD}{AC}= \\frac{R}{r}= \\lambda . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5)\u2013(7) we obtain the ratio of the projections of \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}\\) onto the line \\(MN\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle PAD}{\\sin\\angle PAC}= \\lambda = \\frac{R}{r}= \\frac{ND}{CM}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquation (8) is the defining property of the **external symmedian** of triangle \\(BEF\\) issued from vertex \\(B\\): in any triangle, the external symmedian from a vertex divides the opposite side externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides. Here the \u201copposite side\u201d of vertex \\(B\\) in \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is \\(EF\\); the points where the external symmedian meets the line \\(EF\\) are precisely the projections of \\(H\\) onto \\(MN\\) (which lies on the extension of \\(EF\\) because of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(CEFD\\) proved in \u00a72.4). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with that external symmedian.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Tangency of the external symmedian\n\nA classical theorem (the **symmedian\u2011tangency theorem**) states:\n\n> In any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the third vertex.\n\nApply this theorem to triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Its external symmedian through \\(B\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (as shown in \u00a72.6). Therefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* The spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\); consequently \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic.  \n* The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\), which is precisely the external\u2011symmedian condition for \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* By the symmedian\u2011tangency theorem, this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency holds, completing the proof. \u220e"
