e B A B =0\\ (\\text{or }180^\\circ),\n> \\]\n> where \\(X'=\\mathcal S(X)\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:**  \n- The existence of a spiral similarity centered at\u202fA mapping one intersecting circle onto the other is correct.  \n- The similarity ratio \\(\\lambda=R/r\\) is correct (since \\(AM=r\\) and \\(AN=R\\)).  \n- The claim \u201c\\(\\angle XAX' = \\angle BAB =0\\)\u201d is **false**. The angle of the spiral similarity equals the angle between the circles at\u202fA, i.e. \\(\\angle MAN\\), not zero. The fact that the two intersection points\u202fA and\u202fB are fixed only tells us that \\(\\angle B A B =0\\) for the specific point\u202fB, not for an arbitrary point\u202fX.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The incorrect angle destroys all subsequent calculations that rely on it.\n\n---\n\n### Section\u202f2.2 \u2013 \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the line\u202fMN onto itself  \n\n> \u201cFor any point\u202fX on the line\u202fMN we have the **radical\u2011axis equation**\n> \\[\n> XM^{2}-r^{2}=XN^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{1}\n> \\]\n> Apply \\(\\mathcal S\\) to\u202fX. Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) multiplies all distances from\u202fA by\u202f\\(\\lambda\\),\n> \\[\n> X'A =\\lambda\\, XA .\n> \\]\n> Using the law of cosines in triangles\u202f\\(AXM\\) and\u202f\\(AX'N\\) and the fact that \\(\\angle XAX' =0\\) (the similarity is a homothety composed with a rotation about\u202fA), one obtains\n> \\[\n> X'M^{2}= \\lambda^{2} XM^{2},\\qquad X'N^{2}= \\lambda^{2} XN^{2}.\n> \\]\n> Consequently\n> \\[\n> X'M^{2}-r^{2}\\lambda^{2}= \\lambda^{2}\\bigl(XM^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr)\n>                      =\\lambda^{2}\\bigl(XN^{2}-R^{2}\\bigr)\n>                      = X'N^{2}-R^{2}\\lambda^{2}.\n> \\]\n> Dividing by \\(\\lambda^{2}\\) and using \\(R=\\lambda r\\) gives\n> \\[\n> X'M^{2}-r^{2}=X'N^{2}-R^{2},\n> \\]\n> i.e. \\(X'\\) also satisfies (1). Hence \\(X'\\) lies on the same line\u202fMN. Because \\(\\mathcal S\\) is bijective, the image of the whole line\u202fMN is exactly\u202fMN.\u201d\n\n**Assessment of each claim:**\n\n1. **Radical\u2011axis equation (1) for points on\u202fMN.**  \n   - The radical axis of two circles is the set of points with equal power, which is a line **perpendicular** to the line of centers\u202fMN. Points on\u202fMN (except possibly one special point) do **not** satisfy \\(XM^{2}-r^{2}=XN^{2}-R^{2}\\). Hence (1) is false for generic\u202fX on\u202fMN.  \n\n2. **Use of \\(\\angle XAX'=0\\) in the law of cosines.**  \n   - As identified in Section\u202f2.1, the angle is not zero. Therefore the derived equalities \\(X'M^{2}=\u03bb^{2}XM^{2}\\) and \\(X'N^{2}=\u03bb^{2}XN^{2}\\) are not justified.  \n\n3. **Conclusion that \\(X'\\) also satisfies (1) and thus lies on\u202fMN.**  \n   - Since both the premise (1) and the intermediate equalities are false, this conclusion is unsupported.  \n\n4. **Statement that the image of the whole line\u202fMN is exactly\u202fMN.**  \n   - The image of a line not passing through the centre of a spiral similarity is a circle through the centre, not the same line. Hence this claim is false.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Errors** \u2013 The entire subsection rests on false premises; the logical chain collapses.\n\n---\n\n### Section\u202f2.3 \u2013 Images of the special points  \n\n> \u201c* Since\u202fC is the second intersection of\u202fMN with\u202f\u03a9, by the previous paragraph \\(\\mathcal S(C)\\) lies on\u202fMN and on\u202f\u0393; therefore \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\).  \n> * The point\u202fP is the circumcenter of\u202f\\(\\triangle ACD\\). The similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes\u202fA and interchanges\u202fC and\u202fD; the circumcenter of a triangle is the unique point equidistant from its three vertices, hence it is invariant under any isometry that fixes two vertices and swaps the third. Consequently \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\).  \n> * Because both\u202fA and\u202fP are fixed, the whole line\u202fAP (and the directed ray from\u202fA through\u202fP) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n> * The ray\u202fAP meets\u202f\u03a9 again at\u202fE; applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends\u202f\u03a9 to\u202f\u0393 and leaves the ray unchanged, so the image of\u202fE is precisely the second intersection of the same ray with\u202f\u0393, namely\u202fF. Thus \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n1. **\\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\).**  \n   - This relies on the (false) claim that \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the line\u202fMN onto itself, which was disproved in Section\u202f2.2. Hence the conclusion is unsupported.  \n\n2. **\\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\).**  \n   - The argument treats \\(\\mathcal S\\) as an isometry (distance\u2011preserving), but a spiral similarity includes a dilation (ratio\u202f\u03bb\u202f\u2260\u202f1). Therefore it is **not** an isometry, and the claim that a point equidistant from three vertices must be fixed is invalid. In fact, \\(\\mathcal S\\) does not generally fix the circumcenter of\u202f\\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\n3. **Line\u202fAP invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).**  \n   - Since \\(\\mathcal S(P)\\neq P\\) (as just argued), the line\u202fAP cannot be invariant.  \n\n4. **\\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\).**  \n   - This step depends on the previous (incorrect) invariance of the ray\u202fAP. Without that, there is no justification that the image of\u202fE is\u202fF.  \n\n**Classification:**  \n- The first bullet contains a **Critical Error** (incorrect mapping of\u202fC).  \n- The second bullet is a **Justification Gap** (the claim that \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes\u202fP is unsupported and, in fact, false).  \n- The third and fourth bullets are **Critical Errors** because they rely on the false statements above.\n\n---\n\n### Section\u202f2.4 \u2013 The quadrilateral\u202f\\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic  \n\n> \u201cA spiral similarity with centre\u202fA sends a line not passing through\u202fA to a circle passing through\u202fA. Since\u202fMN does not contain\u202fA, its image under\u202f\\(\\mathcal S\\) is a circle through\u202fA that contains the images of any three points of\u202fMN. Taking the points\u202fC, the point of\u202fMN that coincides with the intersection of the ray\u202fAP with\u202f\u03a9 (i.e.\u202fE), and the point of\u202fMN that coincides with the intersection of the ray\u202fAP with\u202f\u0393 (i.e.\u202fF), we obtain that the image circle passes through\u202fC,E,F. Its fourth point is the image of the remaining point of\u202fMN, namely\u202fD. Hence\u202fC,E,F,D are concyclic. Denote this circle by\u202f\\(\\omega\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:**  \n\n- The first sentence correctly states a known property: a spiral similarity maps a line not through its centre to a circle through the centre.  \n- However, the subsequent description **confuses the sets of points**:\u202fE and\u202fF lie on the ray\u202fAP, **not** on the line\u202fMN. The phrase \u201cthe point of\u202fMN that coincides with the intersection of the ray\u202fAP with\u202f\u03a9 (i.e.\u202fE)\u201d is contradictory because\u202fE is not on\u202fMN.  \n- Consequently the argument that the image circle contains\u202fC,\u202fE,\u202fF, and\u202fD is **logically incoherent**. The conclusion that quadrilateral\u202fC\u202fE\u202fF\u202fD is cyclic is not established by the given reasoning.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The proof of cyclicity is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### Section\u202f2.5 \u2013 Coordinates on the line\u202fMN  \n\n> \u201cPlace a Cartesian system with origin at\u202fA and the\u202fx\u2011axis along the line\u202fAP. Let the similarity ratio be\u202f\\(\\lambda=R/r\\). Write vectors with respect to this system.  \n> Because\u202fM and\u202fN lie on the line\u202fMN which is invariant under\u202f\\(\\mathcal S\\), we have  \n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{AN}= \\lambda\\,\\overrightarrow{AM}. \\tag{2}\n> \\]  \n> From (2) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> MN = AM+AN = AM+\\lambda AM = (1+\\lambda) AM .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Assessment:**  \n\n1. **Claim that\u202fMN is invariant under\u202f\\(\\mathcal S\\).**  \n   - As shown in Section\u202f2.2, this is false.  \n\n2. **Vector equality \\(\\overrightarrow{AN}=\u03bb\\overrightarrow{AM}\\).**  \n   - Even if the line were invariant, the vectors \\(\\overrightarrow{AN}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{AM}\\) are **not parallel** (the points\u202fA,\u202fM,\u202fN are not collinear). Hence the equality is false both geometrically and algebraically.  \n\n3. **Derivation \\(MN = AM+AN\\).**  \n   - This treats the three points as collinear and adds lengths as if they were directed along the same line, which is not the case. The equality is invalid.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Errors** \u2013 All statements in this subsection are false.\n\n---\n\n### Section\u202f2.6 \u2013 Position of the orthocenter\u202f\\(H\\) of\u202f\\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n> \u201cRecall that for any triangle\u202f\\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter\u202f\\(H_{XYZ}\\),\n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}} = \\overrightarrow{XY} + \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n> \\]\n> Applying this to\u202f\\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives\n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM} + \\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{5}\n> \\]\n> Because\u202fP is the circumcenter of\u202f\\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segments\u202fPA,\u202fPC,\u202fPD are radii of the same circle; consequently\n> \\[\n> PA = PC = PD =: \\rho .\n> \\]\n> Moreover,\u202fPM and\u202fPN are the perpendicular bisectors of chords\u202fAD and\u202fAC of that circle, hence\n> \\[\n> \\angle PMA = \\angle PNA = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6}\n> \\]\n> Thus the triangles\u202fPMA and\u202fPNA are right\u2011angled at\u202fM and\u202fN respectively, and\n> \\[\n> PM = \\rho\\sin\\angle PAD,\\qquad PN = \\rho\\sin\\angle PAC .\n> \\]\n> Using the similarity\u202f\\(\\mathcal S\\) (which sends\u202fC to\u202fD and fixes\u202fA), we have\n> \\[\n> \\frac{\\sin\\angle PAD}{\\sin\\angle PAC}= \\frac{AD}{AC}= \\frac{R}{r}= \\lambda . \\tag{7}\n> \\]\n> From (5)\u2013(7) we obtain the ratio of the projections of\u202f\\(\\overrightarrow{PH}\\) onto the line\u202fMN:\n> \\[\n> \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle PAD}{\\sin\\angle PAC}= \\lambda = \\frac{R}{r}= \\frac{ND}{CM}. \\tag{8}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n1. **Vector formula for orthocenter (5).**  \n   - The formula \\(\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}\\) is correct for any triangle \\(XYZ\\). Hence (5) is valid.  \n\n2. **Equality \\(PA=PC=PD\\).**  \n   - Since\u202fP is the circumcenter of\u202f\\(\\triangle ACD\\), this is correct.  \n\n3. **Claim \u201cPM and\u202fPN are the perpendicular bisectors of chords\u202fAD and\u202fAC\u201d.**  \n   - In the circumcircle of\u202f\\(\\triangle ACD\\), the perpendicular bisector of chord\u202fAD passes through the circumcenter\u202fP, but **does not** necessarily pass through\u202fM. The point\u202fM is the centre of\u202f\u03a9, not the midpoint of\u202fAD. Hence PM is **not** generally the perpendicular bisector of\u202fAD. The same applies to PN and chord\u202fAC. Therefore (6) is false.  \n\n4. **Consequences (right\u2011angled triangles, expressions for\u202fPM and\u202fPN).**  \n   - These follow from the false claim (6); thus they are not justified.  \n\n5. **Equation (7): \\(\\frac{\\sin\u2220PAD}{\\sin\u2220PAC}= \\frac{AD}{AC}= \\frac{R}{r}=\u03bb\\).**  \n   - The first equality \\(\\frac{\\sin\u2220PAD}{\\sin\u2220PAC}= \\frac{AD}{AC}\\) would be true in a triangle where the sides opposite those angles are AD and AC, but AD and AC are chords of the circumcircle of\u202f\\(\\triangle ACD\\); there is no reason for \\(\\frac{AD}{AC}= \\frac{R}{r}\\). In fact, AD and AC depend on the geometry of the intersecting circles, not simply on the radii\u202fR and\u202fr. Hence (7) is false.  \n\n6. **Equation (8) for the ratio \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}\\).**  \n   - This ratio is derived from the previous (false) equalities; consequently (8) is not established.  \n\n**Classification:**  \n- (6) \u2013 **Critical Error** (incorrect perpendicular\u2011bisector claim).  \n- (7) \u2013 **Critical Error** (incorrect ratio).  \n- (8) \u2013 **Critical Error** (conclusion based on false premises).  \n\n---\n\n### Section\u202f2.7 \u2013 Tangency of the external symmedian  \n\n> \u201cA classical theorem (the **symmedian\u2011tangency theorem**) states:\n> > In any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle formed by the two opposite vertices and the third vertex.\n> Apply this theorem to triangle\u202f\\(\\triangle BEF\\). Its external symmedian through\u202fB is exactly the line through\u202fH parallel to\u202fAP (as shown in \u00a72.6). Therefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of\u202f\\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:**  \n\n- The quoted \u201csymmedian\u2011tangency theorem\u201d is **not** a standard theorem in Euclidean geometry. The external symmedian of a triangle is not generally tangent to the circumcircle of a triangle formed by two opposite vertices and the third vertex. No known result supports this statement.  \n\n- Moreover, the identification of the line through\u202fH parallel to\u202fAP with the external symmedian of\u202f\\(\\triangle BEF\\) was never rigorously proved (the ratio (8) that was supposed to characterize the external symmedian is itself unproved).  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The theorem invoked is false, and the application rests on earlier invalid steps.\n\n---\n\n### Section\u202f2.8 \u2013 Conclusion  \n\n> \u201cWe have proved:\n> * The spiral similarity with centre\u202fA sends\u202fC to\u202fD and\u202fE to\u202fF; consequently\u202fC,E,F,D are concyclic.  \n> * The orthocenter\u202fH of\u202f\\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\), which is precisely the external\u2011symmedian condition for\u202f\\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n> * Hence the line through\u202fH parallel to\u202fAP is the external symmedian of\u202f\\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n> * By the symmedian\u2011tangency theorem, this line is tangent to the circumcircle of\u202f\\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:**  \n\n- Each bullet point repeats conclusions that were derived from the flawed arguments above. Since the underlying steps contain critical errors, the final statement is not established.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The conclusion is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Evaluation\n\nThe solution contains numerous **Critical Errors**:\n\n1. Mischaracterisation of the spiral similarity\u2019s rotation angle (Section\u202f2.1).  \n2. Incorrect use of the radical\u2011axis equation for points on\u202fMN (Section\u202f2.2).  \n3. False claim that the spiral similarity maps the line\u202fMN onto itself (Section\u202f2.2).  \n4. Incorrect mapping of points\u202fC,\u202fP,\u202fE under the similarity (Section\u202f2.3).  \n5. Invalid proof of cyclicity of\u202fC\u202fE\u202fF\u202fD (Section\u202f2.4).  \n6. Wrong vector relations involving\u202fA,\u202fM,\u202fN (Section\u202f2.5).  \n7. Incorrect statements about perpendicular bisectors and resulting trigonometric relations (Section\u202f2.6).  \n8. Use of a non\u2011existent \u201csymmedian\u2011tangency theorem\u201d (Section\u202f2.7).  \n\nBecause the logical chain collapses at several early stages, the final claim that the line through\u202fH parallel to\u202fAP is tangent to the circumcircle of\u202f\\(\\triangle BEF\\) is **not proved**. The solution is therefore **invalid**."
