is invariant under any isometry that fixes two vertices and swaps the third. Consequently \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\).  \n\n* Because both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed, the whole line \\(AP\\) (and the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n\n* The ray \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) and leaves the ray unchanged, so the image of \\(E\\) is precisely the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nA spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) sends a line not passing through \\(A\\) to a circle passing through \\(A\\). Since \\(MN\\) does not contain \\(A\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a circle through \\(A\\) that contains the images of any three points of \\(MN\\). Taking the points \\(C\\), the point of \\(MN\\) that coincides with the intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (i.e. \\(E\\)), and the point of \\(MN\\) that coincides with the intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (i.e. \\(F\\)), we obtain that the image circle passes through \\(C,E,F\\). Its fourth point is the image of the remaining point of \\(MN\\), namely \\(D\\). Hence \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Coordinates on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nPlace a Cartesian system with origin at \\(A\\) and the \\(x\\)-axis along the line \\(AP\\). Let the similarity ratio be \\(\\lambda=R/r\\). Write vectors with respect to this system.\n\nBecause \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) which is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AN}= \\lambda\\,\\overrightarrow{AM}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(C\\) be the second intersection of \\(MN