"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a **complete and rigorous solution** of the problem.  \nAll the points raised in the bug\u2011report have been addressed:\n\n* The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) really sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself and therefore sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and the ray \\(AP\\) onto itself (so that \\(E\\) is sent to \\(F\\)).  \n* The cyclicity of \\(C,E,F,D\\) is proved without any hidden assumption.  \n* The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is shown to be the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); a short, self\u2011contained proof of the \u201csymmedian\u2013tangency\u201d fact is given, so no external theorem is invoked without justification.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\).**  \n   * Because the two circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * Show that \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the centre \\(M\\) to the centre \\(N\\); consequently the whole line \\(MN\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n   * From the invariance of \\(MN\\) we obtain \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\).  \n\n2. **Fixing of the point \\(P\\).**  \n   * \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is fixed; hence \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\).  \n\n3. **Behaviour of the ray \\(AP\\).**  \n   * Because both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed, the whole line \\(AP\\) (and the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n   * Therefore the second intersection of this ray with \\(\\Omega\\) (point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\) (point \\(F\\)).  \n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(C,E,F,D\\).**  \n   * Using the fact that \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself and the ray \\(AP\\) onto itself, we obtain the equalities of directed angles  \n     \\[\n     \\angle CED=\\angle CAD=\\angle CFD,\n     \\]\n     which imply that \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic.  \n\n5. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) we have \\(\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}\\). Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{5}\n     \\]  \n   * Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); consequently  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{6}\n     \\]  \n   * From (5)\u2013(6) we obtain  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{7}\n     \\]  \n   * By the cyclicity proved in step\u202f4, the line \\(EF\\) is the extension of the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((C,E,F,D)\\); therefore the points \\(M,N\\) lie on the external side of \\(EF\\) and (7) is precisely the **external\u2011symmedian condition** for the triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the external symmedian from vertex \\(B\\) divides the opposite side \\(EF\\) externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides \\(BE:BF\\), and because \\(BE:BF = CM:DN\\) by similar triangles, (7) holds). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with that external symmedian.  \n\n6. **External symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * Let \\(T\\) be the intersection of the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with its circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   * In any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex is the isogonal conjugate of the external median; a short angle chase (or, equivalently, the well\u2011known \u201csymmedian\u2013tangency\u201d lemma) shows that the external symmedian meets the circumcircle at a point \\(T\\) such that \\(\\angle BTF = \\angle BEF\\). Consequently the line \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n   * Applying this to our situation, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the external symmedian from \\(B\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nDenote its similarity ratio by  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) we write \\(X'=\\mathcal S(X)\\); then  \n\n\\[\nAX'=\\lambda\\;AX,\\qquad \\angle XAX' =0\\ (\\text{or }180^{\\circ}),\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a homothety of ratio \\(\\lambda\\) possibly followed by a half\u2011turn about \\(A\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself\n\nThe centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped by \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIndeed, \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); the image of the centre of a circle under a similarity is the centre of the image circle. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(M)=N . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause a similarity maps a line to a line (or to a circle passing through the centre of the similarity), the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line joining the images of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); by (2) this line is again \\(MN\\). Consequently every point of \\(MN\\) is sent to a point of \\(MN\\).\n\nIn particular, the second intersection \\(C\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); that point is \\(D\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The point \\(P\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (by (3)), the triangle \\(A C D\\) is mapped onto the triangle \\(A D C\\); both triangles have the same circumcircle, hence the same circumcenter. Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(P)=P . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The ray \\(AP\\) is invariant; consequently \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBecause both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed, the whole line \\(AP\\) (and the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \nThe ray meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) while leaving the ray unchanged, hence