ties of directed angles \\(\\angle CED = \\angle CAD = \\angle CFD\\). \u2026 Hence \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic.\u201d (Step\u202f5)  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 both (3) and (5) are unproved; the angle equalities and the resulting concyclicity are therefore invalid.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); consequently the right triangles \\(PMA\\) and \\(PNA\\) are similar, whence \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\).\u201d (Step\u202f6)  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the claim that \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\) is false (they are generally of different lengths), and the assertion that \\(\\triangle PMA\\) and \\(\\triangle PNA\\) are right and similar is unfounded. The derived ratio \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) is therefore unjustified.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cComparing (10) and (12) we see that the point \\(X\\) defined by the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2026 satisfies (11). Hence that line **is** the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d (Step\u202f7)  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 this conclusion depends on the false ratio (10) from Step\u202f6 and the unproved cyclicity from Step\u202f5; consequently the identification of the line as an external symmedian is unsupported.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cThe external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\). \u2026 Thus the line \\(BT\\) \u2026 is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\u201d (Step\u202f8)  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the external symmedian of a triangle is **not** generally tangent to its circumcircle; the claimed tangency lacks justification and is false in general.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Definition of the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n> Denote its similarity ratio by \\(\\lambda=\\frac{R}{r}>1\\).  \n> For any point \\(X\\) we write \\(X'=\\mathcal S(X)\\); then  \n> \\[\n> AX'=\\lambda\\;AX,\\qquad \\angle XAX' =0\\ (\\text{or }180^{\\circ}),\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe existence of a spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) sending one intersecting circle to the other is correct. However, the description \u201c\\(\\angle XAX' =0\\) (or \\(180^\\circ\\))\u201d is false. A spiral similarity is, in general, a composition of a dilation (ratio \\(\\lambda\\)) **and a rotation** by some angle \\(\\theta\\neq 0\\) (the angle between the two circles at \\(A\\)). Thus (1) mischaracterises \\(\\mathcal S\\) as a pure homothety (or a half\u2011turn). This factual mistake is a **Critical Error** because later arguments rely on the (incorrect) claim that \\(\\mathcal S\\) preserves directions through \\(A\\).\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Claim that \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself\n\n> \u201cThe centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped by \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n> Indeed, \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); the image of the centre of a circle under a similarity is the centre of the image circle. Hence  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal S(M)=N . \\tag{2}\n> \\]  \n> Because a similarity maps a line to a line (or to a circle passing through the centre of the similarity), the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line joining the images of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); by (2) this line is again \\(MN\\). Consequently every point of \\(MN\\) is sent to a point of \\(MN\\).  \n> In particular, the second intersection \\(C\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); that point is \\(D\\). Thus  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal S(C)=D . \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe first part, \\(\\mathcal S(M)=N\\), is correct: the image of the centre of a circle under a similarity that maps the circle onto another is the centre of the image circle.  \nThe subsequent claim that the image of line \\(MN\\) is the line joining the images of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) is correct **provided we know the image of \\(N\\)**. However, only \\(\\mathcal S(M)=N\\) is established; we have no information about \\(\\mathcal S(N)\\). In general, \\(\\mathcal S(N)\\neq M\\). Therefore we cannot conclude that the image of line \\(MN\\) is the same line \\(MN\\). Consequently the statement \u201cevery point of \\(MN\\) is sent to a point of \\(MN\\)\u201d is unjustified.  \nBecause the invariance of line \\(MN\\) is not proved, the deduction \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\) (which uses the fact that the image of \\(C\\) must lie on the image of \\(MN\\)) is also unsupported. This constitutes a **Critical Error** that invalidates all later arguments that depend on \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\).\n\n### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Claim that \\(P\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\)\n\n> \u201c\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n> Since \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (by (3)), the triangle \\(A C D\\) is mapped onto the triangle \\(A D C\\); both triangles have the same circumcircle, hence the same circumcenter. Therefore  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal S(P)=P . \\tag{4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe reasoning presumes that \\(\\mathcal S\\) indeed interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). This relies on the false claim \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\) from Step\u202f2. Without that, we cannot assert that triangle \\(ACD\\) is mapped to triangle \\(ADC\\). Hence the conclusion \\(\\mathcal S(P)=P\\) is not justified. This is a **Critical Error** (dependent on the earlier error).\n\n### Step\u202f4 \u2013 Invariance of the ray \\(AP\\) and the relation \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed, the whole line \\(AP\\) (and the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n> The ray meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) while leaving the ray unchanged, hence the image of \\(E\\) is precisely the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). Thus  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal S(E)=F . \\tag{5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe invariance of line \\(AP\\) follows only if \\(P\\) is indeed fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\). As shown in Step\u202f3, the fixation of \\(P\\) is unproved. Therefore the claim that the ray \\(AP\\) is invariant is unsupported, and the subsequent identification \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\) is also unsupported. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n### Step\u202f5 \u2013 Concyclicity of \\(C,E,F,D\\)\n\n> \u201cFrom (3) and (5) we have the equalities of directed angles  \n> \\[\n> \\angle CED = \\angle CAD = \\angle CFD . \\tag{6}\n> \\]  \n> Equality (6) is exactly the criterion for concyclicity of the four points \\(C,E,F,D\\). Hence they lie on a common circle, which we denote by \\(\\omega\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBoth (3) and (5) are unproved (see Steps\u202f2 and\u202f4). Consequently the angle equalities in (6) have no foundation. The conclusion that \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic is therefore unjustified. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 Relation involving the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\n> \u201cFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) we have the vector identity  \n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}. \\tag{7}\n> \\]  \n> Applying (7) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{8}\n> \\]  \n> Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); consequently the right triangles \\(PMA\\) and \\(PNA\\) are similar, whence  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{1}{\\lambda}. \\tag{9}\n> \\]  \n> Projecting (8) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (9) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{10}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The vector identity (7) is correct: in any triangle the vector from a vertex to the orthocenter equals the sum of the vectors from that vertex to the other two vertices. Hence (8) follows correctly.\n\n* The subsequent claim \u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\)\u201d is **false**. In a circle with centre \\(P\\), the central angles subtended by chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are \\(\\angle APC\\) and \\(\\angle APD\\); there is no reason for these to be equal unless the chords have equal length, which is not guaranteed (indeed, the spiral similarity sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) with ratio \\(\\lambda\\neq1\\)).  \n\n* The statement \u201cconsequently the right triangles \\(PMA\\) and \\(PNA\\) are similar\u201d is also incorrect. There is no justification that either triangle is right, nor that they are similar.  \n\n* Therefore the derived ratio \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) is unfounded. This makes (9) and consequently (10) unjustified. This constitutes a **Critical Error**.\n\n### Step\u202f7 \u2013 Identification of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) as an external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n> \u201cFrom step\u202f5 the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\) together with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \n> Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of \\(\\omega\\) with the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(EF\\) is the extension of the chord \\(CD\\); consequently the line \\(MN\\) meets the side \\(EF\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) **externally**.  \n> \u2026  \n> Comparing (10) and (12) we see that the point \\(X\\) defined by the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which meets \\(MN\\) at the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) onto \\(MN\\)) satisfies (11). Hence that line **is** the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThis step depends crucially on:\n\n1. The cyclic quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) (Step\u202f5) \u2013 which is unproved.\n2. The ratio \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\) from (10) \u2013 which is unproved (Step\u202f6).\n\nSince both premises are false, the deduction that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is unsupported. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n### Step\u202f8 \u2013 Claim that the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is tangent to its circumcircle\n\n> \u201cLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with its circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n> Because the external symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external median, a standard angle chase gives  \n> \\[\n> \\angle BTF = \\angle BEF .\n> \\]  \n> Thus the line \\(BT\\) makes with the chord \\(BF\\) the same angle as the tangent at \\(F\\) to the circle \\((BEF)\\); consequently \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe statement that the **external symmedian** of a triangle is tangent to its circumcircle is **incorrect**. In a general triangle, the (internal) symmedian meets the circumcircle at a point distinct from the tangency point; the external symmedian is not tangent either. The argument presented (\u201cexternal symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external median\u2026\u201d) does not lead to a tangency conclusion. Hence this step contains a **Critical Error**.\n\n### Step\u202f9 \u2013 Conclusion\n\n> \u201cWe have proved \u2026 Hence the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nSince the preceding steps contain multiple critical errors, the final conclusion is not established. The proof fails.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution contains **multiple critical errors** that break the logical chain at several early stages (incorrect description of the spiral similarity, false claim that line \\(MN\\) is invariant, unproved fixation of point \\(P\\), erroneous ratio involving \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\), and a false statement about the tangency of an external symmedian). Consequently the argument does **not** constitute a valid proof of the problem statement."
