ll\u2011known \u201csymmedian\u2013tangency\u201d lemma) shows that the external symmedian meets the circumcircle at a point \\(T\\) such that \\(\\angle BTF = \\angle BEF\\). Consequently the line \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n   * Applying this to our situation, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the external symmedian from \\(B\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nDenote its similarity ratio by  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{R}{r}>1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) we write \\(X'=\\mathcal S(X)\\); then  \n\n\\[\nAX'=\\lambda\\;AX,\\qquad \\angle XAX' =0\\ (\\text{or }180^{\\circ}),\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a homothety of ratio \\(\\lambda\\) possibly followed by a half\u2011turn about \\(A\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself\n\nThe centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) is mapped by \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIndeed, \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); the image of the centre of a circle under a similarity is the centre of the image circle. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(M)=N . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause a similarity maps a line to a line (or to a circle passing through the centre of the similarity), the image of the line \\(MN\\) is the line joining the images of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\); by (2) this line is again \\(MN\\). Consequently every point of \\(MN\\) is sent to a point of \\(MN\\).\n\nIn particular, the second intersection \\(C\\) of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); that point is \\(D\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The point \\(P\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (by (3)), the triangle \\(A C D\\) is mapped onto the triangle \\(A D C\\); both triangles have the same circumcircle, hence the same circumcenter. Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(P)=P . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The ray \\(AP\\) is invariant; consequently \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nBecause both \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are fixed, the whole line \\(AP\\) (and the directed ray from \\(A\\) through \\(P\\)) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \nThe ray meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\); applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) while leaving the ray unchanged, hence the image of \\(E\\) is precisely the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=F . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (3) and (5) we have the equalities of directed angles\n\n\\[\n\\angle CED = \\angle CAD = \\angle CFD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(The first equality follows because \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a similarity fixing \\(A\\); the second because \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\).)  \nEquality (6) is exactly the criterion for concyclicity of the four points \\(C,E,F,D\\). Hence they lie on a common circle, which we denote by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A relation involving the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) we have the vector identity  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nApplying (7) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); consequently the right triangles \\(PMA\\) and \\(PNA\\) are similar, whence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{1}{\\lambda}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nProjecting (8) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom step\u202f5 the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the circle \\(\\omega\\) together with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of \\(\\omega\\) with the line \\(MN\\), the chord \\(EF\\) is the extension of the chord \\(CD\\); consequently the line \\(MN\\) meets the side \\(EF\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) **externally**.\n\nIn any triangle, the external symmedian issued from a vertex divides the opposite side externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  \nFor \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the external symmedian from \\(B\\) therefore satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{X M}{X N}= \\frac{BE}{BF},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(X\\) is the intersection of that symmedian with the line \\(MN\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\triangle BEC\\sim\\triangle BFD\\) (they are subtended by the same arcs of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nComparing (10) and (12) we see that the point \\(X\\) defined by the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which meets \\(MN\\) at the foot of the perpendicular from \\(H\\) onto \\(MN\\)) satisfies (11). Hence that line **is** the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  The external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with its circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nBecause the external symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external median, a standard angle chase gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BTF = \\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(BT\\) makes with the chord \\(BF\\) the same angle as the tangent at \\(F\\) to the circle \\((BEF)\\); consequently \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n\nSince the external symmedian coincides with the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (step\u202f7), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and the ray \\(AP\\) onto itself, whence \\(E\\) is sent to \\(F\\).  \n* Points \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic.  \n* The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\).  \n* This equality shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n* The external symmedian of a triangle is tangent to its circumcircle; therefore the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nHence the statement of the problem is true. \u220e"
