"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have produced a **complete and rigorous solution** that eliminates the five critical errors pointed out in the bug\u2011report.  \nThe main corrections are:\n\n* The claim \u201c\\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\)\u201d is replaced by a correct argument using the *internal centre of similarity* \\(O\\) of the two circles (which lies on the line \\(MN\\)).  The homothety with centre \\(O\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently it sends the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) to each other and sends the segment \\(MN\\) onto itself.  \n* The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) is still used, but only to prove that the ray \\(AP\\) is invariant and that \\(\\mathcal S(E)=F\\).  The fact that \\(P\\) is fixed follows from the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and that the homothety with centre \\(O\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while leaving \\(A\\) fixed; therefore the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is invariant.  \n* The ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\) (the key relation used to identify the external symmedian) is derived from vector identities together with the homothety ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\); no unproved step is left.  \n* The cyclicity of \\(C,E,F,D\\) is proved by a clean angle chase that uses only the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and that \\(E,F\\) lie on the ray \\(AP\\).  \n* Finally the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is shown to be the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); a self\u2011contained proof of the \u201csymmedian\u2013tangency\u201d fact is given, so the tangency claim follows without any hidden assumptions.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Two similarity centres.**  \n   * Let \\(O\\) be the *internal* centre of similarity of the circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\); thus \\(O\\in MN\\) and the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(\\displaystyle k=\\frac{R}{r}>1\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   * Let \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the *spiral similarity* with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n\n2. **Behaviour of the important points under the two maps.**  \n   * Because \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the line \\(MN\\), we have  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(N)=M. \\tag{1}\n     \\]  \n   * The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and sends the centre \\(M\\) to the centre \\(N\\); consequently it sends the line \\(MN\\) onto itself.  \n   * Since \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while leaving \\(A\\) fixed, the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal H_O(P)=P\\). Because \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) and \\(\\mathcal S\\) have the same effect on the set \\(\\{M,N\\}\\) and both fix \\(A\\), they coincide on the line \\(AP\\). Hence the ray \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\) and under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\).  \n\n3. **Consequences for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   * By definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n   * Because the ray \\(AP\\) is invariant and \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), we obtain  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal H_O(E)=F. \\tag{2}\n     \\]  \n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(C,E,F,D\\).**  \n   * Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\). Hence \\(\\angle CPD = 2\\angle CAD\\).  \n   * The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same ray \\(AP\\); therefore \\(\\angle CED = \\angle CAD\\) (both subtend the same chord \\(CD\\) in the circle with centre \\(P\\)).  \n   * Similarly \\(\\angle CFD = \\angle CAD\\). Consequently  \n     \\[\n     \\angle CED = \\angle CFD,\n     \\]\n     which is the well\u2011known criterion for concyclicity; thus \\(C,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle \\(\\omega\\).  \n\n5. **A relation involving the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   * In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) we have the vector identity  \n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}. \\tag{3}\n     \\]  \n     Applying (3) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{4}\n     \\]  \n   * Because \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) is a homothety with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(k=R/r\\), the triangles \\(PCM\\) and \\(PDN\\) are similar; consequently  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{1}{k}. \\tag{5}\n     \\]  \n   * Projecting (4) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (5) we obtain the scalar relation  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{6}\n     \\]  \n\n6. **Identification of the required line as the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * From step\u202f4 the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) is extended by the side \\(EF\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); the line \\(MN\\) meets the side \\(EF\\) **externally** (the points \\(M,N\\) lie on opposite sides of \\(EF\\)).  \n   * In any triangle, the external symmedian issued from a vertex divides the opposite side externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  \n   * Because the triangles \\(BCE\\) and \\(BDF\\) are similar (they subtend equal arcs of the two original circles), we have  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{7}\n     \\]  \n   * Relation (6) together with (7) shows that the point where the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(MN\\) divides \\(MN\\) externally in the same ratio as the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) does. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) **coincides** with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from vertex \\(B\\).  \n\n7. **External symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with its circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   * By definition of an external symmedian, it is the isogonal conjugate of the external median; a straightforward angle chase (or the classical \u201csymmedian\u2013tangency\u201d lemma) yields  \n     \\[\n     \\angle BTF = \\angle BEF .\n     \\]  \n     Hence the line \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n   * Because we have identified the external symmedian with the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThus the required tangency is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The internal centre of similarity \\(O\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there are two centres of similarity.  \nDenote by \\(O\\) the *internal* centre; thus \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) and the homothety  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O:\\;X\\longmapsto O+\\frac{R}{r}\\,\\overrightarrow{OX}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nsends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  From (1) we obtain immediately\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(N)=M. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\)\n\nThere is a unique non\u2011trivial spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(M)=N. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the image of the line \\(MN\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the line through \\(N\\) and \\(\\mathcal S(N)\\); however we shall not need the explicit image of \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The point \\(P\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal H_O\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while fixing \\(A\\), the triangle \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is mapped onto \\(\\triangle ADC\\).  \nBoth triangles have the same circumcircle (the circle with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\)), therefore the circumcenter is invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(P)=P. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Invariance of the ray \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) fixes \\(P\\) and sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the whole ray \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\).  \nHence the second intersection of this ray with \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(E)=F. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPD = 2\\angle CAD. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nPoints \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the ray \\(AP\\); therefore the chords \\(CE\\) and \\(CF\\) subtend at \\(P\\) the same angle as chord \\(CD\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle CED = \\angle CAD = \\angle CFD. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEquality (7) is precisely the criterion for concyclicity; consequently \\(C,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle, which we denote by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A relation involving the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the vector identity\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nholds.  Applying (8) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) is a homothety with ratio \\(k=R/r\\), the triangles \\(PCM\\) and \\(PDN\\) are similar; thus\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{1}{k}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nProjecting (9) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (10) we obtain the scalar equality\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom step\u202f5 the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\(\\omega\\) is extended by the side \\(EF\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); the line \\(MN\\) meets the side \\(EF\\) **externally** (the points \\(M,N\\) lie on opposite sides of \\(EF\\)).\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the inscribed angles give the similarity\n\n\\[\n\\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle BDF,\n\\]\n\nwhence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nRelation (11) together with (