ore rests on an unfounded similarity and ratio.  \n\n**Conclusion:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the similarity and the derived ratio are unjustified.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f8 \u2013 The external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n> \u201cLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with its circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n> By definition, the external symmedian is the isogonal conjugate of the external median; a direct angle chase gives  \n> \\(\\displaystyle \\angle BTF = \\angle BEF .\\)  \n> Hence the line \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n> Since we have identified the external symmedian with the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \n- The statement that the external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle at its second intersection point is **not a standard theorem**; the usual property is that the *internal* symmedian meets the circumcircle at the point where the tangents at the other two vertices intersect.  \n- The solution merely says \u201ca direct angle chase gives \u2026\u201d without presenting the chase. Hence the claim is **unproved**. This constitutes a **Justification Gap**.  \n\n**Conclusion:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 the tangency claim is asserted without rigorous argument.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution collapses at several early stages (Steps\u202f1,\u202f3,\u202f4,\u202f5,\u202f6,\u202f7) due to **critical errors** that invalidate the logical chain. Even if one were to overlook those, the final step (Step\u202f8) lacks a proper justification. Consequently the proof does **not** establish the required result."
