le BDF\\) (both subtend equal arcs of the original circles) we get  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{3}\n     \\]  \n   * Equation (2) together with (3) shows that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(MN\\) at a point that divides \\(MN\\) externally in the same ratio as the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from \\(B\\).  Hence this line **coincides** with that external symmedian.\n\n6. **External symmedian is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   * Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with its circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \n   * By the well\u2011known symmedian\u2013tangency lemma (proved by an elementary angle chase), \\(\\angle BTF = \\angle BEF\\); therefore \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  \n   * Since the external symmedian has been identified with the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the statement is proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The external centre of similarity \\(O\\)\n\nBecause the circles intersect, there are two similarity centres.  \nDenote by \\(O\\) the **external** centre; thus \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) and the homothety  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O:\\;X\\longmapsto O-\\frac{R}{r}\\,\\overrightarrow{OX}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nhas centre \\(O\\) and **negative** ratio \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(D)=C,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(N)=M. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and interchanges the two intersection points \\(C,D\\) of the circles with the line \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Fixing of \\(P\\) and invariance of the ray \\(AP\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\).  \nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while leaving the **midpoint** of \\(CD\\) fixed (the midpoint is the centre of the homothety\u2019s similarity on the line \\(CD\\)).  Hence the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is invariant, and the unique point on it equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) \u2013 namely \\(P\\) \u2013 is fixed:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(P)=P. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we obtain that the whole line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the second intersection of the invariant line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(E)=F. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nBecause \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nPoints \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the ray \\(AP\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CED = \\angle CAP,\\qquad \n\\angle CFD = \\angle DAP. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8) and (9) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CED = \\angle CFD. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEquality of the two angles subtended by the pair of points \\(C,D\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is precisely the criterion for concyclicity; consequently \\(C,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle, which we denote by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A relation involving the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the vector identity  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nholds (it follows from the definition of the altitudes).  \nApplying (11) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity of triangles \\(PCM\\) and \\(PDN\\) (a direct consequence of (5)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nProjecting (12) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (13) we find  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom the cyclicity of \\(A,B\\) with each of the original circles we have the well\u2011known similarity  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle BDF,\n\\]\n\nbecause the corresponding angles subtend equal arcs of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nEquation (14) together with (15) shows that the point where the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the line \\(MN\\) divides \\(MN\\) **externally** in the same ratio as the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from vertex \\(B\\).  Therefore this line **coincides** with that external symmedian; denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  External symmedian \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\((BEF)\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and let \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is the **external symmedian** from \\(B\\), it is the isogonal conjugate of the external median \\(BM\\) (where \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\)).  An elementary angle chase (or the classical symmedian\u2013tangency lemma) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BTF = \\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nHence the required tangency is proved. \u220e"
