ty preserving angles at \\(B\\); the claim \u201c\\(\\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle BDF\\)\u201d is asserted without proof. Moreover, even if the similarity held, the derived ratio \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\) is incorrect: under the similarity the corresponding sides are \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\), so \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}=1\\), not \\(\\frac{CM}{DN}\\) (which equals \\(\\frac{r}{R}\\neq 1\\) in general). Hence the ratio (15) is false, making the identification of the line through \\(H\\) with the external symmedian baseless. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 External symmedian \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n> \u201cLet \\((BEF)\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and let \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\).  \n> Because \\(\\ell\\) is the **external symmedian** from \\(B\\), it is the isogonal conjugate of the external median \\(BM\\) (where \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(EF\\)). An elementary angle chase (or the classical symmedian\u2013tangency lemma) gives \\(\\angle BTF = \\angle BEF\\). Thus the line \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error #6 \u2013 Misidentification of the tangency property.**  \nIn a triangle, the **tangent** at a vertex is isogonal to the **median**, not to the **symmedian**. The external symmedian from a vertex is not, in general, tangent to the circumcircle; the tangent line at the vertex is the isogonal of the median, while the symmedian is the isogonal of the **cevian** through the vertex that passes through the midpoint of the opposite side\u2019s *tangent* points. Consequently the statement that the external symmedian \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) is false. This is a **Critical Error** that invalidates the final conclusion.\n\n---\n\n### Sum