triangle PMN\\)\n\n> \u201cFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the vector identity  \n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}\n> \\tag{10}\n> \\]  \n> holds (it follows directly from the definition of the altitudes).  \n> Applying (10) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{11}\n> \\]  \n> Projecting (11) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (9) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{12}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis**\n\n* The vector identity (10) is a standard fact about orthocentres and is correct.  \n* Equation (11) follows directly from (10) with \\(X=P\\); this is valid.  \n* The passage from (11) to the ratio (12) uses the ratio \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\) obtained in (9). However, (9) was derived from the **invalid similarity** of Step\u202f4. Since that similarity is not established, the equality \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\) lacks justification. Consequently the derivation of (12) contains a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 The external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the external similarity \\(\\mathcal H_O\\), the inscribed angles give  \n> \\[\n> \\angle CBE = \\angle DBF,\\qquad \n> \\angle BCE = \\angle BDF.\n> \\]  \n> Hence  \n> \\[\n> \\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle BDF, \\tag{13}\n> \\]  \n> and consequently  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{14}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis**\n\n1. **Premise that \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\):** This was claimed in Step\u202f2 via the false statement \\(\\mathcal H_O(E)=F\\). Since that claim is invalid, the subsequent angle equalities have no foundation. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n2. **Similarity \\(\\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle BDF\\):** The similarity is inferred from the (unjustified) angle equalities. Without a valid basis, the similarity is not established \u2013 a **Justification Gap**.\n\n3. **Ratio \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\):** This follows from the (unproved) similarity and from the earlier (unproved) ratio \\(\\frac{CM}{DN}\\). Hence the statement rests on multiple gaps and errors.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f7 \u2013 Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n> \u201cLet \\((BEF)\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and let \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\).  \n> A classical lemma (symmedian\u2013tangency) states that the external symmedian of a triangle is tangent to its circumcircle at the point where it meets the circle again.  An elementary angle chase proves that  \n> \\[\n> \\angle BTF = \\angle BEF,\n> \\]  \n> so \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis**\n\n* The \u201csymmedian\u2013tangency\u201d lemma as stated is **false**: the external symmedian of a triangle is **not** tangent to its circumcircle; the tangent at a vertex is the isogonal line of the symmedian, not the symmedian itself. Therefore the lemma is a **Critical Error**.  \n* Even if the lemma were true, the preceding steps that identified the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the external symmedian rely on the flawed ratio (14). Hence the final tangency claim is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution collapses at several early stages:\n\n* The claim that the external homothety fixes the midpoint \\(K\\) (Step\u202f1) is false.  \n* The derived collinearity \\(P,K,O\\) (Step\u202f2) is incorrect, leading to a cascade of invalid conclusions: \\(\\mathcal H_O(P)=P\\), invariance of line \\(AP\\), and \\(\\mathcal H_O(E)=F\\).  \n* Subsequent geometric deductions (cyclicity of \\(C,E,F,D\\), similarity of triangles, ratio equalities, and identification of a symmedian) all depend on these false premises.  \n* The final \u201csymmedian\u2013tangency\u201d statement is itself incorrect.\n\nBecause the logical chain is broken by **Critical Errors** and several steps lack rigorous justification, the solution does **not** constitute a valid proof of the problem statement."
