  \n   * Apply \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to \\(\\triangle PCM\\).  Points \\(C\\) and \\(M\\) are sent to \\(D\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, while the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\) is fixed.  The composition of \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) with the half\u2011turn about \\(K\\) (which sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) while fixing \\(K\\)) is a similarity with centre \\(P\\).  Hence  \n     \\[\n     \\triangle PCM \\sim \\triangle PDN,\\qquad \n     \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Relation involving the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   * In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) we have the vector identity  \n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}. \\tag{5}\n     \\]  \n     Applying (5) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{6}\n     \\]  \n   * Projecting (6) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (4) yields  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n6. **External symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * Because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of each other under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) and \\(C,D\\) are the corresponding points on the two original circles, the inscribed angles give  \n     \\[\n     \\angle CBE = \\angle DBF,\\qquad \n     \\angle BCE = \\angle BDF,\n     \\]  \n     whence \\(\\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle BDF\\).  Consequently  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{8}\n     \\]  \n   * From (7) and (8) we see that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets \\(MN\\) at a point that divides \\(MN\\) **externally** in the same ratio as the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from \\(B\\).  Therefore this line **coincides** with that external symmedian; denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n7. **Tangency.**  \n   * Let \\((BEF)\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and let