o \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(D)=C,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(N)=M. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the ratio is negative, \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) interchanges the endpoints of the segment \\(CD\\) while leaving its midpoint \\(K\\) fixed; i.e. \\(K\\) is the unique fixed point of \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) on the line \\(CD\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Fixing of the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and of \\(P\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through its midpoint \\(K\\).  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\); hence \\(P\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  Therefore \\(P,K,O\\) are collinear.\n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to \\(P\\) we obtain a point on the line \\(OK\\) that is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (since \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) sends the circle \\((P,PA)\\) onto itself).  The only such point is \\(P\\) itself, so  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(P)=P. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the whole line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\).  Because \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the second intersection of this invariant line with \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(E)=F. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nIn \\(\\triangle ACD\\) the equalities \\(PA=PC=PD\\) imply that \\(\\triangle APC\\) and \\(\\triangle APD\\) are isosceles, whence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle DPA. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the ray \\(AP\\), the oriented angles satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CED = \\angle CPA,\\qquad \n\\angle CFD = \\angle DPA. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and (6) we obtain \\(\\angle CED = \\angle CFD\\); this equality of the angles subtended by the pair of points \\(C,D\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is precisely the condition for concyclicity.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nC,E,F,D\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to \\(\\triangle PCM\\).  By (2) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(M)=N.\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(CD\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal H_O\\).  A half\u2011turn about \\(K\\) (rotation by \\(180^{\\circ}\\)) also sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) while fixing \\(K\\).  The composition of \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) with this half\u2011turn is a similarity whose centre is the point \\(P\\) (the unique point on the line \\(OK\\) that is fixed by both transformations).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle PCM \\sim \\triangle PDN. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity we obtain the ratio of corresponding sides  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Relation involving the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the vector identity  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nholds (it follows directly from the definition of the altitudes).  \nApplying (10) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nProjecting (11) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the external similarity \\(\\mathcal H_O\\), the inscribed angle