e **external** centre of similarity; thus \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) and the homothety  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O:\\;X\\longmapsto O-\\frac{R}{r}\\,\\overrightarrow{OX}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nhas centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(D)=C,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(N)=M. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the ratio is negative, \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) interchanges the endpoints of the segment \\(CD\\) while leaving its midpoint  \n\n\\[\nK\\;=\\;\\text{midpoint of }CD\n\\]\n\nfixed; i.e. \\(\\mathcal H_O(K)=K\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Invariance of the line \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the chords \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(PA\\); equivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA=\\angle DPA. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to the ray \\(PC\\).  By (2) the image of the ray \\(PC\\) is the ray \\(PD\\).  Hence the image of the angle \\(\\angle CPA\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) is \\(\\angle DPA\\).  By (3) the two angles are equal, therefore the ray \\(PA\\) is mapped onto itself.  Consequently the whole line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\).  In particular the second intersection of this line with \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(E)=F. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(\\angle CPA=\\angle DPA\\).  Because \\(E,F\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\), the oriented angles satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CED=\\angle CPA,\\qquad \n\\angle CFD=\\angle DPA. \\tag{5}\n\\]  \n\nHence \\(\\angle CED=\\angle CFD\\); this equality of the angles subtended by the pair of points \\(C,D\\) at the points \\(E,F\\) is precisely the condition for concyclicity.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nC,E,F,D\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Similarity \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\)\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to \\(\\triangle PCM\\).  By (2) we obtain a triangle with vertices \\(D,N\\) and the image of \\(P\\).  The composition of \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) with the half\u2011turn about the fixed point \\(K\\) (which sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) while fixing \\(K\\)) is a similarity whose centre is exactly \\(P\\) (the unique point fixed by both transformations).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN. \\tag{7}\n\\]  \n\nFrom (7) we obtain the ratio of corresponding sides  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the vector identity  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ} \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nholds.  Applying (9) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{10}\n\\]  \n\nProjecting (10) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (8) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause of (6) the quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) is cyclic; together with the fact that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) we obtain the equal inscribed angles  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CBE = \\angle DBF,\\qquad \n\\angle BCE = \\angle BDF.\n\\]  \n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle BCE\\sim\\triangle BDF. \\tag{12}\n\\]  \n\nFrom the similarity we deduce  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{13}\n\\]  \n\nComparing (11) and (13) we see that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in a point that divides \\(MN\\) **externally** in the same ratio as the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from vertex \\(B\\).  Consequently the required line coincides with that external symmedian; denote it by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\((BEF)\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and let \\(T\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\((BEF)\\).  A classical result (the symmedian\u2013tangency lemma) states that the external symmedian of a triangle meets its circumcircle again at the point of tangency of the line through the opposite vertex.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BTF = \\angle BEF,\n\\]  \n\nwhich means that the line \\(BT\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nHence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
