io -R/r would correspond to a composition of a homothety with a half-turn (central symmetry) about O. That would map M to N? Let's check: If we apply a homothety with ratio -R/r about O, then O M = M - O, O N = N - O. We need O N = -R/r * O M. Since O M is vector from O to M, O N is vector from O to N. If O is between M and N, O M points left, O N points right, so O N = -R/r * O M yields O N opposite direction to O M, which is correct. So O internal yields negative ratio -R/r. So O internal is the center of a homothety with negative ratio that maps Omega to Gamma (with orientation reversal). So O internal is the external similarity centre? Actually there are two similarity centres: internal (positive ratio) and external (negative ratio). Some authors call the external similarity centre the one with negative ratio, which lies between the circles (i.e., between the centers). Others call it the internal similarity centre. There's confusion. In geometry, the \"external center of similarity\" is the one that sends one circle to the other with opposite orientation, which indeed lies on the line of centers between them. The \"internal center of similarity\" sends one circle to the other with same orientation, lying outside the segment. However, many textbooks define internal and external differently. Let's check typical definitions: For two circles intersecting, the internal center of similarity is the intersection of the line of centers with the internal common tangents (the tangents that intersect between the circles). The external center of similarity is the intersection of the line of centers with the external common tangents (the tangents that do not intersect between the circles). The internal center lies between the circles, external lies outside. But the homothety mapping one circle to the other with positive ratio is internal? Actually internal common tangents intersect at a point between the circles, which is the internal homothety center. That homothety maps one