ter to a point on the circle is not the angle bisector of the angle between the radii to the other two points. So angle CPA = angle DPA is not generally true. Let's examine: In triangle ACD, P is circumcenter. So PA = PC = PD. So triangle APC is isosceles with PA = PC. Similarly triangle APD is isosceles with PA = PD. But that doesn't guarantee angle CPA = angle DPA. Actually angle CPA is angle between PC and PA. Since PA = PC, triangle APC is isosceles with base AC. So angle CPA is the angle at P opposite side AC. Similarly angle DPA is angle at P opposite side AD. There's no reason these two angles are equal unless AC = AD (i.e., triangle ACD is isosceles with AC = AD). But is that true? Since C and D are symmetric with respect to line MN? Actually C and D are symmetric with respect to O (the similarity center), but not necessarily symmetric with respect to line MN. However, maybe due to the homothety, AC = AD? Let's examine geometry: Points A and B are intersection points of circles Omega and Gamma. C is intersection of line MN with Omega on the far side of M, D is intersection of line MN with Gamma on far side of N. The quadrilateral ACBD is cyclic (since A and B are intersection points). But AC and AD are not necessarily equal. So angle CPA = angle DPA is not generally true. So step 2's claim that chords PC and PD are symmetric with respect to line PA is false. Let's examine more carefully: P is circumcenter of triangle ACD. So P is the intersection of perpendicular bisectors of AC, AD, CD. So PA is perpendicular bisector of CD? Actually the perpendicular bisector of CD passes through P, but not necessarily through A. Wait, the perpendicular bisector of CD is a line through P that is perpendicular to CD. Since A is not necessarily on that line, PA is not necessarily perpendicular to CD. So line PA is not necessarily a symmetry axis of triangle ACD. So angle CPA = angle DPA is not generally true. So step 2 is flawed.\n\nThus there is a critical error at step 2: 