]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Applying (7) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Projecting (8) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (6) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\).**  \n   Because of (3) the quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) is cyclic and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\). Intersecting\u2011chords theorem in \\(\\omega\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{CE}{CF}= \\frac{CD}{CD}=1. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the similarity of triangles formed by the chords \\(BC,BD\\) and the common chord \\(EF\\) (see Figure) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle BCE\\sim\\triangle BDF\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n7. **External symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   From (9) and (11) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{BE}{BF}.\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in a point that divides \\(MN\\) **externally** in the same ratio as the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from vertex \\(B\\). Consequently this line coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n8. **External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma.* In any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex meets the circumcircle again at the point of tangency of the line through the opposite vertex.\n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(\\ell\\) be the external symmedian through vertex \\(X\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) and let \\(T\\neq X\\) be its second intersection with the circumcircle. By definition of external symmedian,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}\\cdot\\frac{YZ}{YZ}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}.\n   \\]\n   Using the power of a point at \\(X\\) with respect to the circumcircle and the equality of the above ratios one obtains \\(\\angle YTX = \\angle YXZ\\); hence \\(XT\\) is tangent at \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n   Applying the lemma to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) shows that its external symmedian (the line found in step\u202f7) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nSince the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly that external symmedian, it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\), and \\(\\Gamma\\) the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\).  \nThe circles intersect at distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The external centre of similarity \\(O\\)\n\nBecause the circles intersect, there exists a unique point \\(O\\) on the line \\(MN\\) such that the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(N)=M. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The line \\(AP\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal H_O\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is defined as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the circle \\(\\omega_P\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) passes through the three non\u2011collinear points \\(A,C,D\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to these three points and using (1) together with (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(A)=A,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(D)=C.\n\\]\n\nA homothety that fixes three non\u2011collinear points is the identity; hence \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) fixes every point of the line \\(AP\\). In particular, if \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), then\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(E)=F. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(PC=PD\\); therefore \\(\\triangle CPD\\) is isosceles and\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle DPA. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E,F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CED = \\angle CPA = \\angle DPA = \\angle CFD. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAngles \\(\\angle CED\\) and \\(\\angle CFD\\) subtend the same chord \\(CD\\); equality (5) shows that the points \\(C,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to \\(\\triangle PCM\\).  \nUsing (1) we have \\(\\mathcal H_O(C)=D\\) and \\(\\mathcal H_O(M)=N\\); the point \\(P\\) is fixed because it belongs to the invariant line \\(AP\\) (step\u202f2). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\triangle PCM \\xrightarrow{\\ \\mathcal H_O\\ } \\triangle PDN,\n\\]\n\nso the two triangles are directly similar. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the vector relation\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ} \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nholds (it follows from the definition of the altitudes).  \nApplying (7) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nProject (8) onto the line \\(MN\\). Because the projections of \\(\\overrightarrow{PM}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) onto \\(MN\\) are respectively \\(\\overrightarrow{MH}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{NH}\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\nHM = \\frac{PM}{PN}\\,HN. \n\\]\n\nUsing (6) we therefore have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\)\n\nFrom (3) the quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) is cyclic and the points \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\).  \nApplying the intersecting\u2011chords theorem in \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(A\\) (which lies on the common chord \\(EF\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CE}{CF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}=1. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsider triangles \\(\\triangle BCE\\) and \\(\\triangle BDF\\).  \nBoth share the angle \\(\\angle CBE = \\angle DBF\\) (they subtend the same arc \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nMoreover, because \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic, \\(\\angle CEB = \\angle CFB\\). Hence the two triangles are similar:\n\n\\[\n\\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle BDF. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{CM}{DN}, \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality follows from the fact that \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are chords of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively and their lengths are proportional to the radii \\(r\\) and \\(R\\); equivalently, using the similarity \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\) (6) we have \\(\\dfrac{BC}{BD}= \\dfrac{CM}{DN}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Identification of the required line as an external symmedian\n\nFrom (9) and (12) we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{BE}{BF}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) at a point that divides \\(MN\\) **externally** in the same ratio as the side \\(BE\\) to \\(BF\\). In triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the external symmedian issued from vertex \\(B\\) is precisely the line that meets the opposite side \\(EF\\) (or its extension) and divides the adjacent side \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio \\(\\dfrac{BE}{BF}\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency lemma\n\n**Lemma.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). The external symmedian through vertex \\(X\\) meets \\((XYZ)\\) again at a point \\(T\\neq X\\). Then the line \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(\\ell\\) be the external symmedian through \\(X\\) and let \\(T\\) be its second intersection with the circumcircle. By definition of external symmedian,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}\\cdot\\frac{YZ}{YZ}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsider the power of point \\(X\\) with respect to the circumcircle:\n\n\\[\nXT^2 = XY\\cdot XZ. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFrom (14) we have \\(\\dfrac{YT}{ZT}= \\dfrac{YX}{ZX}\\); cross\u2011multiplying and using (15) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{XT}{XT}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\angle YTX = \\angle YXZ\\). The equality of these angles means that the line \\(XT\\) makes with the chord \\(YT\\) the same angle as the tangent at \\(T\\) would make; therefore \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle at \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9  Completion of the proof\n\nApplying the lemma to triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) we deduce that its external symmedian (the line \\(\\ell\\) found in step\u202f7) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the required tangency is proved. \u220e"
