wn from it is unsound. Moreover, the preceding steps that were supposed to identify the line through \\(H\\) as the external symmedian are themselves unjustified. Hence the final statement does not follow.\n\n**Conclusion:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the final deduction rests on a false lemma and on earlier invalid steps.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution fails at several foundational points:\n\n1. It incorrectly asserts that the external homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) fixes the intersection point \\(A\\) and the line \\(AP\\) (Step\u202f2.2).  \n2. It derives false angle equalities and consequently an unfounded cyclic quadrilateral (Step\u202f2.3).  \n3. It assumes similarity of triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) without justification (Step\u202f2.4).  \n4. It misapplies vector projection to obtain an incorrect relation involving the orthocenter (Step\u202f2.5).  \n5. It misuses the intersecting\u2011chords theorem, asserts unsupported angle equalities, and claims an unproved similarity leading to an erroneous ratio (Step\u202f2.6).  \n6. It identifies the desired line as an external symmedian based on the flawed ratio (Step\u202f2.7).  \n7. It invokes a false lemma that confuses symmedians with angle bisectors (Step\u202f2.8).  \n\nBecause these errors break the logical chain, the proof does **not** establish the required tangency. The solution is therefore **invalid**."
