**  \n   Since \\(O\\) lies on \\(AP\\) and \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) while fixing \\(A\\), the cross\u2011ratio \\((C,D;E,F)=-1\\). A harmonic quadruple with the two middle points \\(C,D\\) on a line is always cyclic; thus  \n\n   \\[\n   C,E,F,D\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Similarity \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\).**  \n   Applying \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and using (1) together with the fact that \\(P\\) is fixed (step\u202f2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Applying (6) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Projecting (7) onto the line \\(MN\\) and using (5) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\).**  \n   Because of (3) the quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) is cyclic and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\).  \n   Consider triangles \\(\\triangle BCE\\) and \\(\\triangle BDF\\).  \n   - \\(\\angle CBE=\\angle DBF\\) because both subtend arc \\(AB\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   - \\(\\angle CEB=\\angle DFB\\) because they subtend the same chord \\(CB\\) of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\).  \n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\triangle BCE\\sim\\triangle BDF \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BC}{BD}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   From the similarity (4) we have \\(\\dfrac{BC}{BD}= \\dfrac{CM}{DN}\\) (the chords \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are proportional to the radii of the two circles). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n7. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the external similarity centre \\(O\\), the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(MN\\) divides \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio \\(\\dfrac{HM}{HN}\\). By (8) and (10)\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{BE}{BF}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   In \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the *external* symmedian issued from vertex \\(B\\) meets the opposite side \\(EF\\) (or its extension) at a point whose projection on the line \\(MN\\) divides \\(MN\\) externally in exactly the ratio \\(\\dfrac{BE}{BF}\\). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) coincides with this external symmedian.\n\n8. **External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). The external symmedian through vertex \\(X\\) meets \\((XYZ)\\) again at a point \\(T\\neq X\\). Then the line \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Let \\(\\ell\\) be the external symmedian through \\(X\\) and let \\(T\\) be its second intersection with \\((XYZ)\\). By definition of external symmedian,\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}\\cdot\\frac{YZ}{YZ}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the power of point \\(X\\) with respect to \\((XYZ)\\),\n\n   \\[\n   XT^{2}=XY\\cdot XZ. \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   From (12) we obtain \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{XT}{XT}\\). Substituting into the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YTZ\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin\\angle YTX}{\\sin\\angle ZTX}= \\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZX Y}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\angle YTX=\\angle YXZ\\). The equality of the angle formed by \\(XT\\) with chord \\(YT\\) and the angle subtended by the same chord at the circumference