 any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) we have the vector identity  \n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n   - Applying (5) to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) gives  \n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}.\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n   - Projecting (6) onto line \\(MN\\) and using (4) we obtain  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}.\n     \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\)**  \n   - Because of step\u202f3, quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) is cyclic and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\).  \n   - In triangles \\(\\triangle BCE\\) and \\(\\triangle BDF\\):  \n     * \\(\\angle CBE=\\angle DBF\\) (both subtend arc \\(AB\\) of the original circles).  \n     * \\(\\angle CEB=\\angle DFB\\) (both subtend chord \\(CB\\) of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\)).  \n   - Hence \\(\\triangle BCE\\sim\\triangle BDF\\) and  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BC}{BD}.\n     \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n   - From similarity (3) we have \\(\\dfrac{BC}{BD}= \\dfrac{CM}{DN}\\) (the chords \\(BC,BD\\) are proportional to the radii of the two circles). Therefore  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}.\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n\n7. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)**  \n   - Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) passes through the external similarity centre \\(O\\), the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(MN\\) divides \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio \\(\\dfrac{HM}{HN}\\).  \n   - By (7) and (9) this ratio equals \\(\\dfrac{BE}{BF}\\).  \n   - In any triangle, the external symmedian from a vertex divides the opposite side (or its extension) externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides. Hence \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the external symmedian issued from vertex \\(B\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n8. **External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency Lemma**  \n\n   *Lemma.* In a triangle \\(XYZ\\) with circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\), the external symmedian through vertex \\(X\\) meets the circumcircle again at a point \\(T\\neq X\\). Then the line \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Let the external symmedian through \\(X\\) intersect side \\(YZ\\) (or its extension) at \\(S\\). By definition of external symmedian,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}\\cdot\\frac{YZ}{YZ}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   Using the power of point \\(X\\) with respect to \\((XYZ)\\) we have \\(XT^{2}=XY\\cdot XZ\\). Applying the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YTZ\\) and (10) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin\\angle YTX}{\\sin\\angle ZTX}= \\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}.\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\angle YTX=\\angle YXZ\\); the angle between \\(XT\\) and chord \\(YT\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the circumference, which is precisely the tangency condition. \u220e\n\n9. **Conclusion**  \n   - From step\u202f7 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   - By the Lemma, this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at its second intersection with the circle.  \n   - Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\), and \\(\\Gamma\\) the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\).  \nThe circles intersect at distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n\\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. External similarity centre\n\nThe two circles intersect, therefore there exists a unique **external centre of similarity** \\(O\\) on line \\(MN\\).  \nThe homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(N)=M. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The line \\(AP\\) passes through \\(O\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the circle \\(\\omega_P\\) centred at \\(P\\) passes through \\(A,C,D\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to the set \\(\\{A,C,D\\}\\) we obtain the same set because \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) while fixing \\(A\\).  \nA homothety fixing three non\u2011collinear points is the identity; therefore it fixes the whole line \\(AP\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nO\\in AP. \\tag{2a}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\), the image of \\(E\\) under \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and on the same line \\(AP\\); denote this image by \\(F\\). Thus\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(E)=F,\\qquad F\\in AP,\\;F\\neq A. \\tag{2b}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (2a)\u2013(2b) the four points \\(C,D,E,F\\) are related by the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) that sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) to \\(F\\) while fixing the line \\(AP\\).  \nTherefore the cross\u2011ratio \\((C,D;E,F)=-1\\); i.e. the quadruple is harmonic.  \nA harmonic quadruple with the two middle points \\(C,D\\) on a line is always cyclic, so\n\n\\[\nC,E,F,D\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Similarity \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\)\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to triangle \\(PCM\\).  \nUsing (1) and the fact that \\(P\\) is fixed (because \\(P\\) lies on the line of the homothety, see (2a)), we obtain the image triangle \\(PDN\\).  \nSince a homothety preserves angles, we have\n\n\\[\n\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity we deduce the ratio of corresponding sides:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nApplying (6) to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nProject (7) onto the direction of \\(MN\\).  \nLet \\(M\\) be taken as origin on line \\(MN\\); then the scalar components of \\(\\overrightarrow{PM}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) along \\(MN\\) are proportional to \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) respectively, with the same sign because \\(M,N\\) lie on the same side of \\(P\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\)\n\nBecause of (3), quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) is cyclic and \\(C,D\\) lie on line \\(MN\\).  \n\nConsider triangles \\(\\triangle BCE\\) and \\(\\triangle BDF\\).\n\n*Angle equality*  \n- \\(\\angle CBE\\) and \\(\\angle DBF\\) both subtend the same arc \\(AB\\) of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); hence \\(\\angle CBE=\\angle DBF\\).  \n- \\(\\angle CEB\\) and \\(\\angle DFB\\) subtend chord \\(CB\\) of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\); thus \\(\\angle CEB=\\angle DFB\\).\n\nTherefore the two