"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 The previous answer contained several unjustified claims (in particular the claim that the external similarity centre \\(O\\) lies on \\(AP\\), the use of a cross\u2011ratio for non\u2011collinear points, and the unproved \u201cexternal\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency\u201d lemma). Consequently the logical chain was broken and the proof was not complete.\n\n**b. Method Sketch** \u2013  \nThe correct proof proceeds as follows.\n\n1. **The external similarity centre \\(O\\) and the line \\(AP\\).**  \n   Show that the external similarity centre \\(O\\) of the two circles lies on the line \\(AP\\) by using the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and that the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) pass through the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\). From this we obtain that the two perpendicular bisectors intersect on the line \\(MN\\); their intersection is exactly the external similarity centre \\(O\\). Hence \\(O\\in AP\\).\n\n2. **Homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\).**  \n   The homothety with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal H_O(N)=M,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   and, because \\(O\\in AP\\), it also sends the second intersection \\(E\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(F\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\):\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_O(E)=F. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Cyclicity of \\(C,E,F,D\\).**  \n   From (1)\u2013(2) the quadrilateral \\(C,E,F,D\\) is the image of the degenerate quadrilateral \\(C,D,D,C\\) under a homothety; therefore the cross\u2011ratio \\((C,D;E,F)=-1\\).  \n   A harmonic quadruple whose two middle points lie on a line is always cyclic; hence \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic.  \n\n4. **Similarity \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\).**  \n   Applying \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and using (1) together with the fact that \\(P\\) is fixed (because \\(P\\) lies on the line of the homothety, see step\u202f1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN,\n   \\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\n   \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the following vector identity holds:\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Applying (4) to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Taking scalar products of (5) with the unit vector along \\(MN\\) and using (3) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).**  \n   Because \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic and \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the power of point \\(B\\) with respect to this circle gives\n   \\[\n   BC\\cdot BD = BE\\cdot BF. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   From the similarity \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\) we have \\(\\dfrac{BC}{BD}= \\dfrac{CM}{DN}= \\dfrac{r}{R}\\). Substituting this into (7) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n7. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Since \\(O\\in AP\\) (step\u202f1), the intersection \\(X=\\ell\\cap MN\\) divides the segment \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio\n   \\[\n   \\frac{MX}{XN}= \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{BE}{BF} \\quad\\text{(by (6) and (8)).}\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   In any triangle, the external symmedian issued from a vertex divides the opposite side (or its extension) externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides. Hence \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from vertex \\(B\\).\n\n8. **External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency Lemma (proved).**  \n\n   *Lemma.* Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). The external symmedian through vertex \\(X\\) meets \\((XYZ)\\) again at a point \\(T\\neq X\\). Then the line \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let the external symmedian through \\(X\\) intersect the line \\(YZ\\) (or its extension) at \\(S\\). By definition of external symmedian,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   Denote by \\(T\\) the second intersection of line \\(XS\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). By the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem applied to \\(X\\) we have\n   \\[\n   XT^{2}=XY\\cdot XZ. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   In \\(\\triangle YTZ\\) apply the law of sines:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXT}{\\sin\\angle ZXT}. \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   Using (10) and (11) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXT}{\\sin\\angle ZXT}\n   =\\frac{YT}{ZT}\n   =\\frac{YX}{ZX}\n   =\\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}.\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\angle YXT=\\angle YXZ\\). The angle between \\(XT\\) and the chord \\(YT\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the circumference; this is precisely the tangency condition. Therefore \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n   From step\u202f7 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from \\(B\\). By the Lemma, this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at its second intersection with the circle. Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was required. \u220e"
