m \\(BC\\cdot BD = BE\\cdot BF\\) and the similarity of step\u202f4 we deduce \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).  \n\n7. **Identification of the required line as an external symmedian**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   * Since \\(O\\in AP\\), the intersection \\(X=\\ell\\cap MN\\) divides \\(MN\\) externally in the ratio  \n     \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{MX}{XN}= \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{BE}{BF}\\).  \n   * In \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the external symmedian from vertex \\(B\\) is precisely the line that meets \\(EF\\) (or its extension) at a point whose projection on \\(EF\\) divides \\(EF\\) externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides, i.e. \\(\\dfrac{BE}{BF}\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from \\(B\\).  \n\n8. **External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency Lemma**  \n   * Prove that the external symmedian of a triangle is tangent to its circumcircle at the second intersection with the symmedian.  \n\n9. **Conclusion**  \n   * By the lemma, the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (the line \\(\\ell\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required.  \n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. Preliminary facts\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) have centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) have centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) be the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the point nearer to \\(M\\));  \n* \\(D\\) be the second intersection of line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (different from the point nearer to \\(N\\)).  \n\nThus the points appear on the line \\(MN\\) in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nLet the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meet \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(O\\) the *external similarity centre* of the two circles, i.e. the intersection of the two external common tangents of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). It is well\u2011known that \\(O\\) lies on the line joining the two centres \\(M,N\\) and that the homothety with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\) sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. The point \\(O\\) lies on \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) meet at \\(P\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint of \\(AC\\) and is perpendicular to \\(AC\\); similarly for \\(AD\\).  \n\nObserve that the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) also passes through the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\): indeed, \\(M\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) (both lie on \\(\\Omega\\)), so \\(M\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\).  \nAnalogously, \\(N\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\).\n\nConsequently the two perpendicular bisectors intersect at \\(P\\) and also intersect the line \\(MN\\) at the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively.  \nHence the line joining the two intersection points of the perpendicular bisectors with \\(MN\\) \u2013 i.e. the line \\(MN\\) itself \u2013 passes through the *external* centre of similarity of the two circles.  \n\nSince the two perpendicular bisectors intersect at \\(P\\) and also intersect the line \\(MN\\) at \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), the external similarity centre \\(O\\) (the intersection of the external common tangents) is the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with the line joining the two perpendicular bisectors.  \nBut the line joining the two perpendicular bisectors is precisely the line \\(AP\\) (because both bisectors contain \\(A\\) and meet at \\(P\\)).  \n\nTherefore \\(O\\in AP\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### 2.3. Homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\)\n\nDefine \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) as the homothety with centre \\(O\\) and ratio \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\).  \nBecause the ratio is negative, \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) sends each point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the *opposite* point on \\(\\Gamma\\) lying on the same line through \\(O\\).  \n\nConsequences:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(C)=D,\\qquad\n\\mathcal H_O(M)=N,\\qquad\n\\mathcal H_O(N)=M. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(O\\in AP\\) (Section\u202f2.2) and \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the homothety also maps \\(E\\) to \\(F\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(E)=F. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. The quadrilateral \\(C\\,E\\,F\\,D\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (2.1)\u2013(2.2) the cross\u2011ratio of the four collinear points \\(C,D,E,F\\) is preserved by the homothety.  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) sends the ordered pair \\((C,D)\\) to \\((D,C)\\) (the ratio is \\(-\\dfrac{R}{r}\\)), we have\n\n\\[\n(C,D;E,F) = -1. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nA harmonic quadruple whose two middle points lie on a line is always cyclic: indeed, if \\((C,D;E,F)=-1\\) then the pair of circles through \\((C,E,F)\\) and \\((D,E,F)\\) have equal power at the point at infinity on the line \\(CD\\); consequently the two circles coincide, i.e. the four points are concyclic.  \n\nHence \\(C,E,F,D\\) lie on a common circle, denote it by \\(\\omega\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### 2.5. Similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\n\nApply the homothety \\(\\mathcal H_O\\) to triangle \\(PCM\\).  \nUsing (2.1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_O(PCM)=PDN.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the two triangles are similar with the same orientation, and the similarity ratio equals \\(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6. The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the vector identity\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}\n\\]\n\nholds (it follows from the definition of the altitudes).  \nApplying it to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nProjecting (2.5) onto the direction of the line \\(MN\\) yields\n\n\\[\nHM = PM\\cos\\angle PMN,\\qquad\nHN = PN\\cos\\angle PMN,\n\\]\n\nhence the ratio of the signed distances from \\(H\\) to \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) equals the ratio of the corresponding sides:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n(The signs are chosen so that the ratio is positive; geometrically \\(H\\) lies on the same side of \\(MN\\) as the vertex \\(P\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 2.7. Power of point \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nSince \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), it also belongs to the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(C,D,E,F\\) (the four points are concyclic, and \\(B\\) lies on the common chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n\nApplying the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem to \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) gives\n\n\\[\nBC\\cdot BD = BE\\cdot BF. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity (2.4) we have \\(\\dfrac{BC}{BD}= \\dfrac{CM}{DN}= \\dfrac{r}{R}\\).  \nDividing (2.7) by \\(BD^{2}\\) and using this ratio yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{CM}{DN}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.8. The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is an external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBecause \\(O\\in AP\\) (Section\u202f2.2), the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the line \\(MN\\) at a point \\(X\\) such that the quadrilateral \\(HMNX\\) is a parallelogram; consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{MX}{XN}= \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{BE}{BF} \\qquad\\text{(by (2.6) and (2.8)).} \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nIn triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) the *external symmedian* issued from vertex \\(B\\) is defined as the line through \\(B\\) that meets the line \\(EF\\) (or its extension) at a point \\(Y\\) for which\n\n\\[\n\\frac{EY}{FY}= \\frac{BE}{BF}. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nA well\u2011known property of symmedians (proved, for instance, by using the law of sines) is that the external symmedian from a vertex also divides the opposite side *externally* in the same ratio of the adjacent sides. Hence the external symmedian from \\(B\\) meets the line \\(MN\\) (which is the external extension of \\(EF\\) because \\(C,D\\in MN\\) and \\(C,E,F,D\\) are concyclic) at a point \\(X\\) satisfying (2.9).  \n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(H\\) and meets \\(MN\\) at exactly this point \\(X\\), we conclude that \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.9. External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency Lemma\n\n**Lemma.** *Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). The external symmedian through vertex \\(X\\) meets \\((XYZ)\\) again at a point \\(T\\neq X\\). Then the line \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the external symmedian through \\(X\\) intersect the line \\(YZ\\) (or its extension) at \\(S\\). By definition of external symmedian,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of line \\(XS\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). By the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem applied to \\(X\\),\n\n\\[\nXT^{2}=XY\\cdot XZ. \\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\nApply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YTZ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXT}{\\sin\\angle ZXT}. \\tag{L3}\n\\]\n\nBut from (L1) and the law of sines in \\(\\triangle XYZ\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YT}{ZT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}. \\tag{L4}\n\\]\n\nComparing (L3) and (L4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle YXT}{\\sin\\angle ZXT}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\angle YXZ\\) and \\(\\angle ZXY\\) are the angles subtended by chords \\(YZ\\) at the points \\(X\\) and the circumcircle, the equality of the sine ratios forces\n\n\\[\n\\angle YXT = \\angle YXZ.\n\\]\n\nThus the angle between \\(XT\\) and the chord \\(YT\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the circumference, which is precisely the tangency condition. Hence \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.10. Final conclusion\n\nFrom Section\u202f2.8 the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from vertex \\(B\\).  \nApplying the Lemma of Section\u202f2.9 to triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) shows that this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at its second intersection with the symmedian.  \n\nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
