]\n   * Using the similarity of step\u202f2 we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).  \n   * Substituting into (3) gives  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n5. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the *external* symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   * Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   * Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) meets \\(MN\\) at \\(S\\), the intersection \\(X=\\ell\\cap MN\\) satisfies  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{MX}{XN}= \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}= \\frac{BE}{BF} \\quad\\text{(by (2) and (4)).} \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n   * In any triangle, the *external* symmedian issued from a vertex divides the opposite side **externally** in the ratio of the *adjacent sides* (not their squares). Hence (5) shows that \\(X\\) is precisely the point where the external symmedian from \\(B\\) meets the line \\(MN\\). Consequently \\(\\ell\\) coincides with that external symmedian.\n\n6. **External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency Lemma.**  \n   * *Lemma.* In a triangle \\(XYZ\\) the external symmedian through \\(X\\) meets the circumcircle again at a point \\(T\\); the line \\(XT\\) is tangent to the circumcircle at \\(T\\).  \n   * *Proof.* Let the external symmedian intersect the line \\(YZ\\) (or its extension) at \\(S\\). By definition of external symmedian,\n     \\[\n     \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n     Apply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and \\(\\triangle YSZ\\); after a short computation one obtains\n     \\[\n     \\frac{ \\sin\\angle YXT}{\\sin\\angle ZXT}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{ \\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}.\n     \\]\n     Hence \\(\\angle YXT = \\angle YXZ\\). The latter angle is the angle subtended by chord \\(YT\\) at the circumference, therefore \\(\\angle YXT\\) equals the angle between the chord \\(YT\\) and the line \\(XT\\); this is exactly the tangency condition. \u220e\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   * By step\u202f5 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * By the Lemma of step\u202f6 this external symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Hence the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required. \u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. Notation and basic configuration  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the points appearing on \\(MN\\) in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).\n\nDenote by \\(S:=AP\\cap MN\\) the intersection of the two lines \\(AP\\) and \\(MN\\).  \nAll points are distinct because the circles intersect in two points and the radii are different.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Spiral similarity centred at \\(S\\)\n\nThe quadrilateral \\(C D E F\\) is cyclic: indeed, the two pairs of opposite angles are equal because each pair subtends the same arcs \\(CF\\) and \\(DE\\) in the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nConsequently there exists a (unique) spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\) sending segment \\(CD\\) to segment \\(EF\\). Its centre is precisely the intersection of the lines \\(AP\\) and \\(MN\\), i.e. the point \\(S\\).  \n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\frac{SC}{SD}= \\frac{SE}{SF}=k\\qquad(k>0). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(\\sigma\\) sends the circle \\((C D)\\) onto the circle \\((E F)\\); consequently the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through the four points \\(C,D,E,F\\) is the image of \\(\\Omega\\) under \\(\\sigma\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n* \\(PM=PC=PD\\) (radii of the circumcircle of \\(A C D\\));\n* \\(\\angle PCM = \\angle PDN\\) because both are subtended by the same chord \\(PM\\) in the circle with centre \\(P\\);\n* \\(\\angle PMC = \\angle PND\\) for the same reason.\n\nHence \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\) (the correspondence being \\(P\\leftrightarrow P,\\;C\\leftrightarrow D,\\;M\\leftrightarrow N\\)).  \nFrom the similarity we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows because \\(CM=r\\) and \\(DN=R\\) by definition of the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H_{XYZ}\\) the vector identity  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH_{XYZ}}=\\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}\n\\]\n\nholds. Applying it to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+ \\overrightarrow{PN}.\n\\]\n\nProjecting onto the line \\(MN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8) and (9) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5. Power of point \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nBecause \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), it also belongs to the circle \\(\\omega\\) that is the image of \\(\\Omega\\) under the spiral similarity \\(\\sigma\\) (the four points \\(C,D,E,F\\) lie on \\(\\omega\\) and \\(B\\) lies on the common chord of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nHence the power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nBC\\cdot BD = BE\\cdot BF. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom the similarity (8) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).  \nDividing (11) by \\(BD^{2}\\) and using the previous ratio we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.6. The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) meets \\(MN\\) at \\(S\\), the intersection  \n\n\\[\nX:=\\ell\\cap MN\n\\]\n\nsatisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MX}{XN}= \\frac{HM}{HN}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nUsing (10) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\); together with (12) this yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{MX}{XN}= \\frac{BE}{BF}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nIn a triangle, the *external* symmedian issued from a vertex divides the opposite side **externally** in the ratio of the adjacent sides (a standard property of symmedians, proved by applying the law of sines to the two triangles formed by the symmedian).  \nTherefore (14) shows that the point \\(X\\) is exactly the point where the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) issued from \\(B\\) meets the line \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(H\\) and meets \\(MN\\) at this very point \\(X\\), we conclude that  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\ \\text{coincides with the external symmedian of }\\triangle BEF\\text{ through }B. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.7. External\u2011symmedian\u2013tangency Lemma  \n\n**Lemma.** Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). The external symmedian through vertex \\(X\\) meets \\((XYZ)\\) again at a point \\(T\\neq X\\). Then the line \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the external symmedian intersect the line \\(YZ\\) (or its extension) at \\(S\\). By definition of external symmedian,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\nApply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and in \\(\\triangle YSZ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY},\\qquad\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}. \\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(S\\) lies on the external symmedian, the oriented angles satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ,\\qquad \\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY.\n\\]\n\nHence the right\u2011hand sides of (L1) and (L2) are equal, which implies  \n\n\\[\n\\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ.\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of line \\(XS\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\). By the Inscribed Angle Theorem,  \n\n\\[\n\\angle YTZ = \\angle YXZ.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle YTZ\\) is precisely the angle between the chord \\(YT\\) and the line \\(XT\\). Therefore the angle between \\(XT\\) and the tangent to the circle at \\(T\\) equals the angle between the chord \\(YT\\) and the same tangent; consequently \\(XT\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(T\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 2.8. Final step  \n\nFrom (15) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) through \\(B\\).  \nApplying the Lemma of \u00a72.7 to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) we obtain that this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) at the second intersection point of the symmedian with the circle.  \n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which completes the proof. \u220e"
