es the opposite side externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides.\u201d\n  - **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The external symmedian divides the opposite side externally in the ratio of the **squares** of the adjacent sides, not the sides themselves.\n- **Location (Lemma):** \u201cLet\u202f\u0394XYZ\u202fhave circumcircle\u202f(XYZ). The external symmedian through vertex\u202fX\u202fmeets\u202f(XYZ)\u202fagain at a point\u202fT\u2260X. Then the line\u202fXT\u202fis tangent to\u202f(XYZ)\u202fat\u202fT.\u201d\n  - **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 This lemma is false; the external symmedian does not generally produce a tangent line, and the proof uses the incorrect side\u2011ratio property.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Verification Log**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. Notation and basic configuration  \n\n> \u201cLet\u202f\u03a9\u202fbe the circle with centre\u202fM\u202fand radius\u202fr; \u2026 Let\u202fS:=AP\u2229MN\u202f\u2026 All points are distinct because the circles intersect in two points and the radii are different.\u201d\n\n**Verification:** The definitions are clear and correct. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Spiral similarity centred at\u202fS  \n\n> \u201cThe quadrilateral\u202fC\u202fD\u202fE\u202fF\u202fis cyclic: indeed, the two pairs of opposite angles are equal because each pair subtends the same arcs\u202fCF\u202fand\u202fDE\u202fin the circles\u202f\u03a9\u202fand\u202f\u0393.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n- The statement that\u202fC\u202fD\u202fE\u202fF\u202fis cyclic is unsupported.  \n- Arcs\u202fCF\u202fand\u202fDE\u202fare not defined on a single circle (C and\u202fF belong to different circles, as do\u202fD and\u202fE).  \n- Hence the reasoning that opposite angles are equal is invalid, and the cyclicity claim is false.\n\n> \u201cConsequently there exists a (unique) spiral similarity\u202f\u03c3\u202fsending segment\u202fCD\u202fto segment\u202fEF. Its centre is precisely the intersection of the lines\u202fAP\u202fand\u202fMN, i.e. the point\u202fS.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n- The existence of a spiral similarity mapping\u202fCD\u202fto\u202fEF\u202frequires the ratio\u202fSC/SD\u202fto equal\u202fSE/SF\u202f(and the oriented angles to match).  \n- This ratio equality is not established; it was inferred from the (false) cyclicity.  \n- Therefore the claim of a well\u2011defined spiral similarity centred at\u202fS\u202fis unjustified.\n\n> \u201cThus \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{SC}{SD}= \\frac{SE}{SF}=k\\qquad(k>0).\\)\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error (consequence):**  \n- Equation (7) is a direct restatement of the unproved ratio equality.  \n- Since the underlying spiral similarity is not established, (7) lacks any justification.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Similarity of\u202f\u0394PCM\u202fand\u202f\u0394PDN  \n\n> \u201cSince\u202fP\u202fis the circumcenter of triangle\u202fA\u202fC\u202fD,\u202fPM=PC=PD\u202f(radii of the circumcircle of\u202fA\u202fC\u202fD).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n-\u202fP\u202fis the circumcenter of\u202fA\u202fC\u202fD, so\u202fPA=PC=PD.  \n-\u202fM\u202fis the centre of\u202f\u03a9, not a point on the circumcircle of\u202fA\u202fC\u202fD; consequently\u202fPM\u202fis **not** a radius of that circumcircle.  \n- The equality\u202fPM=PC=PD\u202fis false.\n\n> \u201c\u2220PCM = \u2220PDN because both are subtended by the same chord\u202fPM\u202fin the circle with centre\u202fP; \u2220PMC = \u2220PND for the same reason.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n- No circle with centre\u202fP\u202fcontains points\u202fM,\u202fC,\u202fD simultaneously; thus the claimed subtended\u2011by\u2011the\u2011same\u2011chord argument is invalid.  \n- The asserted angle equalities are therefore false.\n\n> \u201cHence\u202f\u0394PCM\u202f\u223c\u202f\u0394PDN\u202f(the correspondence being\u202fP\u2194P,\u202fC\u2194D,\u202fM\u2194N).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n- The similarity conclusion rests on the false angle equalities above; it