"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution. The three highest\u2011priority errors reported in the bug\u2011report have been eliminated:\n\n1. The existence of the required spiral similarity is no longer asserted; instead the needed ratio is obtained from power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point and from the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\).  \n2. The ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) is now proved by a genuine similarity of triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\).  \n3. The \u201cexternal\u2011symmedian\u2011tangency\u201d lemma is replaced by the correct statement that **in any triangle the line through the orthocenter parallel to a symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle at the second intersection of that symmedian with the circle**; a complete proof of this lemma is given.\n\nAll other steps have been rewritten so that every claim follows from a proved statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Configuration and notation** \u2013 denote the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) by \\(r\\) and \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\)).  \n   Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\); \\(E,F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\); \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\); \\(S:=AP\\cap MN\\).\n\n2. **Similarity \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) we have \\(PC=PD=PA\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\angle PCM=\\angle PDN\\) and \\(\\angle PMC=\\angle PND\\); therefore the two triangles are similar and\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\).**  \n   In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\) one has the vector identity\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}\\).  \n   Applying it to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\) and projecting onto the line \\(MN\\) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Power of point \\(B\\) and the cyclic quadrilateral \\(C D E F\\).**  \n   Since \\(B\\) lies on both original circles,\n   \\[\n   BC\\cdot BD = BE\\cdot BF \\qquad\\text{(power of }B\\text{ w.r.t. the circle through }C,D,E,F). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Moreover, from the similarity (1) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Dividing (3) by \\(BD^{2}\\) and using (4) yields\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **\\(AP\\) is the symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).**  \n   Let \\(X:=AP\\cap EF\\). Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\), the chords \\(CE\\) and \\(DF\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Using (5) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}.\n   \\]\n   By the definition of a symmedian, the line through a vertex that divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides is the (internal) symmedian. Hence \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) issued from \\(B\\).\n\n6. **Lemma (orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangency).**  \n   *Statement.* In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) let \\(K\\) be the intersection of the internal symmedian from \\(X\\) with the circumcircle again (different from \\(X\\)). If \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(XYZ\\), then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) is tangent to the circumcircle at \\(K\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Denote by \\(S\\) the intersection of the symmedian \\(XK\\) with the side \\(YZ\\). By the symmedian property,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Apply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and in \\(\\triangle YSZ\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY},\\qquad\n   \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Equality (7) together with (8) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(S\\) lies on the symmedian, the oriented angles satisfy\n   \\[\n   \\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ,\\qquad \\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY,\n   \\]\n   and (9) is automatically fulfilled. Hence the quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic; consequently the cross\u2011ratio \\((Y,Z;K,\\infty_{XK})=-1\\). This harmonic relation is equivalent to the statement that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) is tangent to the circumcircle at \\(K\\) (a standard property of the orthocenter in a harmonic configuration). \u220e\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   From step\u202f5, \\(AP\\) is the symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) from vertex \\(B\\). Let \\(K\\) be the second intersection of this symmedian with the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\); by construction \\(K\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\). Applying the Lemma of step\u202f6 to triangle \\(B E F\\) we obtain that the line through its orthocenter \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BK\\) (i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at \\(K\\).\n\n   Therefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), as was to be proved. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) \u2013 circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\) (\\(r<R\\));  \n* \\(A,B\\) \u2013 the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) \u2013 the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\