a\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the points appearing on \\(MN\\) in the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) \u2013 the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) \u2013 the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) \u2013 the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\);  \n* \\(S:=AP\\cap MN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct because the circles intersect in two points and the radii are different.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n\\[\nPC = PD = PA .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the chords \\(CM\\) and \\(DN\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); explicitly\n\n\\[\n\\angle PCM = \\angle PDN ,\\qquad\n\\angle PMC = \\angle PND .\n\\]\n\nThus the two triangles are similar with the correspondence\n\\(P\\leftrightarrow P,\\;C\\leftrightarrow D,\\;M\\leftrightarrow N\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows because \\(CM=r\\) and \\(DN=R\\) by definition of the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).)\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\)\n\nIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\) one has the vector identity  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n\\]\n\nApplying it to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+ \\overrightarrow{PN}.\n\\]\n\nProjecting this relation onto the line \\(MN\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Power of point \\(B\\) and the ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}\\)\n\nBecause \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles, its power with respect to the (unique) circle passing through the four points \\(C,D,E,F\\) is the same when computed with the pair \\((C,D)\\) or with the pair \\((E,F)\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nBC\\cdot BD = BE\\cdot BF. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom similarity (1) we also have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDividing (4) by \\(BD^{2}\\) and using (5) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  The line \\(AP\\) is the symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)\n\nLet \\(X:=AP\\cap EF\\).  Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\), the chords \\(CE\\) and \\(DF\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); consequently the triangles \\(\\triangle PC E\\) and \\(\\triangle PD F\\) are congruent, which yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nComparing (6) and (7) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}.\n\\]\n\nBy definition, the internal symmedian issued from a vertex of a triangle divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides. Hence the line \\(AP\\) (which passes through \\(B\\) and meets \\(EF\\) at \\(X\\)) is precisely the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) issued from \\(B\\).\n\nDenote by \\(K\\) the second intersection of this symmedian with the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\); by construction \\(K\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Lemma: orthocenter\u202f+\u202fsymmedian \u21d2 tangency  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle, \\(K\\neq X\\) the second intersection of the internal symmedian from \\(X\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\), and \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(XYZ\\). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(S:=XK\\cap YZ\\).  By the symmedian property  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\nApply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and \\(\\triangle YSZ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY},\\qquad\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}. \\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\nEquality (L1) together with (L2) gives 