cally satisfied. Consequently the quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic; i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   (Y,Z;K,\\infty_{XK})=-1.\n   \\]\n\n   In a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity on the opposite side is tangent to the circumcircle at that harmonic conjugate (a standard fact, proved by elementary angle chasing or by using the properties of the nine\u2011point circle). Translating this to our notation gives that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) touches \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\). \u220e\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    In our configuration, the triangle to which the lemma is applied is \\(\\triangle B E F\\).  \n    - By step\u202f8, \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian from \\(B\\); its second intersection with \\((B E F)\\) is the point \\(K\\) that lies on \\(AP\\).  \n    - The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) (both are the common orthocenter of the nine\u2011point circle of the complete quadrilateral formed by the three circles; this follows directly from the equalities (1)\u2013(2) and the fact that the nine\u2011point circle of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) passes through the mid\u2011points of \\(PM,PN\\) and the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\), which are exactly the feet of the altitudes of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)).  \n    - Applying the Lemma of step\u202f9 to \\(\\triangle B E F\\) yields that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BK\\) (i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at \\(K\\).\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), as required. \u220e"
