"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a *complete* and *rigorously justified* solution of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation and basic facts** \u2013 Introduce the two given circles \\(\\Omega(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma(N,R)\\) \\((r<R)\\) with intersection points \\(A,B\\).  The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (order \\(C-M-N-D\\)).  Let \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\); let \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  Denote by \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).  Finally put \\(X:=AP\\cap EF\\).\n\n2. **Similarity \\(\\triangle PCM\\sim\\triangle PDN\\)** \u2013 Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) we have \\(PC=PD=PA\\).  The equal chords \\(CM\\) and \\(DN\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\); consequently \\(\\angle CPM=\\angle DPN\\).  Also \\(\\angle PCM=\\angle PDN\\) (both subtend the equal chord \\(CD\\) in the circle \\((C D P)\\)).  Hence the two triangles are similar and\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Location of the orthocenter \\(H\\) on \\(MN\\)** \u2013 In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n   \\]\n\n   Applying this to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\) and projecting onto the line \\(MN\\) gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(H\\) divides \\(MN\\) in the same ratio as the radii.\n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(C,D,E,F\\)** \u2013 Since \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\), the arcs \\(CE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(DF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at \\(P\\) (both are intercepted by the same central angle \\(\\angle CPA=\\angle DPA\\)).  Hence \\(\\angle CPE=\\angle DPF\\).  Because \\(PC=PD\\), the chords