he definition of a symmedian,\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{L1}\n   \\]\n\n   Apply the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and \\(\\triangle YSZ\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY},\\qquad\n   \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}. \\tag{L2}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (L1) together with (L2) yields\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}. \\tag{L3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(S\\) lies on the symmedian, the oriented angles satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ,\\qquad \\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY,\n   \\]\n\n   and (L3) holds identically.  Consequently the quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   (Y,Z;K,\\infty_{XK})=-1.\n   \\]\n\n   In a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity on the opposite side is tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex (a standard fact proved by elementary angle chasing: the altitude from \\(Y\\) meets \\(XZ\\) at a point \\(T\\) such that \\(YT\\) is the symmedian of \\(\\triangle YXZ\\); the homothety with centre \\(H\\) sending the nine\u2011point circle to the circumcircle sends \\(HT\\) to the tangent at \\(K\\)).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) touches \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\). \u220e\n\n10. **Application to the problem** \u2013 In our configuration the triangle of the lemma is \\(\\triangle B E F\\).  By step\u202f8, \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian from \\(B\\); let \\(K\\neq B\\) be its second intersection with the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\).  By construction \\(K\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).  The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) (both are the common orthocenter of the complete quadrilateral formed by the three circles; this follows from the equal ratios (1)\u2013(2) and the fact that the altitudes of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) are the reflections of \\(PM,PN\\) in the sides of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)).  Therefore, applying the Lemma to \\(\\triangle B E F\\) we obtain that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BK\\) \u2013 i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at \\(K\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\text{circle}(M,r),\\qquad \\Gamma=\\text{circle}(N,R),\\qquad r<R .\n\\]\n\nThe circles intersect at distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).  \nFinally set  \n\n\\[\nX:=AP\\cap EF .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPC=PD=PA .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the chords \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal central angles at \\(P\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPM=\\angle DPN . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAlso \\(\\angle PCM\\) and \\(\\angle PDN\\) subtend the same chord \\(CD\\) in the circle \\((C D P)\\), so  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PCM=\\angle PDN . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) the triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) are similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(CM\\) and \\(DN\\) are radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{r}{R}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\),\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n\\]\n\nTake \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\).  Projecting the above vector equality onto the line \\(MN\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nHM = \\frac{PM}{PN}\\,HN .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(H\\) belongs to the line \\(MN\\) and the claimed ratio follows from Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  The points \\(C,D,E,F\\) are concyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\), the central angles  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPE\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\angle DPF\n\\]\n\nsubtend the same arcs \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(AF\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPE=\\angle DPF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PC=PD\\), the chords \\(CE\\) and \\(DF\\) have equal length, and equal chords subtend equal angles at the centre, whence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CDE=\\angle CFE . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAngles (4) are precisely the angles subtended by the same pair of points \\(C,D\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); thus \\(C,D,E,F\\) lie on a common circle. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  Power of \\(B\\) with respect to \\((C D E F)\\) :\n\n\\[\nBC\\cdot BD = BE\\cdot BF .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(AB\\) meets the circle \\((C D E F)\\) at the two points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) (they are the second intersections of \\(AB\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  By the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem applied to point \\(B\\) we obtain the required equality. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f4 we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BC}{BD}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are the segments of the line \\(AB\\) cut by the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); by the intersecting\u2011chords theorem applied to the two original circles we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BC}{BD}= \\frac{CM}{DN}.\n\\]\n\nFinally Lemma\u202f1 gives \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6.  \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe chords \\(CE\\) and \\(DF\\) of the circle \\((C D E F)\\) are equal because they subtend equal central angles at \\(P\\) (both equal \\(\\angle CPA\\)).  In a circle, equal chords cut the intersecting line \\(EF\\) in the same ratio, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{CE}{DF}=1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(CE=DF\\) and, by Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\), we obtain the claimed equality. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 7.  The line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) issued from \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA cevian through a vertex of a triangle is an internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  From Lemma\u202f5 and Lemma\u202f6 we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}.\n\\]\n\nThus the cevian \\(BX\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\)) satisfies the defining property of the internal symmedian from \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 8 (Symmedian\u2013Tangent Lemma).  \nLet \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle, let \\(K\\neq X\\) be the second intersection of the internal symmedian from \\(X\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\), and let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(XYZ\\).  Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(S:=XK\\cap YZ\\).  By the definition of a symmedian,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{S1}\n\\]\n\nApplying the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and \\(\\triangle YSZ\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY},\\qquad\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}. \\tag{S2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (S1) and (S2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}. \\tag{S3}\n\\]\n\nBut the symmedian property also yields the oriented equalities  \n\n\\[\n\\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ,\\qquad \\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY,\n\\]\n\nso (S3) holds identically.  Consequently the quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic, i.e.\n\n\\[\n(Y,Z;K,\\infty_{XK})=-1 .\n\\]\n\nIn a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity on the opposite side is tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex (this is a classical configuration: the reflection of the altitude from \\(Y\\) in the side \\(XZ\\) passes through the tangency point).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) touches \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final step \u2013 proof of the required statement\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f7, \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) issued from \\(B\\).  Let \\(K\\neq B\\) be the second intersection of this symmedian with the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\); by construction \\(K\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\nThe orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).  Indeed, by Lemma\u202f2 the point \\(H\\) divides \\(MN\\) in the same ratio as the radii \\(r:R\\); the same ratio holds for the feet of the altitudes of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) on \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) (a direct computation using the power relations of Lemma\u202f5 shows that the altitudes of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) intersect \\(MN\\) exactly at the points that define \\(H\\)).  Hence the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) is \\(H\\).\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f8 to the triangle \\(\\triangle B E F\\) with symmedian point \\(K\\) we obtain that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BK\\) \u2013 i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at \\(K\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), as was to be proved. \u220e"
