"**Final Verdict:** The solution contains multiple **Critical Errors** and is therefore **invalid**.\n\n---\n\n### List of Findings\n* **Location:** \u201cIn the circle \\((P C D)\\) the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\); hence \\(\\angle CPM=\\angle DPN\\).\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 \\(CM\\) and \\(DN\\) are not chords of the circle \\((PCD)\\); the statement is false.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cBoth triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) also subtend the same chord \\(CD\\), therefore \\(\\angle PCM=\\angle PDN\\).\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 \\(\\angle PCM\\) and \\(\\angle PDN\\) are not angles subtended by the same chord in a common circle; the claim is false.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cFrom (1.2) and (1.3) the triangles are similar, giving \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 The similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) has not been established; the resulting ratio is unsupported.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}\\).\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 This vector identity is false in general (it holds only when the origin is the circumcenter). Consequently the subsequent ratio \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}\\) is unjustified.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cBecause of (1.1) the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) pass through the centre of inversion; consequently each of them is transformed into a line.\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) has centre \\(P\\); it does **not** pass through \\(P\\). Moreover \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) do not pass through \\(P\\) in general. Hence the inversion description is incorrect.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cApplying this to the point \\(B\\) we have \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot BE}{BP\\cdot BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BE)}{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BF)} = \\frac{BC}{BD}\\).\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 The manipulation of distances under inversion is invalid; inversion does not preserve ratios of the form \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}\\) in the way claimed.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cThus the cevian \\(BX\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\)) satisfies the defining property of the internal symmedian issued from \\(B\\). Consequently \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) from \\(B\\).\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Critical Error \u2013 The equality \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}\\) used to deduce the symmedian property rests on the flawed ratios from Steps\u202f4 and\u202f5.\n\n* **Location:** Lemma (symmedian\u2013tangent) and its proof.  \n  **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 The lemma is stated without a rigorous proof; the argument invokes \u201coriented equalities\u201d and a harmonic\u2011bundle property without justification. The conclusion is therefore not established.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cThe orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\). Indeed, \u2026\u201d  \n  **Issue:** Justification Gap \u2013 No rigorous argument is given that the two orthocentres are identical; the statement is asserted without proof.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow each quoted passage from the solution is followed by a rigorous analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),  \n> \\[\n> PC=PD=PA. \\tag{1.1}\n> \\]  \n> In the circle \\((P C D)\\) the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\); hence  \n> \\[\n> \\angle CPM=\\angle DPN. \\tag{1.2}\n> \\]  \n> Both triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) also subtend the same chord \\(CD\\), therefore  \n> \\[\n> \\angle PCM=\\angle PDN. \\tag{1.3}\n> \\]  \n> From (1.2) and (1.3) the triangles are similar, giving  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1.4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The equality \\(PC=PD=PA\\) follows directly from the definition of a circumcenter; this part is correct.\n\n* The claim \u201cthe chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\((PCD)\\)\u201d is **false**.  \n  - The circle \\((PCD)\\) is the circumcircle of triangle \\(ACD\\). Its points are \\(P, C, D\\) (and \\(A\\)). The segment \\(CM\\) is **not** a chord of this circle because the point \\(M\\) does **not** lie on \\((PCD)\\) in general. Similarly, \\(DN\\) is not a chord of \\((PCD)\\). Consequently the statement that \\(\\angle CPM = \\angle DPN\\) has no justification.\n\n* The next claim \u201cBoth triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) also subtend the same chord \\(CD\\), therefore \\(\\angle PCM=\\angle PDN\\)\u201d is also **incorrect**.  \n  - \\(\\angle PCM\\) is the angle at \\(C\\) formed by lines \\(PC\\) and \\(CM\\). \\(\\angle PDN\\) is the angle at \\(D\\) formed by lines \\(PD\\) and \\(DN\\). Neither of these angles is an angle subtended by chord \\(CD\\) in a common circle. The chord\u2011subtended\u2011angle theorem applies only to angles whose vertices lie on the same circle; here the vertices \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), not on a circle containing \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) together with \\(M\\) or \\(N\\). Hence the equality is unfounded.\n\n* Because the two preceding angle equalities are invalid, the conclusion that the two triangles are similar is **unsupported**. The derived ratio \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) therefore lacks any rigorous basis.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the entire similarity argument collapses, invalidating all subsequent results that depend on (1.4).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\),  \n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n> \\]  \n> Apply this to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\).  Projecting onto the line \\(MN\\) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}. \\tag{2.1}\n> \\]  \n> Using (1.4),  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2.2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The vector identity \\(\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}\\) is **not true** for an arbitrary placement of the origin. The correct relation is \\(\\overrightarrow{OH}= \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{OB}+\\overrightarrow{OC}\\) when the origin \\(O\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\). Without specifying the origin, the claimed identity is false. A simple counter\u2011example is the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\\); its orthocenter coincides with the right\u2011angle vertex, yet \\(\\overrightarrow{XH}\\neq \\overrightarrow{XY}+\\overrightarrow{XZ}\\).\n\n* Because the vector relation is false, the derived proportionality \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}\\) has **no justification**. Even if one attempted to project the (incorrect) vector equality onto line \\(MN\\), the result would be baseless.\n\n* The subsequent use of (1.4) to replace \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}\\) with \\(\\frac{r}{R}\\) compounds the error: (1.4) itself is unproved (see Step\u202f1). Hence the conclusion that \\(H\\) divides \\(MN\\) in the ratio \\(r:R\\) is **unsubstantiated**.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the orthocenter location argument is built on a false vector identity and on the previously invalid similarity result.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  An inversion centred at \\(P\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and arbitrary radius \\(\\rho\\).  \n> Because of (1.1) the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) pass through the centre of inversion; consequently each of them is transformed into a line.  \n> - \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the line \\(C'P\\) where \\(C'=\\mathcal I(C)\\).  \n> - \\(\\Gamma\\) is sent to the line \\(D'P\\) where \\(D'=\\mathcal I(D)\\).  \n> - The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) is sent to the line \\(A'P\\) (with \\(A'=\\mathcal I(A)\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* An inversion with centre \\(P\\) maps a circle **through** \\(P\\) to a line, and a line **through** \\(P\\) to a circle through \\(P\\). It maps a circle **not passing** through \\(P\\) to another circle not passing through \\(P\\).  \n\n* The circles \\(\\Omega\\) (center \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (center \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)) **do not** pass through \\(P\\) in general. The only point guaranteed to lie on both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); there is no reason for \\(P\\) (the circumcenter of \\(ACD\\)) to belong to either \\(\\Omega\\) or \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence the claim that \u201cthe three circles \u2026 pass through the centre of inversion\u201d is false.\n\n* The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) has centre \\(P\\) (by definition of \\(P\\) as the circumcenter). Therefore this circle **does not** pass through \\(P\\); it is centred at \\(P\\). Under inversion, a circle centred at the inversion centre maps to itself (or to a line at infinity), not to a line as claimed.\n\n* Consequently the description of the images of \\(\\Omega\\), \\(\\Gamma\\), and \\((ACD)\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is **incorrect**. The subsequent statements that rely on these images (e.g., \\(\\mathcal I(E)=C\\), \\(\\mathcal I(F)=D\\)) are not justified.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the inversion step is based on a false premise about which objects pass through the inversion centre.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cThe power of a point with respect to an inversion is preserved up to the factor \\(\\rho^{2}\\).  Applying this to the point \\(B\\) we have  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot BE}{BP\\cdot BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BE)}{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BF)}\n>                = \\frac{BC}{BD}. \\tag{4.1}\n> \\]  \n> But \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are the distances from \\(B\\) to the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the radii through \\(B\\); therefore  \n> \\[\n> BC=BM=r,\\qquad BD=BN=R. \\tag{4.2}\n> \\]  \n> Combining (4.1) and (4.2) yields  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}}. \\tag{4.3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The statement \u201cThe power of a point with respect to an inversion is preserved up to the factor \\(\\rho^{2}\\)\u201d is a misinterpretation. Under inversion, a point \\(X\\) maps to \\(X'\\) such that \\(PX\\cdot PX' = \\rho^{2}\\). This does **not** imply that the ratio of two segment lengths from a third point \\(B\\) is preserved.\n\n* The derivation \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot BE}{BP\\cdot BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BE)}{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BF)}\\) is nonsensical: \\(\\mathcal I(BE)\\) denotes the image of the **segment** \\(BE\\) under inversion, which is not a scalar length but a curve (a line or circle). The notation \u201c\\(BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BE)\\)\u201d has no meaning in Euclidean geometry.\n\n* Even if one attempted to use the relation \\(BP\\cdot BP' = \\rho^{2}\\) for the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), the correct relation would be \\(PE\\cdot PC = \\rho^{2}\\) (since \\(\\mathcal I(E)=C\\) according to the (incorrect) earlier claim). There is no direct link to the distances \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).\n\n* The claim that \\(BC = BM = r\\) and \\(BD = BN = R\\) is also **incorrect**. Point \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) at the intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); thus \\(MC = r\\) (radius of \\(\\Omega\\)), but \\(BC\\) is not equal to \\(MC\\) unless \\(B\\) coincides with \\(M\\), which is false. Similarly, \\(BD\\) is not equal to \\(BN\\). The distances \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are not radii of the circles; they are chords from the intersection point \\(B\\) to the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the circles.\n\n* Therefore the entire derivation of \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) is **invalid**.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the ratio is derived from a misuse of inversion and a false geometric claim about distances.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cThe point \\(X\\) is the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(EF\\).  Under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(EF\\) is transformed into the circle through the four points \\(C,D,P\\) (the image of the line \\(AP\\) is the circle \\((C D P)\\)).  Consequently \\(X\\) is mapped to the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with that circle, i.e. to the point \\(P\\) itself.  Hence  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal I(X)=P. \\tag{5.1}\n> \\]  \n> Using the distance\u2011preserving property of inversion as in (4.1),  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{C\\!P}{P\\!F}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{5.2}\n> \\]  \n> From (1.4) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{PC}{PD}= \\frac{r}{R}\\); therefore  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\displaystyle\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{r}{R}}. \\tag{5.3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The description of the image of line \\(EF\\) under inversion is **incorrect** because the inversion itself was mis\u2011described in Step\u202f3. Even assuming the inversion is correctly defined, the image of a line not passing through the centre \\(P\\) is indeed a circle passing through \\(P\\). However, the claim that this circle is precisely the circumcircle of \\(C, D, P\\) is **unjustified**; one would need to verify that the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (which were claimed to be \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) in Step\u202f3) indeed lie on that circle, but Step\u202f3\u2019s claim is already false.\n\n* The statement \u201c\\(\\mathcal I(X)=P\\)\u201d follows from the (flawed) premise that \\(X\\) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with the image circle. Since the image of \\(AP\\) under inversion is a line (because \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\)), the image of \\(AP\\) is **itself** (the line \\(AP\\) is invariant). Therefore the image of \\(X\\) under inversion is not automatically \\(P\\); the reasoning is invalid.\n\n* The subsequent use of a \u201cdistance\u2011preserving property of inversion\u201d to claim \\(\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{CP}{PF}\\) is a **misapplication**. Inversion does not preserve ratios of the form \\(\\frac{CX}{XF}\\) in the way claimed. The correct relation for a point \\(X\\) and its image \\(X'\\) is \\(PX\\cdot PX' = \\rho^{2}\\); there is no simple relation between \\(CX\\) and \\(XF\\) and the distances from \\(P\\) to \\(C\\) and \\(F\\).\n\n* The final equality \\(\\frac{PC}{PD}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) relies on (1.4), which, as shown in Step\u202f1, is unproved. Hence the conclusion \\(\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) is **unsupported**.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the ratio is derived from multiple earlier false statements and a misuse of inversion.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cIn a triangle a cevian through a vertex is an internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  \n> From (4.3) and (5.3),  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}.\n> \\]  \n> Thus the cevian \\(BX\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\)) satisfies the defining property of the internal symmedian issued from \\(B\\). Consequently  \n> \\[\n> AP\\ \\text{is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle B E F\\text{ from }B. \\tag{6.1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The characterization of an internal symmedian is correct: a cevian from a vertex divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides. However, the **application** of this criterion requires that the point \\(X\\) be the intersection of the cevian with the opposite side \\(EF\\). In the problem, \\(X\\) is defined as \\(AP\\cap EF\\); indeed \\(X\\) lies on \\(EF\\). So the geometric setup is appropriate.\n\n* Nevertheless, the equality \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}\\) used to claim that \\(BX\\) is a symmedian is **based on the flawed ratios** derived in Steps\u202f4 and\u202f5. Since those ratios are invalid, the conclusion that \\(AP\\) is a symmedian is **unsubstantiated**.\n\n* Even if the ratio equality held, one would still need to verify that the point \\(X\\) is indeed the intersection of the line \\(BX\\) (i.e., the line through \\(B\\) and \\(X\\)) with side \\(EF\\). The solution identifies \\(BX\\) with \\(AP\\) (since \\(X\\) lies on both), but does not explicitly argue that \\(B, X, A, P\\) are collinear. The statement \u201cthe cevian \\(BX\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\))\u201d presumes that \\(B, A, P\\) are collinear, which is **false**: \\(A, P\\) are collinear with \\(E\\) and \\(F\\), but there is no reason for \\(B\\) to lie on that line. In fact, \\(B\\) is the second intersection of the two circles and generally does **not** lie on line \\(AP\\). Hence the identification of \\(BX\\) with \\(AP\\) is **incorrect**.\n\n* Consequently, the claim that \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is **invalid**.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the symmedian claim rests on false ratios and a mistaken collinearity assumption.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Lemma (symmedian\u2013tangent)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201c*Lemma (symmedian\u2013tangent)*  \n> Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle, let \\(K\\neq X\\) be the second intersection of the internal symmedian from \\(X\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\), and let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(XYZ\\). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\).  \n> *Proof.* \u2026 (the proof invokes oriented equalities, harmonic bundles, and a \u201cclassical result\u201d).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The lemma is a known geometric fact: the line through the orthocenter parallel to a symmedian is indeed tangent to the circumcircle at the symmedian\u2019s second intersection with the circle. However, the **proof supplied** is **incomplete** and contains several gaps:\n\n  - The step \u201c\\(\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}\\)\u201d follows from the definition of a symmedian, but the proof does not justify why the point \\(S = XK\\cap YZ\\) satisfies this ratio; it merely states it.\n\n  - The transition from the ratio of sines (L2) to the \u201coriented equalities\u201d \\(\\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ\\) and \\(\\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY\\) is **unexplained**. These equalities are not generally true; they would hold only if the quadrilateral were harmonic, which itself needs proof.\n\n  - The claim that \u201cthe quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic\u201d is asserted without demonstration. The harmonic property is central to the argument, yet no proof is provided.\n\n  - The final step \u201cIn a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate \u2026 is tangent \u2026 (a classical result)\u201d is cited without reference or proof. This leaves a substantial logical gap.\n\n* Because the lemma\u2019s proof is not rigorous, the lemma cannot be taken as established within the context of this solution. The lemma is used as a **key step** in the final argument, so the lack of a solid proof constitutes a **Justification Gap**.\n\n**Classification:** Justification Gap \u2013 the lemma is stated without a complete, rigorous proof.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Completion of the problem\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cApply the lemma to the triangle \\(\\triangle B E F\\).  \n> - By (6.1) the line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian from \\(B\\); let \\(K\\neq B\\) be its second intersection with the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\). \u2026  \n> - The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\). Indeed, from (2.2) the point \\(H\\) divides \\(MN\\) in the ratio \\(r\\!:\\!R\\); the feet of the altitudes from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) onto \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) are exactly the points where the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) meet \\(MN\\). Those feet determine the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), so the two orthocentres are the same point.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The first bullet assumes that the symmedian property proved in Step\u202f6 holds. Since Step\u202f6 is invalid, this application of the lemma is **unfounded**.\n\n* The second bullet claims that the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The argument is vague:\n\n  - It references (2.2), which itself is based on the false vector relation from Step\u202f2. Hence the ratio \\(HM:HN = r:R\\) is not established.\n\n  - The statement \u201cthe feet of the altitudes from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) onto \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) are exactly the points where the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) meet \\(MN\\)\u201d is **unproved**. There is no demonstration that the perpendiculars from \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) to the sides of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) intersect at the same point as the altitudes from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\). This is a non\u2011trivial claim requiring a detailed geometric argument, which is absent.\n\n* Consequently, the identification of the two orthocentres is **unsubstantiated**; this is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n* Even if the orthocentres coincided, the final conclusion that \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)\u201d depends on the lemma (which itself has a gap) and on the symmedian property (which is false). Hence the overall proof does not establish the required result.\n\n**Classification:** Justification Gap \u2013 the orthocentre coincidence is asserted without proof, and the final deduction rests on earlier invalid steps.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution contains **multiple critical errors** (incorrect similarity claim, false vector identity for the orthocenter, misuse of inversion, erroneous distance ratios, mistaken collinearity, and reliance on an unproved similarity ratio) that break the logical chain of the argument. Additionally, there are **justification gaps** in the lemma about symmedian\u2013tangent and in the identification of the orthocentres. Because the core deductions are invalid, the solution does **not** constitute a correct proof of the problem statement."
