ncorrect inversion behavior and an unjustified ratio transformation.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Claim that \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cIn a triangle a cevian through a vertex is an internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  \n> From (4.3) and (5.3),  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}.\n> \\]  \n> Thus the cevian \\(BX\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\)) satisfies the defining property of the internal symmedian issued from \\(B\\). Consequently  \n> \\[\n> AP\\ \\text{is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle B E F\\text{ from }B. \\tag{6.1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n- The definition of an internal symmedian is correctly recalled: a cevian from a vertex that divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides is a symmedian.\n\n- However, the identification \u201cthe cevian \\(BX\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\))\u201d is not justified. While \\(X\\) was defined as the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(EF\\), there is no proof that the line \\(BX\\) is the same as \\(AP\\); indeed, \\(BX\\) passes through \\(B\\) and \\(X\\), whereas \\(AP\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\). The claim that these two lines are identical would require showing that points \\(A, B, P, X\\) are collinear, which is not established.\n\n- Moreover, the ratios \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}\\) and \\(\\frac{CX}{XF}\\) were derived in Steps\u202f4 and\u202f5, both of which contain critical errors. Even assuming those ratios were correct, the step that equates the ratio \\(\\frac{CX}{XF}\\) with the ratio of sides adjacent to vertex \\(B\\) is not a standard symmedian condition; the symmedian condition involves the division of side \\(EF\\) by the cevian from \\(B\\), i.e., \\(\\frac{EX}{XF}\\), not \\(\\frac{CX}{XF}\\). The appearance of point \\(C\\) (which is not on side \\(EF\\)) makes the argument invalid.\n\n- Consequently, the conclusion that \\(AP\\) is an internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is **unsubstantiated**. This constitutes a **Justification Gap** (the step\u2019s conclusion may be true in some configurations, but the proof is missing).\n\n**Conclusion:** Step\u202f6 lacks a rigorous justification; the claim is not proven.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Lemma (symmedian\u2013tangent) and its proof\n\n**Quoted text (Lemma):**  \n> \u201c*Statement.*  \n> Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle, let \\(K\\neq X\\) be the second intersection of the internal symmedian from \\(X\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\), and let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(XYZ\\). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\).  \n> *Proof.*  \n> Denote \\(S:=XK\\cap YZ\\).  By definition of a symmedian,  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{L1}\n> \\]  \n> Applying the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and \\(\\triangle YSZ\\),  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY},\\qquad\n> \\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}. \\tag{L2}\n> \\]  \n> From (L1) and (L2) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}. \\tag{L3}\n> \\]  \n> Because \\(S\\) lies on the symmedian, the oriented equalities  \n> \\[\n> \\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ,\\qquad \\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY\n> \\]  \n> hold, so (L3) is an identity.  Hence the quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic, i.e.  \n> \\[\n> (Y,Z;K,\\infty_{XK})=-1. \\tag{L4}\n> \\]  \n> In a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity on the opposite side is tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex (a classical result; a short proof follows from reflecting the altitude from \\(Y\\) in the side \\(XZ\\) and using the harmonic relation).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) touches \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\). \u220e\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n- The statement of the lemma is plausible (it is a known property that the line through the orthocenter parallel to a symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle at the symmedian\u2019s intersection point with the circumcircle). However, the **proof** is incomplete and contains gaps:\n\n  1. The transition from the symmedian ratio (L1) to the claim that \u201coriented equalities \\(\\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ\\) and \\(\\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY\\)\u201d hold is **unjustified**. The symmedian condition does not directly imply equality of those angles; rather, it implies a proportionality of sines, but the step that the angles themselves are equal is a **leap**.\n\n  2. The conclusion that \u201cthe quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic\u201d is asserted without a rigorous derivation. While a harmonic division can be derived from the symmedian property, the proof requires a more detailed argument (e.g., using cross\u2011ratios or the definition of harmonic conjugates). This is omitted.\n\n  3. The final claim that \u201cin a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity on the opposite side is tangent to the circumcircle\u201d is cited as a \u201cclassical result\u201d without proof. For a self\u2011contained solution, this result must be either proved or properly referenced. Its use here without justification constitutes a **Justification Gap**.\n\n- Overall, the lemma\u2019s proof is **not rigorous**; it relies on unproven statements and a \u201cclassical result\u201d that is not established within the solution.\n\n**Conclusion:** The lemma contains a justification gap; the proof is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Completion of the problem \u2013 identification of orthocenters\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cApply the lemma to the triangle \\(\\triangle B E F\\).  \n> - By (6.1) the line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian from \\(B\\); let \\(K\\neq B\\) be its second intersection with the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\).  By construction \\(K\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).  \n> - The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).  Indeed, from (2.2) the point \\(H\\) divides \\(MN\\) in the ratio \\(r\\!:\\!R\\); the feet of the altitudes from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) onto \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) are exactly the points where the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) meet \\(MN\\).  Those feet determine the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), so the two orthocentres are the same point.  \n> Consequently, by the Lemma, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BK\\) \u2013 i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at \\(K\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n- The first bullet point assumes the result of Step\u202f6 (that \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian from \\(B\\) in \\(\\triangle BEF\\)). Since Step\u202f6 is not justified, this bullet inherits that gap.\n\n- The second bullet point claims that the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). This is a **non\u2011trivial geometric claim** that requires proof. The argument given is vague:\n\n  * It references (2.2), which itself is based on the false vector relation from Step\u202f2, so the ratio \\(HM:HN = r:R\\) is not established.\n  \n  * It then states that \u201cthe feet of the altitudes from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) onto \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) are exactly the points where the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) meet \\(MN\\).\u201d This assertion is **unproved**; it presumes a special configuration linking the altitudes of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) on line \\(MN\\). No argument is provided to show that the perpendicular from \\(M\\) to \\(BF\\) indeed meets \\(BF\\) at the foot of the altitude from \\(E\\), etc.\n\n- Therefore the identification of the two orthocenters is **unsubstantiated**, representing a **Justification Gap**.\n\n- Even if the orthocenter identification were correct, the final application of the lemma would be valid only if the lemma itself were proven (which it is not, as noted in Step\u202f7).\n\n**Conclusion:** Step\u202f8 contains a justification gap concerning the equality of orthocenters and relies on earlier unproven statements.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution fails at several foundational stages:\n\n1. **Critical Errors** in establishing the similarity of triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) (Step\u202f1) and in the vector relation for the orthocenter (Step\u202f2).  \n2. **Critical Errors** in the treatment of inversion centered at \\(P\\) (Step\u202f3) and the subsequent misuse of inversion to derive ratios (Steps\u202f4 and\u202f5).  \n3. **Justification Gaps** in the claim that \\(AP\\) is a symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (Step\u202f6), in the proof of the symmedian\u2013tangent lemma (Step\u202f7), and in the identification of orthocenters (Step\u202f8).\n\nBecause the early critical errors invalidate the key ratio \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) and the subsequent geometric constructions, the entire argument collapses. Consequently, the solution does **not** constitute a correct proof of the problem statement."
