om \\(B\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nAP\\ \\text{is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle B E F\\text{ from }B. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n* The characterization of a symmedian via the side\u2011ratio \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}\\) is correct **provided** the points are correctly identified: the cevian from \\(B\\) to the point \\(X\\) on \\(EF\\) must satisfy that ratio. However, the ratios \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) (4.3) and \\(\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) (5.3) have **not been established**; both rely on the critical errors identified in Steps\u202f4 and\u202f5.\n\n* Consequently the statement that \u201c\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\u201d is **unsupported**. The reasoning contains a **Justification Gap**: the necessary ratio equality is taken as given without a valid proof.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The conclusion is not justified because the prerequisite ratio equalities are unproved.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f7 \u2013 Lemma (symmedian\u2013tangent) and its proof\n\n**Quote (selected parts):**  \n\u201c*Lemma (symmedian\u2013tangent)*  \nLet \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle, let \\(K\\neq X\\) be the second intersection of the internal symmedian from \\(X\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\), and let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(XYZ\\). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote \\(S:=XK\\cap YZ\\).  By definition of a symmedian,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\nApplying the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and \\(\\triangle YSZ\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY},\\qquad\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}. \\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (L1) and (L2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}. \\tag{L3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(S\\) lies on the symmedian, the oriented equalities  \n\n\\[\n\\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ,\\qquad \\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY\n\\]\n\nhold, so (L3) is an identity.  Hence the quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n(Y,Z;K,\\infty_{XK})=-1. \\tag{L4}\n\\]\n\nIn a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity on the opposite side is tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex (a classical result; a short proof follows from reflecting the altitude from \\(Y\\) in the side \\(XZ\\) and using the harmonic relation).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) touches \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\). \u220e\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n* The lemma itself is a known geometric fact, but the **proof** presented contains several gaps:\n\n  1. The step \u201cBecause \\(S\\) lies on the symmedian, the oriented equalities \\(\\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ\\) and \\(\\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY\\) hold\u201d is **incorrect**. The definition of a symmedian does **not** imply these angle equalities; a symmedian is isogonal to the median, not necessarily preserving those specific angles.\n\n  2. The transition from (L3) to the claim that \u201cthe quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic\u201d is not justified. Harmonic division requires a cross\u2011ratio condition, which has not been derived.\n\n  3. The final statement \u201cIn a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity on the opposite side is tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex\u201d is quoted as a \u201cclassical result\u201d without any proof or reference. For a rigorous solution, this statement must be proved or properly cited.\n\n* Hence the lemma\u2019s proof contains **Justification Gaps**; the argument is incomplete and relies on unproved properties.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The lemma is not proved rigorously.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f8 \u2013 Completion of the problem\n\n**Quote (selected parts):**  \n\u201cApply the lemma to the triangle \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n- By (6.1) the line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian from \\(B\\); let \\(K\\neq B\\) be its second intersection with the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\).  By construction \\(K\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n- The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).  Indeed, from (2.2) the point \\(H\\) divides \\(MN\\) in the ratio \\(r\\!:\\!R\\); the feet of the altitudes from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) onto \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) are exactly the points where the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) meet \\(MN\\).  Those feet determine the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), so the two orthocentres are the same point.\n\nConsequently, by the Lemma, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BK\\) \u2013 i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at \\(K\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n* The claim that the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) is **unsubstantiated**. No rigorous argument is provided that the altitudes of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) intersect at the same point as the altitudes of \\(\\triangle P M N\\). The description \u201cthe feet of the altitudes from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) onto \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) are exactly the points where the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) meet \\(MN\\)\u201d is a **hand\u2011wavy assertion** without proof. In general, there is no reason for these two orthocentres to coincide.\n\n* Since the identification of orthocentres is not proved, the application of the lemma (which requires the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)) is **invalid**.\n\n* Moreover, the lemma itself (Step\u202f7) was not proved rigorously, compounding the issue.\n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The orthocentre coincidence is asserted without justification; the final conclusion rests on this unproved identification.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution contains several **Critical Errors** that break the logical chain at early stages (incorrect similarity, false vector relation, misuse of inversion). Subsequent steps depend on these false statements, leading to further unjustified conclusions (ratios of lengths, symmedian identification, orthocenter coincidence). The lemma invoked is not proved rigorously, adding additional gaps.\n\nBecause the foundational arguments are incorrect, the final statement that \u201cthe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)\u201d is **not established**. The solution is therefore **invalid**."
