ircumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),  \n> \\[\n> PC=PD=PA. \\tag{1.1}\n> \\]  \n> In the circle \\((P C D)\\) the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\); hence  \n> \\[\n> \\angle CPM=\\angle DPN. \\tag{1.2}\n> \\]  \n> Both triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) also subtend the same chord \\(CD\\), therefore  \n> \\[\n> \\angle PCM=\\angle PDN. \\tag{1.3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\n* The equality \\(PC=PD=PA\\) follows directly from the definition of a circumcenter and is correct.  \n\n* The claim \u201c\\(CM\\) and \\(DN\\) are chords of the circle \\((PCD)\\)\u201d is false. A chord of a circle must have both endpoints on that circle. Points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are **centres** of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); they do **not** lie on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) (unless in a degenerate configuration). Consequently \\(\\angle CPM\\) and \\(\\angle DPN\\) are not angles subtended by chords of \\((PCD)\\), and the asserted equality \\(\\angle CPM=\\angle DPN\\) has no justification.  \n\n* Similarly, \\(\\angle PCM\\) is the angle between \\(PC\\) and \\(CM\\) at vertex \\(C\\); \\(\\angle PDN\\) is the angle between \\(PD\\) and \\(DN\\) at vertex \\(D\\). Neither of these angles is subtended by the chord \\(CD\\) of the circle \\((PCD)\\). Hence (1.3) is also unfounded.  \n\nBecause both (1.2) and (1.3) are baseless, the conclusion that the two triangles are similar is **invalid**. This is a **Critical Error** that destroys the subsequent ratio (1.4).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\),  \n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n> \\]  \n> Apply this to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\).  Projecting onto the line \\(MN\\) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}. \\tag{2.1}\n