) is not established, and the derived ratio \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) is unjustified.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the entire similarity claim collapses, breaking the logical chain for all subsequent steps that depend on (1.4).\n\n---\n\n#### Step\u202f2 \u2013 \u201cPosition of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}\\).  \n> Apply this to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\).  Projecting onto the line \\(MN\\) we obtain \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}\\).  \n> Using (1.4), \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\).  \n> Thus \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and divides it in the same ratio as the radii.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The vector identity \\(\\vec{PH}= \\vec{PM}+\\vec{PN}\\) is correct for the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n- However, projecting this vector equation onto the line \\(MN\\) does **not** imply that the point \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\). The projection yields a scalar relation involving the components of \\(\\vec{PH}\\) along \\(MN\\), but the orthocenter of a generic triangle is not collinear with the two base vertices. Only in the special case where \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is right\u2011angled at \\(P\\) would \\(H\\) lie on \\(MN\\). No such right\u2011angle has been established.  \n- Consequently the derived ratio \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}\\) is not valid, and the subsequent use of (1.4) to obtain \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) is baseless.  \n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the claim that \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and the ratio (2.1) are false, invalidating the whole \u201cposition of the orthocenter\u201d argument.\n\n---\n\n#### Step\u202f3 \u2013 \u201cAn inversion centred at \\(P\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and arbitrary radius \\(\\rho\\).  \n> Because of (1.1) the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) pass through the centre of inversion; consequently each of them is transformed into a line.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- Equation (1.1) only tells us that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\). It does **not** imply that \\(P\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) or \\(\\Gamma\\). In fact, \\(\\Omega\\) is centred at \\(M\\) with radius \\(r\\); unless \\(PM=r\\) (which is not given), \\(P\\) is not on \\(\\Omega\\). The same holds for \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n- Therefore only the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) passes through \\(P\\); \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) do **not**. Under inversion, a circle that does **not** pass through the centre becomes another circle, not a line. The claim that all three circles become lines is false.  \n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the inversion mapping is incorrectly described, undermining all later statements that rely on it (e.g., the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and the treatment of line \\(EF\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### Step\u202f4 \u2013 \u201cRatio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cThe power of a point with respect to an inversion is preserved up to the factor \\(\\rho^{2}\\).  Applying this to the point \\(B\\) we have  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot BE}{BP\\cdot BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BE)}{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BF)} = \\frac{BC}{BD}.\n> \\]  \n> But \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are the distances from \\(B\\) to the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the radii through \\(B\\); therefore  \n> \\[\n> BC=BM=r,\\qquad BD=BN=R.\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point argument is misapplied. The inversion does not preserve the ratio \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}\\) in the way claimed; the correct relation involves products \\(BP\\cdot BE\\) and \\(BP\\cdot BF\\) being equal to \\(\\rho^{2}\\) times the distances from the inverse points to