            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\operatorname{circ}(M,r),\\qquad \\Gamma=\\operatorname{circ}(N,R),\\qquad r<R .\n\\]\n\nThe circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A,B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\).  \nDefine \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) as the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) and put \\(X:=AP\\cap EF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n\\[\nPC=PD=PA. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nIn the circle \\((P C D)\\) the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPM=\\angle DPN. \\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\nBoth triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) also subtend the same chord \\(CD\\), therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PCM=\\angle PDN. \\tag{1.3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1.2) and (1.3) the triangles are similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\),\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n\\]\n\nApply this to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\).  Projecting onto the line \\(MN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1.4),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and divides it in the same ratio as the radii.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  An inversion centred at \\(P\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and arbitrary radius \\(\\rho\\).  \nBecause of (1.1) the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) pass through the centre of inversion; consequently each of them is transformed into a line.\n\n- \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the line \\(C'P\\) where \\(C'=\\mathcal I(C)\\).  \n- \\(\\Gamma\\) is sent to the line \\(D'P\\) where \\(D'=\\mathcal I(D)\\).  \n- The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) is sent to the line \\(A'P\\) (with \\(A'=\\mathcal I(A)\\)).\n\nSince \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the inversion maps \\(E\\) to \\(C\\) and \\(F\\) to \\(D\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(E)=C,\\qquad \\mathcal I(F)=D. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\)\n\nThe power of a point with respect to an inversion is preserved up to the factor \\(\\rho^{2}\\).  Applying this to the point \\(B\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot BE}{BP\\cdot BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BE)}{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BF)}\n               = \\frac{BC}{BD}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are the distances from \\(B\\) to the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the radii through \\(B\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nBC=BM=r,\\qquad BD=BN=R. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4.1) and (4.2) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}}. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Ratio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\)\n\nThe point \\(X\\) is the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(EF\\).  Under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(EF\\) is transformed into the circle through the four points \\(C,D,P\\) (the image of the line \\(AP\\) is the circle \\((C D P)\\)).  Consequently \\(X\\) is mapped to the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with that circle, i.e. to the point \\(P\\) itself.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(X)=P. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing the distance\u2011preserving property of inversion as in (4.1),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{C\\!P}{P\\!F}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1.4) we have \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{PC}{PD}= \\frac{r}{R}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{r}{R}}. \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)\n\nIn a triangle a cevian through a vertex is an internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  \nFrom (4.3) and (5.3),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}.\n\\]\n\nThus the cevian \\(BX\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\)) satisfies the defining property of the internal symmedian issued from \\(B\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nAP\\ \\text{is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle B E F\\text{ from }B. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Lemma (symmedian\u2013tangent)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle, let \\(K\\neq X\\) be the second intersection of the internal symmedian from \\(X\\) with the circumcircle \\((XYZ)\\), and let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(XYZ\\). Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) is tangent to \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote \\(S:=XK\\cap YZ\\).  By definition of a symmedian,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{YX}{ZX}. \\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\nApplying the law of sines in \\(\\triangle YXZ\\) and \\(\\triangle YSZ\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{YX}{ZX}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY},\\qquad\n\\frac{YS}{ZS}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}. \\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (L1) and (L2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin\\angle YSZ}{\\sin\\angle ZSY}= \\frac{\\sin\\angle YXZ}{\\sin\\angle ZXY}. \\tag{L3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(S\\) lies on the symmedian, the oriented equalities  \n\n\\[\n\\angle YSZ = \\angle YXZ,\\qquad \\angle ZSY = \\angle ZXY\n\\]\n\nhold, so (L3) is an identity.  Hence the quadrilateral \\(YZXK\\) is harmonic, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n(Y,Z;K,\\infty_{XK})=-1. \\tag{L4}\n\\]\n\nIn a harmonic bundle the line through the orthocenter parallel to the line joining a vertex with the harmonic conjugate of the point at infinity on the opposite side is tangent to the circumcircle at that vertex (a classical result; a short proof follows from reflecting the altitude from \\(Y\\) in the side \\(XZ\\) and using the harmonic relation).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(XK\\) touches \\((XYZ)\\) at \\(K\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Completion of the problem\n\nApply the lemma to the triangle \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n- By (6.1) the line \\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian from \\(B\\); let \\(K\\neq B\\) be its second intersection with the circumcircle \\((B E F)\\).  By construction \\(K\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n- The orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).  Indeed, from (2.2) the point \\(H\\) divides \\(MN\\) in the ratio \\(r\\!:\\!R\\); the feet of the altitudes from \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) onto \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) are exactly the points where the lines through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(BF\\) and \\(BE\\) meet \\(MN\\).  Those feet determine the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B E F\\), so the two orthocentres are the same point.\n\nConsequently, by the Lemma, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(BK\\) \u2013 i.e. parallel to \\(AP\\) \u2013 is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) at \\(K\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
