 \\(\\Omega=\\operatorname{circ}(M,r)\\) and \\(\\Gamma=\\operatorname{circ}(N,R)\\) with \\(r<R\\), the intersection points \\(A,B\\), the points \\(C,M,N,D\\) on line \\(MN\\), the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the points \\(E,F\\) as second intersections of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\), the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), and \\(X:=AP\\cap EF\\). These definitions are fine.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 \u201cSimilarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),\n\n\\[\nPC=PD=PA. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nIn the circle \\((P C D)\\) the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPM=\\angle DPN. \\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\nBoth triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) also subtend the same chord \\(CD\\), therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle PCM=\\angle PDN. \\tag{1.3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1.2) and (1.3) the triangles are similar, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1.4}\n\\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- (1.1) is correct: as the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A,C,D\\).  \n- The claim \u201cthe chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) \u2026 subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\) of \\((PCD)\\)\u201d is **false** because \\(CM\\) is not a chord of the circle \\((PCD)\\); the only points on that circle are \\(P,C,D\\). Hence \\(\\angle CPM\\) is not defined as an angle subtended by a chord of \\((PCD)\\).  \n- Similarly, the statement \u201cBoth triangles \u2026 also subtend the same chord \\(CD\\)\u201d is misleading: while both triangles share the side \\(CD\\) as a segment of the line \\(MN\\), the angles \\(\\angle PCM\\) and \\(\\angle PDN\\) are not angles subtended by the same chord in a common circle. No justification is provided that these angles are equal.  \n- Consequently, the similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) is **unproved** and, in fact, generally false. The ratio \\(\\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}\\) does **not** hold for arbitrary intersecting circles.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This step contains a **Critical Error** that invalidates the subsequent ratio (1.4).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 \u201cPosition of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\),\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n\\]\n\nApply this to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\).  Projecting onto the line \\(MN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1.4),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and divides it in the same ratio as the radii.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The vector identity \\(\\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}\\) is correct for the orthocenter.  \n- Substituting \\(X=P, Y=M, Z=N\\) yields \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+ \\overrightarrow{PN}\\), i.e. \\(H = M+N-P\\). This shows that the **midpoint of \\(PH\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\)**, but it does **not** imply that \\(H\\) lies on line \\(MN\\). In fact, unless \\(P\\) itself lies on \\(MN\\) (which is not generally true), \\(H\\) is symmetric to \\(P\\) with respect to the midpoint of \\(MN\\) and therefore lies off the line \\(MN\\).  \n- The projection argument that \u201c\\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}\\)\u201d is therefore **incorrect**; the ratio of the signed distances from \\(H\\) to \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) is not equal to the ratio of the distances from \\(P\\) to \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \n- Since (1.4) is already false, the derived equality \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}\\) is baseless.  \n- The statement \u201c\\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\)\u201d is false in general.\n\n**Conclusion:** This step contains a **Critical Error** (incorrect projection argument and false conclusion about the location of \\(H\\)).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f3 \u2013 \u201cAn inversion centred at \\(P\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and arbitrary radius \\(\\rho\\).  \nBecause of (1.1) the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) pass through the centre of inversion; consequently each of them is transformed into a line.\n\n- \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the line \\(C'P\\) where \\(C'=\\mathcal I(C)\\).  \n- \\(\\Gamma\\) is sent to the line \\(D'P\\) where \\(D'=\\mathcal I(D)\\).  \n- The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) is sent to the line \\(A'P\\) (with \\(A'=\\mathcal I(A)\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- From (1.1) we only know that \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A, C, D\\). This **does not** imply that \\(P\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) or \\(\\Gamma\\). In fact, \\(P\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) but **not** on the two original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (unless the circles are concentric, which they are not).  \n- Therefore only the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) passes through the inversion centre; \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) do **not**. Under inversion, a circle **not** passing through the centre maps to another circle, not to a line. Hence the statements that \u201c\\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the line \\(C'P\\)\u201d and \u201c\\(\\Gamma\\) is sent to the line \\(D'P\\)\u201d are false.  \n- Moreover, a circle through the inversion centre maps to a line **not** passing through the centre. The claim that the image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the line \\(A'P\\) (which passes through \\(P\\)) contradicts this basic property of inversion.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This step contains multiple **Critical Errors** concerning the behaviour of the inversion.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f4 \u2013 \u201cRatio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cThe power of a point with respect to an inversion is preserved up to the factor \\(\\rho^{2}\\).  Applying this to the point \\(B\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot BE}{BP\\cdot BF}= \\frac{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BE)}{BP\\cdot \\mathcal I(BF)}\n               = \\frac{BC}{BD}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are the distances from \\(B\\) to the centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the radii through \\(B\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nBC=BM=r,\\qquad BD=BN=R. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The \u201cpower of a point\u201d is **not** preserved under inversion in the manner claimed. For a point \\(X\\) not at the centre, the relation is \\(X\\!X' = \\rho^{2}\\) where \\(X'\\) is the image of \\(X\\). There is no simple multiplicative preservation of ratios of segment lengths as written. Hence the derivation of \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{BC}{BD}\\) is **incorrect**.  \n- The assertion that \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the radii through \\(B\\) is false. The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of line \\(MN\\) with the two circles; they are **not** collinear with \\(B\\) and the respective centres. Consequently \\(BC\\neq BM\\) and \\(BD\\neq BN\\). The equalities \\(BC=BM=r\\) and \\(BD=BN=R\\) are **false**.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This step contains a **Critical Error** (misuse of inversion and false distance equalities).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f5 \u2013 \u201cRatio \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{r}{R}\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cThe point \\(X\\) is the intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(EF\\).  Under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(EF\\) is transformed into the circle through the four points \\(C,D,P\\) (the image of the line \\(AP\\) is the circle \\((C D P)\\)).  Consequently \\(X\\) is mapped to the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with that circle, i.e. to the point \\(P\\) itself.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(X)=P. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing the distance\u2011preserving property of inversion as in (4.1),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{C\\!P}{P\\!F}= \\frac{PC}{PD}. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- By construction, \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the **same line** \\(AP\\) with the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\). Hence the segment \\(EF\\) lies **on** the line \\(AP\\); consequently \\(AP\\) and \\(EF\\) are the same line. The intersection \\(X:=AP\\cap EF\\) is therefore not a single point but the whole line, making the definition of \\(X\\) meaningless.  \n- Because \\(EF\\) coincides with \\(AP\\) and passes through the inversion centre \\(P\\), its image under inversion is a **line**, not a circle. The claim that \u201cthe line \\(EF\\) is transformed into the circle through \\(C,D,P\\)\u201d is false.  \n- The statement \\(\\mathcal I(X)=P\\) is therefore baseless; \\(X\\) is not defined, and even if one considered a specific point on \\(AP\\), its image would not necessarily be \\(P\\).  \n- The ratio \\(\\frac{CX}{XF}= \\frac{CP}{PF}\\) is derived using the same erroneous inversion properties as in Step\u202f4. Since the underlying mapping is incorrect, the ratio is unjustified.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This step contains a **Critical Error** (ill\u2011defined point \\(X\\) and incorrect inversion mapping).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 \u201c\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\u201d\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n\u201cIn a triangle a cevian through a vertex is an internal symmedian iff it divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides.  \nFrom (4.3) and (5.3),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BE}{BF}= \\frac{CX}{XF}.\n\\]\n\nThus the cevian \\(BX\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP\\)) satisfies the defining property of the internal symmedian issued from \\(B\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nAP\\ \\text{is the internal symmedian of }\\triangle B E F\\text{ from }B. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The characterization of a symmedian