\).\u201d (Step\u202f4)  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The relationship between distances under inversion is mis\u2011applied; the ratio \\(\\frac{BE}{BF}\\) is **not** equal to \\(\\frac{BC}{BD}\\) by any standard inversion property.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(EF\\) is transformed into the circle through the four points \\(C,D,P\\) \u2026 Consequently \\(X\\) is mapped to the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with that circle, i.e. to the point \\(P\\) itself.\u201d (Step\u202f5)  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The description of the image of \\(EF\\) is incorrect (a line not through the centre inverts to a circle **through** the centre, not to the circle \\((CDP)\\) as claimed), and \\(X\\) does **not** map to \\(P\\).\n\n* **Location:** \u201c\\(AP\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\u201d (Step\u202f6)  \n  **Issue:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The conclusion relies on the previously false ratio statements; no rigorous argument establishing the symmedian property is provided.\n\n* **Location:** Lemma (symmedian\u2013tangent) and its proof (Step\u202f7).  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The lemma is **false** in general (e.g. for a right\u2011angled triangle the claimed tangency does not occur). The proof contains unsupported statements about harmonic bundles and a \u201cclassical result\u201d that is not proved.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cThe orthocenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) coincides with the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\u201d (Step\u202f8)  \n  **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 No justification is given; the two orthocentres are not generally the same point.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\n### Preliminary definitions (correct)\n- The solution correctly defines \\(\\Omega=\\operatorname{circ}(M,r)\\), \\(\\Gamma=\\operatorname{circ}(N,R)\\) with \\(r<R\\), and introduces points \\(A,B,C,D,P,E,F,H\\) as in the problem statement.  \n- The statement \u201c\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) \u21d2 \\(PC=PD=PA\\)\u201d (Equation\u202f(1.1)) is **correct**.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Similarity of \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle A C D\\),  \n> \\[\n> PC=PD=PA. \\tag{1.1}\n> \\]  \n> In the circle \\((P C D)\\) the chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and the chord \\(DN\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\); hence  \n> \\[\n> \\angle CPM=\\angle DPN. \\tag{1.2}\n> \\]  \n> Both triangles \\(\\triangle PCM\\) and \\(\\triangle PDN\\) also subtend the same chord \\(CD\\), therefore  \n> \\[\n> \\angle PCM=\\angle PDN. \\tag{1.3}\n> \\]  \n> From (1.2) and (1.3) the triangles are similar, giving  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{PM}{PN}= \\frac{CM}{DN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{1.4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- (1.1) is correct.  \n- The claim \u201cthe chord \\(CM\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) \u2026 subtend equal angles at the centre \\(P\\)\u201d is **false** because \\(CM\\) is **not** a chord of the circle \\((PCD)\\); the only points of that circle are \\(P, C, D\\). Hence \\(\\angle CPM\\) is not an angle subtended by a chord of that circle, and (1.2) has no justification.  \n- Similarly, \\(\\angle PCM\\) does **not** subtend chord \\(CD\\); the angle subtended by chord \\(CD\\) at a point on the circle is \\(\\angle CPD\\), not \\(\\angle PCM\\). Thus (1.3) is also unjustified.  \n- Consequently the similarity conclusion (1.4) is **unsupported**.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the similarity claim is based on nonexistent chord\u2011angle relations, breaking the logical chain for all later results that depend on (1.4).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Position of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cIn any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with orthocenter \\(H\\),  \n> \\[\n> \\overrightarrow{XH}= \\overrightarrow{XY}+ \\overrightarrow{XZ}.\n> \\]  \n> Apply this to \\(X=P,\\;Y=M,\\;Z=N\\).  Projecting onto the line \\(MN\\) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}. \\tag{2.1}\n> \\]  \n> Using (1.4),  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{2.2}\n> \\]  \n> Thus \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and divides it in the same ratio as the radii.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The vector identity \\(\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) is a **standard** property of the orthocenter (true).  \n- However, from this vector equation one **cannot** deduce that the orthogonal projection of \\(H\\) onto \\(MN\\) satisfies \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}= \\frac{PM}{PN}\\). In fact, solving the vector equation shows that \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) across the midpoint of \\(MN\\); consequently \\(H\\) is generally **not** on the line \\(MN\\) and the ratio \\(\\frac{HM}{HN}\\) equals \\(\\frac{PN}{PM}\\), the reciprocal of the claimed ratio.  \n- Therefore (2.1) and the subsequent conclusion that \\(H\\) lies on \\(MN\\) are **incorrect**.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the projection argument is invalid, and the derived ratio (2.2) is false.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Inversion centred at \\(P\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and arbitrary radius \\(\\rho\\).  \n> Because of (1.1) the three circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) pass through the centre of inversion; consequently each of them is transformed into a line.  \n> - \\(\\Omega\\) is sent to the line \\(C'P\\) where \\(C'=\\mathcal I(C)\\).  \n> - \\(\\Gamma\\) is sent to the line \\(D'P\\) where \\(D'=\\mathcal I(D)\\).  \n> - The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle A C D\\) is sent to the line \\(A'P\\) (with \\(A'=\\mathcal I(A)\\)).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The inversion centre is \\(P\\). While the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) indeed passes through \\(P\\) (by definition of \\(P\\) as its centre), **the